{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:37:22.100Z","ordinal":22079,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-afc9a982-cb1e-4fb9-a967-6d79173063f4","query":"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents.md","action":{"type":"openPage","url":"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents.md"},"results":[{"type":"text_result","ref_id":"turn30view0","snippet":"Total lines: 1","title":"Internal Error"}]},"started_at_ms":1788831441025,"completed_at_ms":1788831442100}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:37:22.462Z","ordinal":22080,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-10c7f4a4-58ee-4966-ae28-71977d1fce2b","process_id":"59140","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat AGENTS.md; cat .codex/agents/cakebrew-*.toml; cat .codex/config.toml; git diff -- .codex/config.toml; cat CLAUDE.md CONTRIBUTING.md; cat .agents/skills/*/SKILL.md; cat .agents/skills/*/agents/openai.yaml"],"cwd":"file:///Users/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/cakebrew","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat AGENTS.md; cat .codex/agents/cakebrew-*.toml; cat .codex/config.toml; git diff -- .codex/config.toml; cat CLAUDE.md CONTRIBUTING.md; cat .agents/skills/*/SKILL.md; cat .agents/skills/*/agents/openai.yaml"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew (Objective-C / AppKit).\n\n**This file is the single source of truth** for how work happens in this repo —\nconventions, architecture decisions, and the delivery workflow. It is written\nfor coding agents and human contributors alike. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to it.\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` is a short human-facing entry point that links here rather\nthan restating anything. Do not add a second workflow, roadmap, or status\ndocument: issues are the live status, and a second instruction file drifts.\n\nFollow this exactly; it encodes decisions the maintainer has already made, and\nmeasurements that cost real time to obtain.\n\n---\n\n## The workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype & spike] --> B[2. Inspect & branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. TDD: red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX review gate]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification gate]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Expert code review gate]\n    I -- Findings or changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated merge, linear history]\n```\n\n### Phase 0 — Discovery\n\n#### 1. Plan, prototype and spike\n\n**Spike before you commit to a design.** Explore the problem, then build the\nsmallest throwaway prototype that can test framework assumptions, feasibility,\nintegration boundaries, edge cases or performance risks. Never assume a\nthird-party API behaves as documented. Two examples from this repo's history:\nthe App Sandbox question was settled by measuring what actually broke, and\nlayered app-icon support was settled by feeding `actool` a probe entry and\nreading the warning. Both answers were the opposite of the documentation-level\nguess.\n\nWeigh alternatives on architectural fit, complexity, performance and\nmaintenance burden. Then break the design into an ordered list of thin vertical\nslices, and **throw the prototype away** — production code is rebuilt under\nTDD, not promoted from a spike. Clear all spike code and scratch artifacts from\nthe workspace before implementation begins.\n\n### Phase 1 — Context and scoping\n\n#### 2. Inspect and branch\n\n**Inspect before mutating.** Read the repo state, branches, and working tree\nfirst. Preserve unrelated staged, unstaged and untracked changes — this machine\nroutinely carries modifications under `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/` that\nare not yours to commit or discard. Stash them around a rebase; never\n`git checkout --` a file you did not write.\n\n**Branch off latest `main`.** Prefixes: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`,\n`chore/`, `test/`, `perf/`. Never commit to `main` directly.\n\n#### 3. Scope a thin vertical slice\n\n**Take one thin vertical slice** — the smallest cohesive end-to-end outcome\nthat can be tested, reviewed and shipped on its own. One PR per logical unit of\nwork; unrelated changes go on their own branch. A horizontal layer spanning the\nwhole app is not a slice.\n\n### Phase 2 — Test-driven implementation\n\n#### 4. Red, green, refactor\n\n**Red → green → refactor, and the red is not optional.** Write the smallest\nfailing test first and confirm it fails *for the expected reason* — a compile\nerror on a not-yet-existing API counts, a crash in the runner does not. Then\nthe minimum code to pass. Then clean up with the suite green.\n\nThe test and the code that satisfies it land in the **same commit**, and the\ncommit message names what the test covers. Every behaviour change ships with a\ntest written first: bugs, features, behaviour-changing refactors. Only pure\nformatting and documentation are exempt.\n\nIf you write the test and the implementation together and never observe the\nred, you have not done TDD — **prove the test bites** by mutation instead:\nbreak the implementation deliberately, confirm the suite fails, and restore it.\nSay which of the two you did in the PR.\n\n#### 5. Inspect the workspace diff\n\n**Then inspect the whole workspace diff**, including untracked files\n(`git status --untracked-files=all`). Remove scratch files, probes and\ndebugging artifacts. Temporary instrumentation must not reach a commit.\n\n### Phase 3 — Quality gates\n\nUse distinct reviewer roles or separate passes so that planning, implementation,\nUI review, verification and expert code review do not collapse into one\nself-review. Give reviewers the diff and observed evidence, not merely the\nimplementation intent. Run the gates in order. **If any gate causes a code\nchange, restart from the verification gate and obtain fresh code review\napproval** — a fix invalidates every result that came before it.\n\n#### 6. UI/UX and design review\n\n**UI/UX review** *(only if a user-facing surface changed)*. Verify it visually:\nlaunch the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`), look at it, confirm layout, dark mode,\nand badges. For behaviour rather than looks, add a `CakebrewUITests` journey —\nan assertion beats eyeballing a screenshot. Check accessibility and platform\nidioms, not just that it renders.\n\n#### 7. Verification and automated checks\n\n**Verification.** Build **Debug and Release**, both warning-free — a new\nwarning is a failure. Run the full unit suite. Compile the UI test target.\nRun the UI journeys before opening the PR. Treat every warning or failing check\nas a finding; after any code edit, rerun the full verification sequence from\nthe beginning.\n\n#### 8. Pre-commit expert code review\n\n**Code review** over the full branch diff plus anything uncommitted. Language\nidioms, memory and concurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases.\nResolve every actionable finding, then re-verify and repeat the review until it\nis approved.\n\n### Phase 4 — Integration and delivery\n\n#### 9. Structured atomic commit\n\n**Conventional Commits.** `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`, where type\nis one of `feat` `fix` `refactor` `docs` `chore` `test` `perf` `build` `ci`\nand scope names the area (`sidebar`, `reload`, `toolbar`, `brewfile`, `l10n`,\n…). The body explains *why*, and names what the test covers. History before\nthis convention was adopted is left alone; do not rewrite it.\n\nCommit only the reviewed and verified slice. The test and production code for a\nbehaviour land together in the same atomic commit.\n\n#### 10. Pull request\n\n**Open a PR with the `gh` CLI**, never the web UI, and never a draft unless\nasked. Describe what changed, why, and how it was tested — including what you\ncould *not* verify and the reason.\n\n#### 11. Gated merge and linear history\n\n**Green CI is the merge gate.** Both jobs (Build & Test, UI Tests) must pass.\nNever merge on pending or failing checks. If a UI test fails on CI, read the\nfailure and the `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the job log before assuming a\nflake — re-run once only when the evidence says infrastructure. If a human or\nautomated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass an assigned\nreview.\n\n**Merge and clean up:** `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch` (squash is\nthe maintainer's explicit choice, and keeps history linear and bisectable),\nthen `git checkout main`, `git pull --ff-only`.\n\n> **Stacked PRs:** retarget a child branch's base to `main` *before* merging its\n> parent. Deleting a merged branch auto-closes any PR still based on it, and\n> GitHub will not reopen a PR whose base is gone.\n\n### Quick reference\n\n| Stage | Pass criteria |\n| --- | --- |\n| Discovery | Feasibility measured, alternatives weighed, vertical slices ordered, spike discarded |\n| Setup | Repository inspected, unrelated work preserved, branch created from latest `main` |\n| Scope | Smallest independently shippable end-to-end outcome selected |\n| TDD | Focused test observed red, minimal implementation green, refactor stays green |\n| Diff | Staged, unstaged and untracked files inspected; no scratch or debug artifacts |\n| UI review | Changed surfaces checked for layout, appearance, accessibility and platform idioms |\n| Verification | Debug and Release warning-free; unit suite, UI target and UI journeys pass |\n| Code review | Full branch and workspace diff reviewed; all findings resolved; verification repeated after edits |\n| Delivery | Atomic Conventional Commit, ready PR, assigned approvals and both CI jobs green |\n| Merge | Squash merge completed, branch deleted, local `main` fast-forwarded |\n\n### Configured skills and subagents\n\nFor every feature, bugfix or behaviour-changing refactor, the primary agent\ninvokes `$cakebrew-workflow` and coordinates the configured roles below. The\nprimary agent owns requirements, cross-role decisions and delivery; subagents\nreturn bounded evidence and never silently expand their assignment.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary agent | Route the slice through every applicable phase and repeat invalidated gates |\n| Planner / architect | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measure unknowns, compare alternatives and define the vertical slice before implementation |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Own explicitly assigned files and produce red-green-refactor evidence |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Independently review every user-visible change after implementation stabilizes |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Run the complete applicable verification sequence without editing source |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Independently review the full branch and workspace diff after verification passes |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary agent | Perform only the commit, PR or merge actions the user authorized |\n\nRun the mutating implementer separately from gate agents. UI review,\nverification and code review do not fix their own findings: send them back to\nthe implementer, then rerun every invalidated gate. If independent work must run\nin parallel, give each agent an isolated worktree and explicit file ownership.\n\nThe skill and custom-agent files are routing adapters, not additional sources\nof project policy. They must defer to this file. Change workflow rules here\nfirst and keep the adapters narrow.\n\n---\n\n## Principles for agents working here\n\n**Prototypes are disposable.** Spike code exists to answer a question. Once\nanswered, delete it and rebuild under TDD. Do not promote a spike.\n\n**Separate the roles.** Planner, implementer, UI reviewer, verifier and code\nreviewer are different jobs with different biases. One prompt wearing all five\nhats reviews its own work and finds it good. Use separate agents or separate\npasses, and give the reviewer the diff rather than the intent.\n\n**Validate the instrument before believing its silence.** A probe that prints\nnothing has not proven anything — it may not have run, may have hit a stale\nbinary, or may have been filtered out. Confirm the probe executed and observed\nwhat you think it did. Concretely, in this repo: `xcodebuild analyze` exits 0\neven with findings; `xcodebuild -scheme Cakebrew` never compiles the UI test\ntarget; `xcodebuild test` output has to be *grepped for the assertion*, not\njust for `TEST SUCCEEDED`; and a `log show` predicate that matches nothing\nlooks exactly like a feature that did not fire.\n\n**Isolate shared state when working in parallel.** Agents on one machine\ncontend for real singletons: the Xcode derived-data directory, the app's\n`NSUserDefaults` domain, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, the running app itself,\nand the display. Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or\nreview pass is running against it; use a git worktree instead.\n\n**One source of truth.** This file. Pointer files may reference it; nothing may\nrestate it.\n\n---\n\n## Build & test commands\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests (fast, hermetic — run these locally every time)\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests (own scheme; needs ad-hoc signing; on CI this is authoritative)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nAfter touching `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither command above\ndoes, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs both jobs on `macos-26` (latest SDK),\nbuilds Debug and Release, runs the static analyzer, and uploads crash logs from\n`~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` on failure. A separate weekly\n`brew-compat.yml` pushes the runner's *real* brew output through the parsers,\nbecause fixture tests cannot notice upstream drift.\n\n## Testing architecture\n\n- **Mock brew:** launching the app with `-BPMockBrew` swaps\n  `BPHomebrewInterface` for `BPMockHomebrewInterface` (resolved in\n  `+sharedInterface`). The mock serves deterministic fixtures (`mockwget`,\n  `mockgit` [pinned], `mockchrome`/`mockvscode` [casks], …) and stubs every\n  operation as a no-op. **Any new interface method must get a mock override**\n  so UI tests never shell out to real brew — `BPMockFidelityTests` fails if a\n  mutating selector lacks one. Opt-in launch flags shape it for a specific\n  journey: `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`, `-BPMockEmptyCleanup`, and\n  `-BPMockSlowCatalog` (holds the catalog calls so progress and cancel are\n  observable; the mock is otherwise instant, which makes both unobservable).\n- **Unit tests** (`CakebrewTests/`) cover parsers, model logic, and manager\n  state. The `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` parsers are the standard TDD seam:\n  pure input → `BPFormula` output. Private classes are re-declared in the test\n  file to reach them.\n- **UI tests** (`CakebrewUITests/`) are journey tests against the mock. The\n  shared launch helper waits for the initial load to settle (mockwget rendered)\n  before navigating — do not remove that; it closes a reselect race in\n  `homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`. Sidebar item *names* repeat across groups\n  (two \"Installed\", two \"Outdated\"), so journeys address rows by their stable\n  unlocalized identifier — `[self sidebarRow:@\"sidebar.casks.installed\"]` —\n  never by index. Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` (pinned rows carry\n  a pin glyph after the name).\n- **CI environment limits:** the headless runner's window is never key, so\n  typing/keyboard focus is untestable; system file panels (NSSave/NSOpenPanel)\n  are out-of-process and undrivable. Pattern: unit-test the logic, UI-test\n  presence/navigation.\n- **Five ways a green local UI run lies.** Each of these has cost a red CI run\n  or a misdiagnosed failure:\n  1. **`isHittable` is meaningless on CI.** Hit testing needs a key window, so\n     it reports `false` there whatever is on screen. Assert with `exists` and\n     geometry (`frame.size`) instead.\n  2. **Anything persisted that changes the launch view makes the suite\n     order-dependent.** Each journey would inherit whatever the last one left\n     stored. Pin it in the shared launch helper through the argument domain,\n     which outranks stored defaults — `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1`,\n     `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"` — never by special-casing `-BPMockBrew` in\n     the app, so the feature itself stays exercised.\n  3. **Neither verify command compiles the UI test target.** `-scheme Cakebrew`\n     and `-scheme CakebrewTests` both skip `CakebrewUITests.m`, so a syntax\n     error there passes locally and fails on CI. After touching it, run\n     `xcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests …` — about two\n     seconds, versus ~7 minutes for the suite.\n  4. **An alert's buttons are mirrored to the Touch Bar.** `self.app.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`\n     therefore matches twice, and `firstMatch` can resolve to the mirror, which\n     is not clickable (\"cannot be called with Touch Bar elements\"). Scope to the\n     sheet: `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`. Note that\n     `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` tolerates a multi-match query, so an\n     existing assertion passing is not evidence that clicking will work.\n  5. **A locked screen breaks the suite in ways that look like product bugs.**\n     XCUITest cannot drive a locked display: clicks resolve to infinite points\n     and elements never become interactable. Before believing a UI failure that\n     appeared without a related change, check\n     `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked`; `screencapture` failing with\n     \"could not create image from window\" is the same signal. A ~7 minute run\n     can also be killed part-way by display sleep.\n\n## Architecture crib sheet\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` — all brew execution funnels through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async, streams) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may be nil — always\n  guard (`invokeOutputBlock:withString:`), passing nil is legitimate.\n- **List pattern:** a `BPListMode` enum case → a `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall`\n  subclass (arguments + line parser) → a `BPHomebrewManager` property fetched\n  in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:` → a sidebar item + badge → a mock\n  fixture. Follow this groove for any new list.\n- **Casks** are `BPFormula` instances with `cask == YES` (set by the cask list\n  parsers; survives copy/coding). Operations dispatch on that flag in\n  `BPInstallationWindowController` (`--cask` variants). `statusForFormula:` is\n  namespace-aware — casks read only the cask lists.\n- **Slow catalogs** (`brew formulae`, `brew casks`) share a 24h disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). `brew casks` can take 80+ s cold.\n- **Sidebar:** `FormulaeSideBarItem` enum values are **outline row indices**\n  (groups included). Inserting a row renumbers everything after it — update\n  the enum, the View-menu item tags in `MainMenu.xib`, and check every\n  `switch`/comparison on the enum (`grep -rn FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- **Never `reloadData` the sidebar to refresh a badge.** Reloading an\n  `NSOutlineView` clears its selection, and `-configureSidebarSettings` restores\n  the user's last row at setup — so a stray reload silently drops it, and the\n  next `sourceListSelectionDidChange` persists whatever replaced it. Use\n  `-refreshBadgeForListMode:`, which redraws one row with `reloadItem:`. The\n  old all-at-once reload got away with it only because it ran inside\n  `-homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`, which reselects immediately afterwards.\n- **Row identity for VoiceOver and XCUITest lives on the cell's text field**,\n  not the `NSTableCellView` — the container is not an accessibility element, so\n  an identifier set there reaches neither. Rows carry unlocalized identifiers\n  (`sidebar.casks.installed`); journeys address rows by those, never by index.\n\n## UI conventions\n\n- **Sheets, not app-modal:** every alert/confirmation uses\n  `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …` — never `runModal` (blocks\n  the app and is invisible to XCUITest). Attach to `_appDelegate.window`, NOT\n  `self.view.window` (nil under the split-view reparenting; caused a crash).\n- **Xibs are edited as XML by hand.** After editing: `xmllint --noout` the\n  file, keep new `id`s unique (prefix `cbk-…`), and grow an `NSStackView`'s\n  `visibilityPriorities`/`customSpacing` arrays when adding arranged subviews.\n  Menu items enable via Cocoa **bindings** on `currentFormula` /\n  `currentFormulaPinned` (see existing items), not `validateMenuItem:`.\n- **Localization:** UI strings go in all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/\n  Localizable.strings` files (lint with `plutil -lint`). New strings use the\n  English text in every locale until translated. Homebrew terms (\"Casks\") stay\n  untranslated. Xib menu titles are Base-internationalized literals — no\n  `.strings` churn for new menu items.\n- Icons are SF Symbols (`imageWithSystemSymbolName:`); no bundled image assets\n  for UI chrome.\n\n## Distribution & sandboxing\n\nCakebrew ships **Developer ID + hardened runtime + notarized**, and is\n**not** App-Sandboxed. `.github/workflows/release.yml` does the signing,\nnotarization and stapling on a `v*` tag (see its header for the required\nsecrets).\n\nThe sandbox question was settled by measurement, not assumption — repeat these\nif you revisit it:\n\n| Configuration | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Plain `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` | `brew` cannot even be executed: `zsh:1: operation not permitted: brew` |\n| Sandbox + `temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write` for the prefix | `brew --version` / `brew list` **work**; `/usr/local/Cellar`, `/usr/local/Caskroom`, `/Applications` are writable |\n| …but the home directory is redirected | `$HOME` becomes `~/Library/Containers/<id>/Data`, so `~/Library/Caches/Homebrew` and `~/Library/LaunchAgents` resolve **inside the container** |\n\nThat last row is the blocker: brew's download cache would diverge from the\nuser's real one (re-downloading, and disagreeing with `brew` in Terminal), and\n**`brew services` writes LaunchAgents to `~/Library/LaunchAgents`** — under the\nsandbox those land in the container where `launchd` never sees them, silently\nbreaking the Services feature. Temporary-exception entitlements are also an\nApp-Store-review mechanism (routinely rejected, and they hardcode a prefix that\ndiffers between `/usr/local` and `/opt/homebrew`), so they buy nothing for\nDeveloper ID distribution.\n\n**Therefore:** don't add `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Hardened runtime,\nno ATS weakening, a privacy manifest, and validated user input are the\nhardening this app can actually carry.\n\n## Signed local builds (helper testing)\n\nDay-to-day development needs nothing special: an Xcode build (Cmd-R) is signed\nwith your Apple Development certificate, and that **already satisfies the\nhelper's designated requirement** (it pins `anchor apple generic` plus the team\nOU, which development certs carry). So the debug build can drive the helper.\n\nTo exercise helper registration and Login Items approval for real, the app has\nto be signed and living in `/Applications`:\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-signed.sh          # Release, or pass Debug\n```\n\nIt builds into a temporary derived-data dir (so it can't leave a stale\nunsigned helper in your normal one), verifies both signatures, replaces\n`/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, and prints the next steps.\n\nA final build phase, **Verify embedded helper signature**, fails the build if\nthe embedded helper wouldn't satisfy the app's requirement — otherwise the app\nsilently refuses to talk to its own helper at runtime. It skips automatically\nfor unsigned/ad-hoc builds (CI), and declares the helper as a script input\nbecause Xcode sandboxes script phases.\n\n**Distribution certificates:** notarized releases need a *Developer ID\nApplication* certificate, which is separate from Apple Development. Until one\nexists in the account, `.github/workflows/release.yml` cannot run.\n\n## OS support policy\n\nMinimum deployment target = **latest macOS minus one** (currently 15.0);\nbuild and CI against the **latest SDK** (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned;\nCI runner tracks the newest image, currently `macos-26`). When a new major\nmacOS ships: bump the deployment target one, bump the CI runner, never pin\n`SDKROOT`. `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Known environment quirks (primary dev machine)\n\n- **`git push` hangs** on the Entire pre-push hook (session-log sync). Push\n  with `git push --no-verify` — it skips only that hook, nothing code-related.\n- **Local XCUITest works** (since 2026-08-15). It used to die at init with\n  \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\"; the cause was macOS developer\n  mode being off, fixed once with `sudo DevToolsSecurity -enable`. Being in the\n  `_developer` group is not sufficient on its own, and Accessibility permission\n  is *not* required. If that init timeout ever returns, check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. The full local run takes ~6 minutes (vs ~4s\n  for the unit suite), so it is a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one. CI remains\n  the merge gate.\n- **A locked screen blocks the whole visual toolchain**, not just XCUITest:\n  `screencapture` fails with \"could not create image from window\", and the UI\n  suite fails with `Failed to activate application … (current state: Running\n  Background)`. Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before\n  believing either. There is no way around it from a shell — the work is a\n  pre-PR step that needs the display awake.\n- **You cannot force one app to light mode from the command line.**\n  `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` in the argument domain is ignored: AppKit reads\n  the appearance from the system preference directly rather than through\n  `NSUserDefaults` precedence, so the app comes up in whatever the system is.\n  Capturing a light-mode screenshot means flipping System Settings ▸ Appearance\n  by hand.\n- Commit signing is temporarily disabled (`git -c commit.gpgsign=false`) while\n  the 1Password SSH agent is broken. Re-enable when fixed.\nname = \"cakebrew-code-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent pre-commit Cakebrew reviewer focused on correctness, regressions, Objective-C/AppKit safety, architecture, and missing tests.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"read-only\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-code-review. Review the full branch diff plus staged, unstaged, and untracked files after verification passes.\nDo not edit source files. Lead with actionable findings ordered by severity and include precise file and line evidence. Avoid style-only comments. Explicitly approve the gate when no actionable findings remain; route fixes to the implementer and require fresh verification afterward.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-implementer\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew implementation specialist for one explicitly assigned vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-implement. You own only the files and behavior explicitly assigned by the primary agent. You are not alone in the codebase: preserve unrelated work and accommodate concurrent changes without reverting them.\nObserve the focused test fail for the expected reason before production implementation, then make the minimum change, refactor green, and inspect the whole workspace diff.\nReturn changed files and red-green-refactor evidence. Do not approve your own UI, verification, or code-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs to the primary agent.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-planner\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew discovery and planning specialist for spikes, architectural decisions, and thin vertical slice definition before implementation.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-plan and stay in the planner role. Inspect and measure assumptions, compare alternatives, and return the smallest testable vertical slice with acceptance criteria and an expected red test.\nAny spike is disposable. Keep it isolated, remove it before returning, and never promote it into production code.\nDo not implement the slice, commit, push, open a PR, or modify project instructions. Preserve every unrelated change.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-ui-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent Cakebrew AppKit UI and UX reviewer for visual changes, accessibility, platform conventions, and UI journey coverage.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-ui-review. Review the implemented user-visible surface against the branch and workspace diff using the mock build and the repository's documented UI constraints.\nDo not edit source files. Return reproducible findings ordered by impact, or explicitly approve the UI gate when no actionable findings remain. Route fixes to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-verifier\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew verification specialist for warning-free Debug and Release builds, unit tests, UI target compilation, and UI journeys.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-verify and run the complete applicable verification gate from the beginning. Validate that each instrument exercised the intended target.\nDo not edit source files. Separate real failures from sandbox, signing, locked-display, and headless-CI artifacts. Return compact command evidence and an explicit pass or fail; route failures to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\n\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\napproval_policy = \"never\"\n[features]\nhooks = true\ndiff --git a/.codex/config.toml b/.codex/config.toml\nindex 3d76ec0..b28a6e6 100644\n--- a/.codex/config.toml\n+++ b/.codex/config.toml\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@\n \n+sandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\n+approval_policy = \"never\"\n [features]\n hooks = true\n@AGENTS.md\n# Contributing\n\n**[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) is the single source of truth** for how work happens\nhere — the workflow, the conventions, and the decisions already made. It is\nwritten for human contributors and coding agents alike.\n\nThis file deliberately does not restate any of it. Two documents describing one\nworkflow drift apart, and then neither can be trusted.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) once, end to end. It is not long, and most of it\n   is things that cost someone a wasted afternoon to learn.\n2. Branch off `main`, take one thin slice, write the failing test first.\n3. Open a PR with `gh`. Green CI is the merge gate.\n\n## The commands you will need every time\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests — seconds, run them every time\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests — a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one (~8 minutes)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nIf you touch `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither test command\nabove does, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n## Reporting bugs\n\nPlease use the issue templates. The environment questions are not boilerplate:\nthe macOS version, the Homebrew version and prefix, and whether the problem\nreproduces under `-BPMockBrew` are what separate a parser bug from an\nenvironment one.\n\nSecurity issues go through [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a public issue.\n---\nname: cakebrew-code-review\ndescription: Perform the independent pre-commit expert review of a Cakebrew branch and all uncommitted files after verification passes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Code Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the full branch diff against its base plus\nall staged, unstaged and untracked files. Do not edit source files.\n\nPrioritize correctness, regressions, Objective-C and AppKit idioms, memory and\nconcurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, mock fidelity and\nmissing tests. Trace the actual execution path before reporting a problem.\nAvoid style-only comments unless they reveal a concrete maintenance or defect\nrisk.\n\nReturn findings ordered by severity with precise file and line references,\nimpact and a reproducible rationale. If there are no actionable findings, say\nthe gate is approved. Any fix belongs to the implementer and invalidates the\nprior verification result.\n---\nname: cakebrew-deliver\ndescription: Commit, publish, review, merge, and clean up a fully verified Cakebrew slice when the user explicitly requests the applicable delivery actions.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Deliver\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Delivery does not grant authority: perform only the\ncommit, push, PR or merge actions the user requested.\n\n- Confirm UI review when applicable, complete verification, and expert approval\n  all correspond to the current source state.\n- Reinspect the entire workspace and stage only the intended slice. Preserve\n  unrelated modifications and untracked files.\n- Create the atomic Conventional Commit required by `AGENTS.md`, including why\n  the change exists and what the test covers.\n- Open a ready PR with `gh` only when requested, reporting all verification and\n  any genuine gaps.\n- Wait for both CI jobs and every assigned human or automated review. Never\n  bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n- Squash merge and clean up only with explicit merge authorization, including\n  the stacked-PR precaution in `AGENTS.md`.\n\nStop and report any stale gate evidence, changed head, failed CI, unresolved\nreview, authentication issue or authorization boundary.\n---\nname: cakebrew-implement\ndescription: Implement an assigned Cakebrew vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD after its scope and acceptance criteria are established.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Implement\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and accept explicit ownership of the assigned files\nor behavior. Other work may exist in the checkout; never revert or absorb it.\n\n1. Confirm the slice, acceptance criteria and intended test seam.\n2. Add the smallest focused test first and run it to observe the expected red.\n3. Implement only enough production code to make it green.\n4. Refactor with the focused test and relevant fast suite green.\n5. Inspect all staged, unstaged and untracked files and remove scratch or debug\n   artifacts created by this implementation.\n\nIf red was not observed, perform and report the mutation proof required by\n`AGENTS.md`. Stop after implementation evidence; do not self-approve the UI,\nverification or expert-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs\nto the primary agent under `$cakebrew-deliver`.\n---\nname: cakebrew-plan\ndescription: Plan or spike a Cakebrew change before implementation when requirements, architecture, framework behavior, integration boundaries, or vertical-slice scope need to be resolved.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Plan\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Stay in the planner role: do not implement\nproduction code.\n\n- Inspect the repository and trace the relevant execution paths.\n- Identify unknowns that require measurement. Use the smallest disposable\n  spike needed, keep it out of production paths, and remove it before returning.\n- Compare viable alternatives by architectural fit, complexity, performance\n  and maintenance burden.\n- Define the smallest cohesive vertical slice, its acceptance criteria, test\n  seams and expected red test.\n- Report assumptions, evidence, risks, rejected alternatives and an ordered\n  slice plan. Clearly distinguish measured behavior from inference.\n\nPreserve all unrelated changes. Do not commit, push, open a PR or change\nproject instructions.\n---\nname: cakebrew-ui-review\ndescription: Review a user-visible Cakebrew change for AppKit conventions, layout, appearance, accessibility, and UI journey coverage after implementation stabilizes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Ui Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the changed surface independently; do not\nedit source files.\n\n- Inspect the branch and workspace diff to identify affected journeys.\n- Launch against `-BPMockBrew` and verify the relevant state visually.\n- Check layout, dark mode, badges, accessibility identity and macOS idioms.\n- Prefer an existing or proposed `CakebrewUITests` assertion for behavior that\n  should not depend on visual judgment.\n- Account for the documented locked-screen and headless-CI limitations before\n  classifying a failure.\n\nReturn prioritized, reproducible findings with file or UI location and expected\nbehavior. Say explicitly when the gate passes. Send fixes back to the\nimplementer rather than making them in the review pass.\n---\nname: cakebrew-verify\ndescription: Run Cakebrew's complete local verification gate after implementation or fixes, including required builds, tests, warnings checks, and UI-target coverage.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Verify\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and use its current commands as the authority. Do\nnot edit source files.\n\nRun the gate from the beginning in its documented order:\n\n1. Debug build, warning-free.\n2. Release build, warning-free.\n3. Full unit suite.\n4. UI test target compilation.\n5. UI journeys before PR creation, subject to the documented display checks.\n\nValidate that each instrument actually exercised the intended target; do not\ntreat an exit code or silent output alone as proof. Separate genuine product or\nsetup failures from sandbox, signing, locked-screen or headless-environment\nartifacts. Return a compact command-by-command result with decisive diagnostics\nand an explicit pass/fail. Route failures to the implementer.\n---\nname: cakebrew-workflow\ndescription: Orchestrate a complete Cakebrew feature, bugfix, or behavior-changing refactor through planning, TDD implementation, UI review when applicable, verification, expert review, and delivery gates.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Workflow\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely before acting. It is the sole authority for the\nworkflow, commands, architecture and delivery rules; this skill only routes the\nwork through the configured roles.\n\n1. Use `cakebrew-planner` with `$cakebrew-plan` to validate assumptions and\n   define the smallest vertical slice. Any spike is disposable.\n2. Inspect repository state, synchronize the latest `main`, preserve unrelated\n   changes, and create the required branch before production edits.\n3. Give `cakebrew-implementer` explicit ownership of the slice and invoke\n   `$cakebrew-implement`. Require observed red-green-refactor evidence.\n4. If a user-visible surface changed, run `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` with\n   `$cakebrew-ui-review` after implementation is stable.\n5. Run `cakebrew-verifier` with `$cakebrew-verify`, then\n   `cakebrew-code-reviewer` with `$cakebrew-code-review`.\n6. Route every actionable finding back to the implementer. After any source\n   edit, repeat verification and expert review; repeat UI review too when the\n   fix changes the visible surface.\n7. Keep delivery with the primary agent. Invoke `$cakebrew-deliver` only when\n   the user has authorized the relevant commit, push, PR or merge action.\n\nDo not run a mutating agent concurrently with a verifier or reviewer against\nthe same worktree. Use isolated worktrees when independent passes truly need to\nrun in parallel. Return concise role summaries and evidence to the primary\nagent instead of raw logs.\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Code Review\"\n  short_description: \"Independently review the full Cakebrew diff\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-code-review to perform the pre-commit expert review of this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Deliver\"\n  short_description: \"Deliver a verified Cakebrew slice safely\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-deliver to perform the authorized delivery steps for this verified Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Implement\"\n  short_description: \"Implement one Cakebrew slice with strict TDD\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-implement to implement this scoped Cakebrew slice with red-green-refactor evidence.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Plan\"\n  short_description: \"Plan and de-risk a Cakebrew vertical slice\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-plan to investigate and scope this Cakebrew change before implementation.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew UI Review\"\n  short_description: \"Review Cakebrew UI, UX, and accessibility\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-ui-review to review the user-visible changes in this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Verify\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's complete verification gate\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-verify to verify the current Cakebrew branch from the start.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Workflow\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's gated engineering lifecycle\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-workflow to deliver this Cakebrew change through every required gate.\"\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew (Objective-C / AppKit).\n\n**This file is the single source of truth** for how work happens in this repo —\nconventions, architecture decisions, and the delivery workflow. It is written\nfor coding agents and human contributors alike. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to it.\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` is a short human-facing entry point that links here rather\nthan restating anything. Do not add a second workflow, roadmap, or status\ndocument: issues are the live status, and a second instruction file drifts.\n\nFollow this exactly; it encodes decisions the maintainer has already made, and\nmeasurements that cost real time to obtain.\n\n---\n\n## The workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype & spike] --> B[2. Inspect & branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. TDD: red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX review gate]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification gate]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Expert code review gate]\n    I -- Findings or changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated merge, linear history]\n```\n\n### Phase 0 — Discovery\n\n#### 1. Plan, prototype and spike\n\n**Spike before you commit to a design.** Explore the problem, then build the\nsmallest throwaway prototype that can test framework assumptions, feasibility,\nintegration boundaries, edge cases or performance risks. Never assume a\nthird-party API behaves as documented. Two examples from this repo's history:\nthe App Sandbox question was settled by measuring what actually broke, and\nlayered app-icon support was settled by feeding `actool` a probe entry and\nreading the warning. Both answers were the opposite of the documentation-level\nguess.\n\nWeigh alternatives on architectural fit, complexity, performance and\nmaintenance burden. Then break the design into an ordered list of thin vertical\nslices, and **throw the prototype away** — production code is rebuilt under\nTDD, not promoted from a spike. Clear all spike code and scratch artifacts from\nthe workspace before implementation begins.\n\n### Phase 1 — Context and scoping\n\n#### 2. Inspect and branch\n\n**Inspect before mutating.** Read the repo state, branches, and working tree\nfirst. Preserve unrelated staged, unstaged and untracked changes — this machine\nroutinely carries modifications under `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/` that\nare not yours to commit or discard. Stash them around a rebase; never\n`git checkout --` a file you did not write.\n\n**Branch off latest `main`.** Prefixes: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`,\n`chore/`, `test/`, `perf/`. Never commit to `main` directly.\n\n#### 3. Scope a thin vertical slice\n\n**Take one thin vertical slice** — the smallest cohesive end-to-end outcome\nthat can be tested, reviewed and shipped on its own. One PR per logical unit of\nwork; unrelated changes go on their own branch. A horizontal layer spanning the\nwhole app is not a slice.\n\n### Phase 2 — Test-driven implementation\n\n#### 4. Red, green, refactor\n\n**Red → green → refactor, and the red is not optional.** Write the smallest\nfailing test first and confirm it fails *for the expected reason* — a compile\nerror on a not-yet-existing API counts, a crash in the runner does not. Then\nthe minimum code to pass. Then clean up with the suite green.\n\nThe test and the code that satisfies it land in the **same commit**, and the\ncommit message names what the test covers. Every behaviour change ships with a\ntest written first: bugs, features, behaviour-changing refactors. Only pure\nformatting and documentation are exempt.\n\nIf you write the test and the implementation together and never observe the\nred, you have not done TDD — **prove the test bites** by mutation instead:\nbreak the implementation deliberately, confirm the suite fails, and restore it.\nSay which of the two you did in the PR.\n\n#### 5. Inspect the workspace diff\n\n**Then inspect the whole workspace diff**, including untracked files\n(`git status --untracked-files=all`). Remove scratch files, probes and\ndebugging artifacts. Temporary instrumentation must not reach a commit.\n\n### Phase 3 — Quality gates\n\nUse distinct reviewer roles or separate passes so that planning, implementation,\nUI review, verification and expert code review do not collapse into one\nself-review. Give reviewers the diff and observed evidence, not merely the\nimplementation intent. Run the gates in order. **If any gate causes a code\nchange, restart from the verification gate and obtain fresh code review\napproval** — a fix invalidates every result that came before it.\n\n#### 6. UI/UX and design review\n\n**UI/UX review** *(only if a user-facing surface changed)*. Verify it visually:\nlaunch the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`), look at it, confirm layout, dark mode,\nand badges. For behaviour rather than looks, add a `CakebrewUITests` journey —\nan assertion beats eyeballing a screenshot. Check accessibility and platform\nidioms, not just that it renders.\n\n#### 7. Verification and automated checks\n\n**Verification.** Build **Debug and Release**, both warning-free — a new\nwarning is a failure. Run the full unit suite. Compile the UI test target.\nRun the UI journeys before opening the PR. Treat every warning or failing check\nas a finding; after any code edit, rerun the full verification sequence from\nthe beginning.\n\n#### 8. Pre-commit expert code review\n\n**Code review** over the full branch diff plus anything uncommitted. Language\nidioms, memory and concurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases.\nResolve every actionable finding, then re-verify and repeat the review until it\nis approved.\n\n### Phase 4 — Integration and delivery\n\n#### 9. Structured atomic commit\n\n**Conventional Commits.** `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`, where type\nis one of `feat` `fix` `refactor` `docs` `chore` `test` `perf` `build` `ci`\nand scope names the area (`sidebar`, `reload`, `toolbar`, `brewfile`, `l10n`,\n…). The body explains *why*, and names what the test covers. History before\nthis convention was adopted is left alone; do not rewrite it.\n\nCommit only the reviewed and verified slice. The test and production code for a\nbehaviour land together in the same atomic commit.\n\n#### 10. Pull request\n\n**Open a PR with the `gh` CLI**, never the web UI, and never a draft unless\nasked. Describe what changed, why, and how it was tested — including what you\ncould *not* verify and the reason.\n\n#### 11. Gated merge and linear history\n\n**Green CI is the merge gate.** Both jobs (Build & Test, UI Tests) must pass.\nNever merge on pending or failing checks. If a UI test fails on CI, read the\nfailure and the `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the job log before assuming a\nflake — re-run once only when the evidence says infrastructure. If a human or\nautomated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass an assigned\nreview.\n\n**Merge and clean up:** `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch` (squash is\nthe maintainer's explicit choice, and keeps history linear and bisectable),\nthen `git checkout main`, `git pull --ff-only`.\n\n> **Stacked PRs:** retarget a child branch's base to `main` *before* merging its\n> parent. Deleting a merged branch auto-closes any PR still based on it, and\n> GitHub will not reopen a PR whose base is gone.\n\n### Quick reference\n\n| Stage | Pass criteria |\n| --- | --- |\n| Discovery | Feasibility measured, alternatives weighed, vertical slices ordered, spike discarded |\n| Setup | Repository inspected, unrelated work preserved, branch created from latest `main` |\n| Scope | Smallest independently shippable end-to-end outcome selected |\n| TDD | Focused test observed red, minimal implementation green, refactor stays green |\n| Diff | Staged, unstaged and untracked files inspected; no scratch or debug artifacts |\n| UI review | Changed surfaces checked for layout, appearance, accessibility and platform idioms |\n| Verification | Debug and Release warning-free; unit suite, UI target and UI journeys pass |\n| Code review | Full branch and workspace diff reviewed; all findings resolved; verification repeated after edits |\n| Delivery | Atomic Conventional Commit, ready PR, assigned approvals and both CI jobs green |\n| Merge | Squash merge completed, branch deleted, local `main` fast-forwarded |\n\n### Configured skills and subagents\n\nFor every feature, bugfix or behaviour-changing refactor, the primary agent\ninvokes `$cakebrew-workflow` and coordinates the configured roles below. The\nprimary agent owns requirements, cross-role decisions and delivery; subagents\nreturn bounded evidence and never silently expand their assignment.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary agent | Route the slice through every applicable phase and repeat invalidated gates |\n| Planner / architect | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measure unknowns, compare alternatives and define the vertical slice before implementation |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Own explicitly assigned files and produce red-green-refactor evidence |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Independently review every user-visible change after implementation stabilizes |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Run the complete applicable verification sequence without editing source |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Independently review the full branch and workspace diff after verification passes |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary agent | Perform only the commit, PR or merge actions the user authorized |\n\nRun the mutating implementer separately from gate agents. UI review,\nverification and code review do not fix their own findings: send them back to\nthe implementer, then rerun every invalidated gate. If independent work must run\nin parallel, give each agent an isolated worktree and explicit file ownership.\n\nThe skill and custom-agent files are routing adapters, not additional sources\nof project policy. They must defer to this file. Change workflow rules here\nfirst and keep the adapters narrow.\n\n---\n\n## Principles for agents working here\n\n**Prototypes are disposable.** Spike code exists to answer a question. Once\nanswered, delete it and rebuild under TDD. Do not promote a spike.\n\n**Separate the roles.** Planner, implementer, UI reviewer, verifier and code\nreviewer are different jobs with different biases. One prompt wearing all five\nhats reviews its own work and finds it good. Use separate agents or separate\npasses, and give the reviewer the diff rather than the intent.\n\n**Validate the instrument before believing its silence.** A probe that prints\nnothing has not proven anything — it may not have run, may have hit a stale\nbinary, or may have been filtered out. Confirm the probe executed and observed\nwhat you think it did. Concretely, in this repo: `xcodebuild analyze` exits 0\neven with findings; `xcodebuild -scheme Cakebrew` never compiles the UI test\ntarget; `xcodebuild test` output has to be *grepped for the assertion*, not\njust for `TEST SUCCEEDED`; and a `log show` predicate that matches nothing\nlooks exactly like a feature that did not fire.\n\n**Isolate shared state when working in parallel.** Agents on one machine\ncontend for real singletons: the Xcode derived-data directory, the app's\n`NSUserDefaults` domain, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, the running app itself,\nand the display. Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or\nreview pass is running against it; use a git worktree instead.\n\n**One source of truth.** This file. Pointer files may reference it; nothing may\nrestate it.\n\n---\n\n## Build & test commands\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests (fast, hermetic — run these locally every time)\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests (own scheme; needs ad-hoc signing; on CI this is authoritative)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nAfter touching `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither command above\ndoes, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs both jobs on `macos-26` (latest SDK),\nbuilds Debug and Release, runs the static analyzer, and uploads crash logs from\n`~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` on failure. A separate weekly\n`brew-compat.yml` pushes the runner's *real* brew output through the parsers,\nbecause fixture tests cannot notice upstream drift.\n\n## Testing architecture\n\n- **Mock brew:** launching the app with `-BPMockBrew` swaps\n  `BPHomebrewInterface` for `BPMockHomebrewInterface` (resolved in\n  `+sharedInterface`). The mock serves deterministic fixtures (`mockwget`,\n  `mockgit` [pinned], `mockchrome`/`mockvscode` [casks], …) and stubs every\n  operation as a no-op. **Any new interface method must get a mock override**\n  so UI tests never shell out to real brew — `BPMockFidelityTests` fails if a\n  mutating selector lacks one. Opt-in launch flags shape it for a specific\n  journey: `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`, `-BPMockEmptyCleanup`, and\n  `-BPMockSlowCatalog` (holds the catalog calls so progress and cancel are\n  observable; the mock is otherwise instant, which makes both unobservable).\n- **Unit tests** (`CakebrewTests/`) cover parsers, model logic, and manager\n  state. The `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` parsers are the standard TDD seam:\n  pure input → `BPFormula` output. Private classes are re-declared in the test\n  file to reach them.\n- **UI tests** (`CakebrewUITests/`) are journey tests against the mock. The\n  shared launch helper waits for the initial load to settle (mockwget rendered)\n  before navigating — do not remove that; it closes a reselect race in\n  `homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`. Sidebar item *names* repeat across groups\n  (two \"Installed\", two \"Outdated\"), so journeys address rows by their stable\n  unlocalized identifier — `[self sidebarRow:@\"sidebar.casks.installed\"]` —\n  never by index. Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` (pinned rows carry\n  a pin glyph after the name).\n- **CI environment limits:** the headless runner's window is never key, so\n  typing/keyboard focus is untestable; system file panels (NSSave/NSOpenPanel)\n  are out-of-process and undrivable. Pattern: unit-test the logic, UI-test\n  presence/navigation.\n- **Five ways a green local UI run lies.** Each of these has cost a red CI run\n  or a misdiagnosed failure:\n  1. **`isHittable` is meaningless on CI.** Hit testing needs a key window, so\n     it reports `false` there whatever is on screen. Assert with `exists` and\n     geometry (`frame.size`) instead.\n  2. **Anything persisted that changes the launch view makes the suite\n     order-dependent.** Each journey would inherit whatever the last one left\n     stored. Pin it in the shared launch helper through the argument domain,\n     which outranks stored defaults — `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1`,\n     `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"` — never by special-casing `-BPMockBrew` in\n     the app, so the feature itself stays exercised.\n  3. **Neither verify command compiles the UI test target.** `-scheme Cakebrew`\n     and `-scheme CakebrewTests` both skip `CakebrewUITests.m`, so a syntax\n     error there passes locally and fails on CI. After touching it, run\n     `xcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests …` — about two\n     seconds, versus ~7 minutes for the suite.\n  4. **An alert's buttons are mirrored to the Touch Bar.** `self.app.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`\n     therefore matches twice, and `firstMatch` can resolve to the mirror, which\n     is not clickable (\"cannot be called with Touch Bar elements\"). Scope to the\n     sheet: `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`. Note that\n     `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` tolerates a multi-match query, so an\n     existing assertion passing is not evidence that clicking will work.\n  5. **A locked screen breaks the suite in ways that look like product bugs.**\n     XCUITest cannot drive a locked display: clicks resolve to infinite points\n     and elements never become interactable. Before believing a UI failure that\n     appeared without a related change, check\n     `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked`; `screencapture` failing with\n     \"could not create image from window\" is the same signal. A ~7 minute run\n     can also be killed part-way by display sleep.\n\n## Architecture crib sheet\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` — all brew execution funnels through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async, streams) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may be nil — always\n  guard (`invokeOutputBlock:withString:`), passing nil is legitimate.\n- **List pattern:** a `BPListMode` enum case → a `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall`\n  subclass (arguments + line parser) → a `BPHomebrewManager` property fetched\n  in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:` → a sidebar item + badge → a mock\n  fixture. Follow this groove for any new list.\n- **Casks** are `BPFormula` instances with `cask == YES` (set by the cask list\n  parsers; survives copy/coding). Operations dispatch on that flag in\n  `BPInstallationWindowController` (`--cask` variants). `statusForFormula:` is\n  namespace-aware — casks read only the cask lists.\n- **Slow catalogs** (`brew formulae`, `brew casks`) share a 24h disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). `brew casks` can take 80+ s cold.\n- **Sidebar:** `FormulaeSideBarItem` enum values are **outline row indices**\n  (groups included). Inserting a row renumbers everything after it — update\n  the enum, the View-menu item tags in `MainMenu.xib`, and check every\n  `switch`/comparison on the enum (`grep -rn FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- **Never `reloadData` the sidebar to refresh a badge.** Reloading an\n  `NSOutlineView` clears its selection, and `-configureSidebarSettings` restores\n  the user's last row at setup — so a stray reload silently drops it, and the\n  next `sourceListSelectionDidChange` persists whatever replaced it. Use\n  `-refreshBadgeForListMode:`, which redraws one row with `reloadItem:`. The\n  old all-at-once reload got away with it only because it ran inside\n  `-homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`, which reselects immediately afterwards.\n- **Row identity for VoiceOver and XCUITest lives on the cell's text field**,\n  not the `NSTableCellView` — the container is not an accessibility element, so\n  an identifier set there reaches neither. Rows carry unlocalized identifiers\n  (`sidebar.casks.installed`); journeys address rows by those, never by index.\n\n## UI conventions\n\n- **Sheets, not app-modal:** every alert/confirmation uses\n  `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …` — never `runModal` (blocks\n  the app and is invisible to XCUITest). Attach to `_appDelegate.window`, NOT\n  `self.view.window` (nil under the split-view reparenting; caused a crash).\n- **Xibs are edited as XML by hand.** After editing: `xmllint --noout` the\n  file, keep new `id`s unique (prefix `cbk-…`), and grow an `NSStackView`'s\n  `visibilityPriorities`/`customSpacing` arrays when adding arranged subviews.\n  Menu items enable via Cocoa **bindings** on `currentFormula` /\n  `currentFormulaPinned` (see existing items), not `validateMenuItem:`.\n- **Localization:** UI strings go in all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/\n  Localizable.strings` files (lint with `plutil -lint`). New strings use the\n  English text in every locale until translated. Homebrew terms (\"Casks\") stay\n  untranslated. Xib menu titles are Base-internationalized literals — no\n  `.strings` churn for new menu items.\n- Icons are SF Symbols (`imageWithSystemSymbolName:`); no bundled image assets\n  for UI chrome.\n\n## Distribution & sandboxing\n\nCakebrew ships **Developer ID + hardened runtime + notarized**, and is\n**not** App-Sandboxed. `.github/workflows/release.yml` does the signing,\nnotarization and stapling on a `v*` tag (see its header for the required\nsecrets).\n\nThe sandbox question was settled by measurement, not assumption — repeat these\nif you revisit it:\n\n| Configuration | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Plain `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` | `brew` cannot even be executed: `zsh:1: operation not permitted: brew` |\n| Sandbox + `temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write` for the prefix | `brew --version` / `brew list` **work**; `/usr/local/Cellar`, `/usr/local/Caskroom`, `/Applications` are writable |\n| …but the home directory is redirected | `$HOME` becomes `~/Library/Containers/<id>/Data`, so `~/Library/Caches/Homebrew` and `~/Library/LaunchAgents` resolve **inside the container** |\n\nThat last row is the blocker: brew's download cache would diverge from the\nuser's real one (re-downloading, and disagreeing with `brew` in Terminal), and\n**`brew services` writes LaunchAgents to `~/Library/LaunchAgents`** — under the\nsandbox those land in the container where `launchd` never sees them, silently\nbreaking the Services feature. Temporary-exception entitlements are also an\nApp-Store-review mechanism (routinely rejected, and they hardcode a prefix that\ndiffers between `/usr/local` and `/opt/homebrew`), so they buy nothing for\nDeveloper ID distribution.\n\n**Therefore:** don't add `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Hardened runtime,\nno ATS weakening, a privacy manifest, and validated user input are the\nhardening this app can actually carry.\n\n## Signed local builds (helper testing)\n\nDay-to-day development needs nothing special: an Xcode build (Cmd-R) is signed\nwith your Apple Development certificate, and that **already satisfies the\nhelper's designated requirement** (it pins `anchor apple generic` plus the team\nOU, which development certs carry). So the debug build can drive the helper.\n\nTo exercise helper registration and Login Items approval for real, the app has\nto be signed and living in `/Applications`:\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-signed.sh          # Release, or pass Debug\n```\n\nIt builds into a temporary derived-data dir (so it can't leave a stale\nunsigned helper in your normal one), verifies both signatures, replaces\n`/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, and prints the next steps.\n\nA final build phase, **Verify embedded helper signature**, fails the build if\nthe embedded helper wouldn't satisfy the app's requirement — otherwise the app\nsilently refuses to talk to its own helper at runtime. It skips automatically\nfor unsigned/ad-hoc builds (CI), and declares the helper as a script input\nbecause Xcode sandboxes script phases.\n\n**Distribution certificates:** notarized releases need a *Developer ID\nApplication* certificate, which is separate from Apple Development. Until one\nexists in the account, `.github/workflows/release.yml` cannot run.\n\n## OS support policy\n\nMinimum deployment target = **latest macOS minus one** (currently 15.0);\nbuild and CI against the **latest SDK** (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned;\nCI runner tracks the newest image, currently `macos-26`). When a new major\nmacOS ships: bump the deployment target one, bump the CI runner, never pin\n`SDKROOT`. `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Known environment quirks (primary dev machine)\n\n- **`git push` hangs** on the Entire pre-push hook (session-log sync). Push\n  with `git push --no-verify` — it skips only that hook, nothing code-related.\n- **Local XCUITest works** (since 2026-08-15). It used to die at init with\n  \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\"; the cause was macOS developer\n  mode being off, fixed once with `sudo DevToolsSecurity -enable`. Being in the\n  `_developer` group is not sufficient on its own, and Accessibility permission\n  is *not* required. If that init timeout ever returns, check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. The full local run takes ~6 minutes (vs ~4s\n  for the unit suite), so it is a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one. CI remains\n  the merge gate.\n- **A locked screen blocks the whole visual toolchain**, not just XCUITest:\n  `screencapture` fails with \"could not create image from window\", and the UI\n  suite fails with `Failed to activate application … (current state: Running\n  Background)`. Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before\n  believing either. There is no way around it from a shell — the work is a\n  pre-PR step that needs the display awake.\n- **You cannot force one app to light mode from the command line.**\n  `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` in the argument domain is ignored: AppKit reads\n  the appearance from the system preference directly rather than through\n  `NSUserDefaults` precedence, so the app comes up in whatever the system is.\n  Capturing a light-mode screenshot means flipping System Settings ▸ Appearance\n  by hand.\n- Commit signing is temporarily disabled (`git -c commit.gpgsign=false`) while\n  the 1Password SSH agent is broken. Re-enable when fixed.\nname = \"cakebrew-code-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent pre-commit Cakebrew reviewer focused on correctness, regressions, Objective-C/AppKit safety, architecture, and missing tests.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"read-only\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-code-review. Review the full branch diff plus staged, unstaged, and untracked files after verification passes.\nDo not edit source files. Lead with actionable findings ordered by severity and include precise file and line evidence. Avoid style-only comments. Explicitly approve the gate when no actionable findings remain; route fixes to the implementer and require fresh verification afterward.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-implementer\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew implementation specialist for one explicitly assigned vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-implement. You own only the files and behavior explicitly assigned by the primary agent. You are not alone in the codebase: preserve unrelated work and accommodate concurrent changes without reverting them.\nObserve the focused test fail for the expected reason before production implementation, then make the minimum change, refactor green, and inspect the whole workspace diff.\nReturn changed files and red-green-refactor evidence. Do not approve your own UI, verification, or code-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs to the primary agent.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-planner\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew discovery and planning specialist for spikes, architectural decisions, and thin vertical slice definition before implementation.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-plan and stay in the planner role. Inspect and measure assumptions, compare alternatives, and return the smallest testable vertical slice with acceptance criteria and an expected red test.\nAny spike is disposable. Keep it isolated, remove it before returning, and never promote it into production code.\nDo not implement the slice, commit, push, open a PR, or modify project instructions. Preserve every unrelated change.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-ui-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent Cakebrew AppKit UI and UX reviewer for visual changes, accessibility, platform conventions, and UI journey coverage.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-ui-review. Review the implemented user-visible surface against the branch and workspace diff using the mock build and the repository's documented UI constraints.\nDo not edit source files. Return reproducible findings ordered by impact, or explicitly approve the UI gate when no actionable findings remain. Route fixes to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-verifier\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew verification specialist for warning-free Debug and Release builds, unit tests, UI target compilation, and UI journeys.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-verify and run the complete applicable verification gate from the beginning. Validate that each instrument exercised the intended target.\nDo not edit source files. Separate real failures from sandbox, signing, locked-display, and headless-CI artifacts. Return compact command evidence and an explicit pass or fail; route failures to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\n\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\napproval_policy = \"never\"\n[features]\nhooks = true\ndiff --git a/.codex/config.toml b/.codex/config.toml\nindex 3d76ec0..b28a6e6 100644\n--- a/.codex/config.toml\n+++ b/.codex/config.toml\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@\n \n+sandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\n+approval_policy = \"never\"\n [features]\n hooks = true\n@AGENTS.md\n# Contributing\n\n**[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) is the single source of truth** for how work happens\nhere — the workflow, the conventions, and the decisions already made. It is\nwritten for human contributors and coding agents alike.\n\nThis file deliberately does not restate any of it. Two documents describing one\nworkflow drift apart, and then neither can be trusted.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) once, end to end. It is not long, and most of it\n   is things that cost someone a wasted afternoon to learn.\n2. Branch off `main`, take one thin slice, write the failing test first.\n3. Open a PR with `gh`. Green CI is the merge gate.\n\n## The commands you will need every time\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests — seconds, run them every time\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests — a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one (~8 minutes)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nIf you touch `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither test command\nabove does, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n## Reporting bugs\n\nPlease use the issue templates. The environment questions are not boilerplate:\nthe macOS version, the Homebrew version and prefix, and whether the problem\nreproduces under `-BPMockBrew` are what separate a parser bug from an\nenvironment one.\n\nSecurity issues go through [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a public issue.\n---\nname: cakebrew-code-review\ndescription: Perform the independent pre-commit expert review of a Cakebrew branch and all uncommitted files after verification passes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Code Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the full branch diff against its base plus\nall staged, unstaged and untracked files. Do not edit source files.\n\nPrioritize correctness, regressions, Objective-C and AppKit idioms, memory and\nconcurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, mock fidelity and\nmissing tests. Trace the actual execution path before reporting a problem.\nAvoid style-only comments unless they reveal a concrete maintenance or defect\nrisk.\n\nReturn findings ordered by severity with precise file and line references,\nimpact and a reproducible rationale. If there are no actionable findings, say\nthe gate is approved. Any fix belongs to the implementer and invalidates the\nprior verification result.\n---\nname: cakebrew-deliver\ndescription: Commit, publish, review, merge, and clean up a fully verified Cakebrew slice when the user explicitly requests the applicable delivery actions.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Deliver\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Delivery does not grant authority: perform only the\ncommit, push, PR or merge actions the user requested.\n\n- Confirm UI review when applicable, complete verification, and expert approval\n  all correspond to the current source state.\n- Reinspect the entire workspace and stage only the intended slice. Preserve\n  unrelated modifications and untracked files.\n- Create the atomic Conventional Commit required by `AGENTS.md`, including why\n  the change exists and what the test covers.\n- Open a ready PR with `gh` only when requested, reporting all verification and\n  any genuine gaps.\n- Wait for both CI jobs and every assigned human or automated review. Never\n  bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n- Squash merge and clean up only with explicit merge authorization, including\n  the stacked-PR precaution in `AGENTS.md`.\n\nStop and report any stale gate evidence, changed head, failed CI, unresolved\nreview, authentication issue or authorization boundary.\n---\nname: cakebrew-implement\ndescription: Implement an assigned Cakebrew vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD after its scope and acceptance criteria are established.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Implement\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and accept explicit ownership of the assigned files\nor behavior. Other work may exist in the checkout; never revert or absorb it.\n\n1. Confirm the slice, acceptance criteria and intended test seam.\n2. Add the smallest focused test first and run it to observe the expected red.\n3. Implement only enough production code to make it green.\n4. Refactor with the focused test and relevant fast suite green.\n5. Inspect all staged, unstaged and untracked files and remove scratch or debug\n   artifacts created by this implementation.\n\nIf red was not observed, perform and report the mutation proof required by\n`AGENTS.md`. Stop after implementation evidence; do not self-approve the UI,\nverification or expert-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs\nto the primary agent under `$cakebrew-deliver`.\n---\nname: cakebrew-plan\ndescription: Plan or spike a Cakebrew change before implementation when requirements, architecture, framework behavior, integration boundaries, or vertical-slice scope need to be resolved.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Plan\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Stay in the planner role: do not implement\nproduction code.\n\n- Inspect the repository and trace the relevant execution paths.\n- Identify unknowns that require measurement. Use the smallest disposable\n  spike needed, keep it out of production paths, and remove it before returning.\n- Compare viable alternatives by architectural fit, complexity, performance\n  and maintenance burden.\n- Define the smallest cohesive vertical slice, its acceptance criteria, test\n  seams and expected red test.\n- Report assumptions, evidence, risks, rejected alternatives and an ordered\n  slice plan. Clearly distinguish measured behavior from inference.\n\nPreserve all unrelated changes. Do not commit, push, open a PR or change\nproject instructions.\n---\nname: cakebrew-ui-review\ndescription: Review a user-visible Cakebrew change for AppKit conventions, layout, appearance, accessibility, and UI journey coverage after implementation stabilizes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Ui Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the changed surface independently; do not\nedit source files.\n\n- Inspect the branch and workspace diff to identify affected journeys.\n- Launch against `-BPMockBrew` and verify the relevant state visually.\n- Check layout, dark mode, badges, accessibility identity and macOS idioms.\n- Prefer an existing or proposed `CakebrewUITests` assertion for behavior that\n  should not depend on visual judgment.\n- Account for the documented locked-screen and headless-CI limitations before\n  classifying a failure.\n\nReturn prioritized, reproducible findings with file or UI location and expected\nbehavior. Say explicitly when the gate passes. Send fixes back to the\nimplementer rather than making them in the review pass.\n---\nname: cakebrew-verify\ndescription: Run Cakebrew's complete local verification gate after implementation or fixes, including required builds, tests, warnings checks, and UI-target coverage.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Verify\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and use its current commands as the authority. Do\nnot edit source files.\n\nRun the gate from the beginning in its documented order:\n\n1. Debug build, warning-free.\n2. Release build, warning-free.\n3. Full unit suite.\n4. UI test target compilation.\n5. UI journeys before PR creation, subject to the documented display checks.\n\nValidate that each instrument actually exercised the intended target; do not\ntreat an exit code or silent output alone as proof. Separate genuine product or\nsetup failures from sandbox, signing, locked-screen or headless-environment\nartifacts. Return a compact command-by-command result with decisive diagnostics\nand an explicit pass/fail. Route failures to the implementer.\n---\nname: cakebrew-workflow\ndescription: Orchestrate a complete Cakebrew feature, bugfix, or behavior-changing refactor through planning, TDD implementation, UI review when applicable, verification, expert review, and delivery gates.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Workflow\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely before acting. It is the sole authority for the\nworkflow, commands, architecture and delivery rules; this skill only routes the\nwork through the configured roles.\n\n1. Use `cakebrew-planner` with `$cakebrew-plan` to validate assumptions and\n   define the smallest vertical slice. Any spike is disposable.\n2. Inspect repository state, synchronize the latest `main`, preserve unrelated\n   changes, and create the required branch before production edits.\n3. Give `cakebrew-implementer` explicit ownership of the slice and invoke\n   `$cakebrew-implement`. Require observed red-green-refactor evidence.\n4. If a user-visible surface changed, run `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` with\n   `$cakebrew-ui-review` after implementation is stable.\n5. Run `cakebrew-verifier` with `$cakebrew-verify`, then\n   `cakebrew-code-reviewer` with `$cakebrew-code-review`.\n6. Route every actionable finding back to the implementer. After any source\n   edit, repeat verification and expert review; repeat UI review too when the\n   fix changes the visible surface.\n7. Keep delivery with the primary agent. Invoke `$cakebrew-deliver` only when\n   the user has authorized the relevant commit, push, PR or merge action.\n\nDo not run a mutating agent concurrently with a verifier or reviewer against\nthe same worktree. Use isolated worktrees when independent passes truly need to\nrun in parallel. Return concise role summaries and evidence to the primary\nagent instead of raw logs.\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Code Review\"\n  short_description: \"Independently review the full Cakebrew diff\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-code-review to perform the pre-commit expert review of this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Deliver\"\n  short_description: \"Deliver a verified Cakebrew slice safely\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-deliver to perform the authorized delivery steps for this verified Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Implement\"\n  short_description: \"Implement one Cakebrew slice with strict TDD\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-implement to implement this scoped Cakebrew slice with red-green-refactor evidence.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Plan\"\n  short_description: \"Plan and de-risk a Cakebrew vertical slice\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-plan to investigate and scope this Cakebrew change before implementation.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew UI Review\"\n  short_description: \"Review Cakebrew UI, UX, and accessibility\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-ui-review to review the user-visible changes in this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Verify\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's complete verification gate\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-verify to verify the current Cakebrew branch from the start.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Workflow\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's gated engineering lifecycle\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-workflow to deliver this Cakebrew change through every required gate.\"\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":3429},"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 10354)\nTotal output lines: 777\n\n# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew (Objective-C / AppKit).\n\n**This file is the single source of truth** for how work happens in this repo —\nconventions, architecture decisions, and the delivery workflow. It is written\nfor coding agents and human contributors alike. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to it.\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` is a short human-facing entry point that links here rather\nthan restating anything. Do not add a second workflow, roadmap, or status\ndocument: issues are the live status, and a second instruction file drifts.\n\nFollow this exactly; it encodes decisions the maintainer has already made, and\nmeasurements that cost real time to obtain.\n\n---\n\n## The workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype & spike] --> B[2. Inspect & branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. TDD: red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX review gate]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification gate]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Expert code review gate]\n    I -- Findings or changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated merge, linear history]\n```\n\n### Phase 0 — Discovery\n\n#### 1. Plan, prototype and spike\n\n**Spike before you commit to a design.** Explore the problem, then build the\nsmallest throwaway prototype that can test framework assumptions, feasibility,\nintegration boundaries, edge cases or performance risks. Never assume a\nthird-party API behaves as documented. Two examples from this repo's history:\nthe App Sandbox question was settled by measuring what actually broke, and\nlayered app-icon support was settled by feeding `actool` a probe entry and\nreading the warning. Both answers were the opposite of the documentation-level\nguess.\n\nWeigh alternatives on architectural fit, complexity, performance and\nmaintenance burden. Then break the design into an ordered list of thin vertical\nslices, and **throw the prototype away** — production code is rebuilt under\nTDD, not promoted from a spike. Clear all spike code and scratch artifacts from\nthe workspace before implementation begins.\n\n### Phase 1 — Context and scoping\n\n#### 2. Inspect and branch\n\n**Inspect before mutating.** Read the repo state, branches, and working tree\nfirst. Preserve unrelated staged, unstaged and untracked changes — this machine\nroutinely carries modifications under `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/` that\nare not yours to commit or discard. Stash them around a rebase; never\n`git checkout --` a file you did not write.\n\n**Branch off latest `main`.** Prefixes: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`,\n`chore/`, `test/`, `perf/`. Never commit to `main` directly.\n\n#### 3. Scope a thin vertical slice\n\n**Take one thin vertical slice** — the smallest cohesive end-to-end outcome\nthat can be tested, reviewed and shipped on its own. One PR per logical unit of\nwork; unrelated changes go on their own branch. A horizontal layer spanning the\nwhole app is not a slice.\n\n### Phase 2 — Test-driven implementation\n\n#### 4. Red, green, refactor\n\n**Red → green → refactor, and the red is not optional.** Write the smallest\nfailing test first and confirm it fails *for the expected reason* — a compile\nerror on a not-yet-existing API counts, a crash in the runner does not. Then\nthe minimum code to pass. Then clean up with the suite green.\n\nThe test and the code that satisfies it land in the **same commit**, and the\ncommit message names what the test covers. Every behaviour change ships with a\ntest written first: bugs, features, behaviour-changing refactors. Only pure\nformatting and documentation are exempt.\n\nIf you write the test and the implementation together and never observe the\nred, you have not done TDD — **prove the test bites** by mutation instead:\nbreak the implementation deliberately, confirm the suite fails, and restore it.\nSay which of the two you did in the PR.\n\n#### 5. Inspect the workspace diff\n\n**Then inspect the whole workspace diff**, including untracked files\n(`git status --untracked-files=all`). Remove scratch files, probes and\ndebugging artifacts. Temporary instrumentation must not reach a commit.\n\n### Phase 3 — Quality gates\n\nUse distinct reviewer roles or separate passes so that planning, implementation,\nUI review, verification and expert code review do not collapse into one\nself-review. Give reviewers the diff and observed evidence, not merely the\nimplementation intent. Run the gates in order. **If any gate causes a code\nchange, restart from the verification gate and obtain fresh code review\napproval** — a fix invalidates every result that came before it.\n\n#### 6. UI/UX and design review\n\n**UI/UX review** *(only if a user-facing surface changed)*. Verify it visually:\nlaunch the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`), look at it, confirm layout, dark mode,\nand badges. For behaviour rather than looks, add a `CakebrewUITests` journey —\nan assertion beats eyeballing a screenshot. Check accessibility and platform\nidioms, not just that it renders.\n\n#### 7. Verification and automated checks\n\n**Verification.** Build **Debug and Release**, both warning-free — a new\nwarning is a failure. Run the full unit suite. Compile the UI test target.\nRun the UI journeys before opening the PR. Treat every warning or failing check\nas a finding; after any code edit, rerun the full verification sequence from\nthe beginning.\n\n#### 8. Pre-commit expert code review\n\n**Code review** over the full branch diff plus anything uncommitted. Language\nidioms, memory and concurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases.\nResolve every actionable finding, then re-verify and repeat the review until it\nis approved.\n\n### Phase 4 — Integration and delivery\n\n#### 9. Structured atomic commit\n\n**Conventional Commits.** `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`, where type\nis one of `feat` `fix` `refactor` `docs` `chore` `test` `perf` `build` `ci`\nand scope names the area (`sidebar`, `reload`, `toolbar`, `brewfile`, `l10n`,\n…). The body explains *why*, and names what the test covers. History before\nthis convention was adopted is left alone; do not rewrite it.\n\nCommit only the reviewed and verified slice. The test and production code for a\nbehaviour land together in the same atomic commit.\n\n#### 10. Pull request\n\n**Open a PR with the `gh` CLI**, never the web UI, and never a draft unless\nasked. Describe what changed, why, and how it was tested — including what you\ncould *not* verify and the reason.\n\n#### 11. Gated merge and linear history\n\n**Green CI is the merge gate.** Both jobs (Build & Test, UI Tests) must pass.\nNever merge on pending or failing checks. If a UI test fails on CI, read the\nfailure and the `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the job log before assuming a\nflake — re-run once only when the evidence says infrastructure. If a human or\nautomated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass an assigned\nreview.\n\n**Merge and clean up:** `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch` (squash is\nthe maintainer's explicit choice, and keeps history linear and bisectable),\nthen `git checkout main`, `git pull --ff-only`.\n\n> **Stacked PRs:** retarget a child branch's base to `main` *before* merging its\n> parent. Deleting a merged branch auto-closes any PR still based on it, and\n> GitHub will not reopen a PR whose base is gone.\n\n### Quick reference\n\n| Stage | Pass criteria |\n| --- | --- |\n| Discovery | Feasibility measured, alternatives weighed, vertical slices ordered, spike discarded |\n| Setup | Repository inspected, unrelated work preserved, branch created from latest `main` |\n| Scope | Smallest independently shippable end-to-end outcome selected |\n| TDD | Focused test observed red, minimal implementation green, refactor stays green |\n| Diff | Staged, unstaged and untracked files inspected; no scratch or debug artifacts |\n| UI review | Changed surfaces checked for layout, appearance, accessibility and platform idioms |\n| Verification | Debug and Release warning-free; unit suite, UI target and UI journeys pass |\n| Code review | Full branch and workspace diff reviewed; all findings resolved; verification repeated after edits |\n| Delivery | Atomic Conventional Commit, ready PR, assigned approvals and both CI jobs green |\n| Merge | Squash merge completed, branch deleted, local `main` fast-forwarded |\n\n### Configured skills and subagents\n\nFor every feature, bugfix or behaviour-changing refactor, the primary agent\ninvokes `$cakebrew-workflow` and coordinates the configured roles below. The\nprimary agent owns requirements, cross-role decisions and delivery; subagents\nreturn bounded evidence and never silently expand their assignment.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary agent | Route the slice through every applicable phase and repeat invalidated gates |\n| Planner / architect | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measure unknowns, compare alternatives and define the vertical slice before implementation |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Own explicitly assigned files and produce red-green-refactor evidence |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Independently review every user-visible change after implementation stabilizes |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Run the complete applicable verification sequence without editing source |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Independently review the full branch and workspace diff after verification passes |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary agent | Perform only the commit, PR or merge actions the user authorized |\n\nRun the mutating implementer separately from gate agents. UI review,\nverification and code review do not fix their own findings: send them back to\nthe implementer, then rerun every invalidated gate. If independent work must run\nin parallel, give each agent an isolated worktree and explicit file ownership.\n\nThe skill and custom-agent files are routing adapters, not additional sources\nof project policy. They must defer to this file. Change workflow rules here\nfirst and keep the adapters narrow.\n\n---\n\n## Principles for agents working here\n\n**Prototypes are disposable.** Spike code exists to answer a question. Once\nanswered, delete it and rebuild under TDD. Do not promote a spike.\n\n**Separate the roles.** Planner, implementer, UI reviewer, verifier and code\nreviewer are different jobs with different biases. One prompt wearing all five\nhats reviews its own work and finds it good. Use separate agents or separate\npasses, and give the reviewer the diff rather than the intent.\n\n**Validate the instrument before believing its silence.** A probe that prints\nnothing has not proven anything — it may not have run, may have hit a stale\nbinary, or may have been filtered out. Confirm the probe executed and observed\nwhat you think it did. Concretely, in this repo: `xcodebuild analyze` exits 0\neven with findings; `xcodebuild -scheme Cakebrew` never compiles the UI test\ntarget; `xcodebuild test` output has to be *grepped for the assertion*, not\njust for `TEST SUCCEEDED`; and a `log show` predicate that matches nothing\nlooks exactly like a feature that did not fire.\n\n**Isolate shared state when working in parallel.** Agents on one machine\ncontend for real singletons: the Xcode derived-data directory, the app's\n`NSUserDefaults` domain, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, the running app itself,\nand the display. Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or\nreview pass is running against it; use a git worktree instead.\n\n**One source of truth.** This file. Pointer files may reference it; nothing may\nrestate it.\n\n---\n\n## Build & test commands\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests (fast, hermetic — run these locally every time)\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests (own scheme; needs ad-hoc signing; on CI this is authoritative)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nAfter touching `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither command above\ndoes, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs both jobs on `macos-26` (latest SDK),\nbuilds Debug and Release, runs the static analyzer, and uploads crash logs from\n`~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` on failure. A separate weekly\n`brew-compat.yml` pushes the runner's *real* brew output through the parsers,\nbecause fixture tests cannot notice upstream drift.\n\n## Testing architecture\n\n- **Mock brew:** launching the app with `-BPMockBrew` swaps\n  `BPHomebrewInterface` for `BPMockHomebrewInterface` (resolved in\n  `+sharedInterface`). The mock serves deterministic fixtures (`mockwget`,\n  `mockgit` [pinned], `mockchrome`/`mockvscode` [casks], …) and stubs every\n  operation as a no-op. **Any new interface method must get a mock override**\n  so UI tests never shell out to real brew — `BPMockFidelityTests` fails if a\n  mutating selector lacks one. Opt-in launch flags shape it for a specific\n  journey: `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`, `-BPMockEmptyCleanup`, and\n  `-BPMockSlowCatalog` (holds the catalog calls so progress and cancel are\n  observable; the mock is otherwise instant, which makes both unobservable).\n- **Unit tests** (`CakebrewTests/`) cover parsers, model logic, and manager\n  state. The `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` parsers are the standard TDD seam:\n  pure input → `BPFormula` output. Private classes are re-declared in the test\n  file to reach them.\n- **UI tests** (`CakebrewUITests/`) are journey tests against the mock. The\n  shared launch helper waits for the initial load to settle (mockwget rendered)\n  before navigating — do not remove that; it closes a reselect race in\n  `homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`. Sidebar item *names* repeat across groups\n  (two \"Installed\", two \"Outdated\"), so journeys address rows by their stable\n  unlocalized identifier — `[self sidebarRow:@\"sidebar.casks.installed\"]` —\n  never by index. Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` (pinned rows carry\n  a pin glyph after the name).\n- **CI environment limits:** the headless runner's window is never key, so\n  typing/keyboard focus is untestable; system file panels (NSSave/NSOpenPanel)\n  are out-of-process and undrivable. Pattern: unit-test the logic, UI-test\n  presence/navigation.\n- **Five ways a green local UI run lies.** Each of these has cost a red CI run\n  or a misdiagnosed failure:\n  1. **`isHittable` is meaningless on CI.** Hit testing needs a key window, so\n     it reports `false` there whatever is on screen. Assert with `exists` and\n     geometry (`frame.size`) instead.\n  2. **Anything persisted that changes the launch view makes the suite\n     order-dependent.** Each journey would inherit whatever the last one left\n     stored. Pin it in the shared launch helper through the argument domain,\n     which outranks stored defaults — `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1`,\n     `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"` — never by special-casing `-BPMockBrew` in\n     the app, so the feature itself stays exercised.\n  3. **Neither verify command compiles the UI test target.** `-scheme Cakebrew`\n     and `-scheme CakebrewTests` both skip `CakebrewUITests.m`, so a syntax\n     error there passes locally and fails on CI. After touching it, run\n     `xcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests …` — about two\n     seconds, versus ~7 minutes for the suite.\n  4. **An alert's buttons are mirrored to the Touch Bar.** `self.app.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`\n     therefore matches twice, and `firstMatch` can resolve to the mirror, which\n     is not clickable (\"cannot be called with Touch Bar elements\"). Scope to the\n     sheet: `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons[@\"Yes\"]`. Note that\n     `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` tolerates a multi-match query, so an\n     existing assertion passing is not evidence that clicking will work.\n  5. **A locked screen breaks the suite in ways that look like product bugs.**\n     XCUITest cannot drive a locked display: clicks resolve to infinite points\n     and elements never become interactable. Before believing a UI failure that\n     appeared without a related change, check\n     `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked`; `screencapture` failing with\n     \"could not create image from window\" is the same signal. A ~7 minute run\n     can also be killed part-way by display sleep.\n\n## Architecture crib sheet\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` — all brew execution funnels through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async, streams) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may be nil — always\n  guard (`invokeOutputBlock:withString:`), passing nil is legitimate.\n- **List pattern:** a `BPListMode` enum case → a `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall`\n  subclass (arguments + line parser) → a `BPHomebrewManager` property fetched\n  in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:` → a sidebar item + badge → a mock\n  fixture. Follow this groove for any new list.\n- **Casks** are `BPFormula` instances with `cask == YES` (set by the cask list\n  parsers; survives copy/coding). Operations dispatch on that flag in\n  `BPInstallationWindowController` (`--cask` variants). `statusForFormula:` is\n  namespace-aware — casks read only the cask lists.\n- **Slow catalogs** (`brew formulae`, `brew casks`) share a 24h disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). `brew casks` can take 80+ s cold.\n- **Sidebar:** `FormulaeSideBarItem` enum values are **outline row indices**\n  (groups included). Inserting a row renumbers everything after it — update\n  the enum, the View-menu item tags in `MainMenu.xib`, and check every\n  `switch`/comparison on the enum (`grep -rn FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- **Never `reloadData` the sidebar to refresh a badge.** Reloading an\n  `NSOutlineView` clears its selection, and `-configureSidebarSettings` restores\n  the user's last row at setup — so a stray reload silently drops it, and the\n  next `sourceListSelectionDidChange` persists whatever replaced it. Use\n  `-refreshBadgeForListMode:`, which redraws one row with `reloadItem:`. The\n  old all-at-once reload got away with it only because it ran inside\n  `-homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`, which reselects immediately afterwards.\n- **Row identity for VoiceOver and XCUITest lives on the cell's text field**,\n  not the `NSTableCellView` — the container is not an accessibility element, so\n  an identifier set there reaches neither. Rows carry unlocalized identifiers\n  (`sidebar.casks.installed`); journeys address rows by those, never by index.\n\n## UI conventions\n\n- **Sheets, not app-modal:** every alert/confirmation uses\n  `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …` — never `runModal` (blocks\n  the app and is invisible to XCUITest). Attach to `_appDelegate.window`, NOT\n  `self.view.window` (nil under the split-view reparenting; caused a crash).\n- **Xibs are edited as XML by hand.** After editing: `xmllint --noout` the\n  file, keep new `id`s unique (prefix `cbk-…`), and grow an `NSStackView`'s\n  `visibilityPriorities`/`customSp…354 tokens truncated…, so `~/Library/Caches/Homebrew` and `~/Library/LaunchAgents` resolve **inside the container** |\n\nThat last row is the blocker: brew's download cache would diverge from the\nuser's real one (re-downloading, and disagreeing with `brew` in Terminal), and\n**`brew services` writes LaunchAgents to `~/Library/LaunchAgents`** — under the\nsandbox those land in the container where `launchd` never sees them, silently\nbreaking the Services feature. Temporary-exception entitlements are also an\nApp-Store-review mechanism (routinely rejected, and they hardcode a prefix that\ndiffers between `/usr/local` and `/opt/homebrew`), so they buy nothing for\nDeveloper ID distribution.\n\n**Therefore:** don't add `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Hardened runtime,\nno ATS weakening, a privacy manifest, and validated user input are the\nhardening this app can actually carry.\n\n## Signed local builds (helper testing)\n\nDay-to-day development needs nothing special: an Xcode build (Cmd-R) is signed\nwith your Apple Development certificate, and that **already satisfies the\nhelper's designated requirement** (it pins `anchor apple generic` plus the team\nOU, which development certs carry). So the debug build can drive the helper.\n\nTo exercise helper registration and Login Items approval for real, the app has\nto be signed and living in `/Applications`:\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-signed.sh          # Release, or pass Debug\n```\n\nIt builds into a temporary derived-data dir (so it can't leave a stale\nunsigned helper in your normal one), verifies both signatures, replaces\n`/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, and prints the next steps.\n\nA final build phase, **Verify embedded helper signature**, fails the build if\nthe embedded helper wouldn't satisfy the app's requirement — otherwise the app\nsilently refuses to talk to its own helper at runtime. It skips automatically\nfor unsigned/ad-hoc builds (CI), and declares the helper as a script input\nbecause Xcode sandboxes script phases.\n\n**Distribution certificates:** notarized releases need a *Developer ID\nApplication* certificate, which is separate from Apple Development. Until one\nexists in the account, `.github/workflows/release.yml` cannot run.\n\n## OS support policy\n\nMinimum deployment target = **latest macOS minus one** (currently 15.0);\nbuild and CI against the **latest SDK** (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned;\nCI runner tracks the newest image, currently `macos-26`). When a new major\nmacOS ships: bump the deployment target one, bump the CI runner, never pin\n`SDKROOT`. `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Known environment quirks (primary dev machine)\n\n- **`git push` hangs** on the Entire pre-push hook (session-log sync). Push\n  with `git push --no-verify` — it skips only that hook, nothing code-related.\n- **Local XCUITest works** (since 2026-08-15). It used to die at init with\n  \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\"; the cause was macOS developer\n  mode being off, fixed once with `sudo DevToolsSecurity -enable`. Being in the\n  `_developer` group is not sufficient on its own, and Accessibility permission\n  is *not* required. If that init timeout ever returns, check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. The full local run takes ~6 minutes (vs ~4s\n  for the unit suite), so it is a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one. CI remains\n  the merge gate.\n- **A locked screen blocks the whole visual toolchain**, not just XCUITest:\n  `screencapture` fails with \"could not create image from window\", and the UI\n  suite fails with `Failed to activate application … (current state: Running\n  Background)`. Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before\n  believing either. There is no way around it from a shell — the work is a\n  pre-PR step that needs the display awake.\n- **You cannot force one app to light mode from the command line.**\n  `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` in the argument domain is ignored: AppKit reads\n  the appearance from the system preference directly rather than through\n  `NSUserDefaults` precedence, so the app comes up in whatever the system is.\n  Capturing a light-mode screenshot means flipping System Settings ▸ Appearance\n  by hand.\n- Commit signing is temporarily disabled (`git -c commit.gpgsign=false`) while\n  the 1Password SSH agent is broken. Re-enable when fixed.\nname = \"cakebrew-code-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent pre-commit Cakebrew reviewer focused on correctness, regressions, Objective-C/AppKit safety, architecture, and missing tests.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"read-only\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-code-review. Review the full branch diff plus staged, unstaged, and untracked files after verification passes.\nDo not edit source files. Lead with actionable findings ordered by severity and include precise file and line evidence. Avoid style-only comments. Explicitly approve the gate when no actionable findings remain; route fixes to the implementer and require fresh verification afterward.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-implementer\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew implementation specialist for one explicitly assigned vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-implement. You own only the files and behavior explicitly assigned by the primary agent. You are not alone in the codebase: preserve unrelated work and accommodate concurrent changes without reverting them.\nObserve the focused test fail for the expected reason before production implementation, then make the minimum change, refactor green, and inspect the whole workspace diff.\nReturn changed files and red-green-refactor evidence. Do not approve your own UI, verification, or code-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs to the primary agent.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-planner\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew discovery and planning specialist for spikes, architectural decisions, and thin vertical slice definition before implementation.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-plan and stay in the planner role. Inspect and measure assumptions, compare alternatives, and return the smallest testable vertical slice with acceptance criteria and an expected red test.\nAny spike is disposable. Keep it isolated, remove it before returning, and never promote it into production code.\nDo not implement the slice, commit, push, open a PR, or modify project instructions. Preserve every unrelated change.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-ui-reviewer\"\ndescription = \"Independent Cakebrew AppKit UI and UX reviewer for visual changes, accessibility, platform conventions, and UI journey coverage.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-ui-review. Review the implemented user-visible surface against the branch and workspace diff using the mock build and the repository's documented UI constraints.\nDo not edit source files. Return reproducible findings ordered by impact, or explicitly approve the UI gate when no actionable findings remain. Route fixes to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\nname = \"cakebrew-verifier\"\ndescription = \"Cakebrew verification specialist for warning-free Debug and Release builds, unit tests, UI target compilation, and UI journeys.\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\ndeveloper_instructions = \"\"\"\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\nInvoke $cakebrew-verify and run the complete applicable verification gate from the beginning. Validate that each instrument exercised the intended target.\nDo not edit source files. Separate real failures from sandbox, signing, locked-display, and headless-CI artifacts. Return compact command evidence and an explicit pass or fail; route failures to the implementer.\n\"\"\"\n\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\napproval_policy = \"never\"\n[features]\nhooks = true\ndiff --git a/.codex/config.toml b/.codex/config.toml\nindex 3d76ec0..b28a6e6 100644\n--- a/.codex/config.toml\n+++ b/.codex/config.toml\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@\n \n+sandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\n+approval_policy = \"never\"\n [features]\n hooks = true\n@AGENTS.md\n# Contributing\n\n**[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) is the single source of truth** for how work happens\nhere — the workflow, the conventions, and the decisions already made. It is\nwritten for human contributors and coding agents alike.\n\nThis file deliberately does not restate any of it. Two documents describing one\nworkflow drift apart, and then neither can be trusted.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) once, end to end. It is not long, and most of it\n   is things that cost someone a wasted afternoon to learn.\n2. Branch off `main`, take one thin slice, write the failing test first.\n3. Open a PR with `gh`. Green CI is the merge gate.\n\n## The commands you will need every time\n\n```sh\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# Unit tests — seconds, run them every time\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n# UI tests — a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one (~8 minutes)\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\nIf you touch `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither test command\nabove does, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n## Reporting bugs\n\nPlease use the issue templates. The environment questions are not boilerplate:\nthe macOS version, the Homebrew version and prefix, and whether the problem\nreproduces under `-BPMockBrew` are what separate a parser bug from an\nenvironment one.\n\nSecurity issues go through [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a public issue.\n---\nname: cakebrew-code-review\ndescription: Perform the independent pre-commit expert review of a Cakebrew branch and all uncommitted files after verification passes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Code Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the full branch diff against its base plus\nall staged, unstaged and untracked files. Do not edit source files.\n\nPrioritize correctness, regressions, Objective-C and AppKit idioms, memory and\nconcurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, mock fidelity and\nmissing tests. Trace the actual execution path before reporting a problem.\nAvoid style-only comments unless they reveal a concrete maintenance or defect\nrisk.\n\nReturn findings ordered by severity with precise file and line references,\nimpact and a reproducible rationale. If there are no actionable findings, say\nthe gate is approved. Any fix belongs to the implementer and invalidates the\nprior verification result.\n---\nname: cakebrew-deliver\ndescription: Commit, publish, review, merge, and clean up a fully verified Cakebrew slice when the user explicitly requests the applicable delivery actions.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Deliver\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Delivery does not grant authority: perform only the\ncommit, push, PR or merge actions the user requested.\n\n- Confirm UI review when applicable, complete verification, and expert approval\n  all correspond to the current source state.\n- Reinspect the entire workspace and stage only the intended slice. Preserve\n  unrelated modifications and untracked files.\n- Create the atomic Conventional Commit required by `AGENTS.md`, including why\n  the change exists and what the test covers.\n- Open a ready PR with `gh` only when requested, reporting all verification and\n  any genuine gaps.\n- Wait for both CI jobs and every assigned human or automated review. Never\n  bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n- Squash merge and clean up only with explicit merge authorization, including\n  the stacked-PR precaution in `AGENTS.md`.\n\nStop and report any stale gate evidence, changed head, failed CI, unresolved\nreview, authentication issue or authorization boundary.\n---\nname: cakebrew-implement\ndescription: Implement an assigned Cakebrew vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD after its scope and acceptance criteria are established.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Implement\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and accept explicit ownership of the assigned files\nor behavior. Other work may exist in the checkout; never revert or absorb it.\n\n1. Confirm the slice, acceptance criteria and intended test seam.\n2. Add the smallest focused test first and run it to observe the expected red.\n3. Implement only enough production code to make it green.\n4. Refactor with the focused test and relevant fast suite green.\n5. Inspect all staged, unstaged and untracked files and remove scratch or debug\n   artifacts created by this implementation.\n\nIf red was not observed, perform and report the mutation proof required by\n`AGENTS.md`. Stop after implementation evidence; do not self-approve the UI,\nverification or expert-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs\nto the primary agent under `$cakebrew-deliver`.\n---\nname: cakebrew-plan\ndescription: Plan or spike a Cakebrew change before implementation when requirements, architecture, framework behavior, integration boundaries, or vertical-slice scope need to be resolved.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Plan\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Stay in the planner role: do not implement\nproduction code.\n\n- Inspect the repository and trace the relevant execution paths.\n- Identify unknowns that require measurement. Use the smallest disposable\n  spike needed, keep it out of production paths, and remove it before returning.\n- Compare viable alternatives by architectural fit, complexity, performance\n  and maintenance burden.\n- Define the smallest cohesive vertical slice, its acceptance criteria, test\n  seams and expected red test.\n- Report assumptions, evidence, risks, rejected alternatives and an ordered\n  slice plan. Clearly distinguish measured behavior from inference.\n\nPreserve all unrelated changes. Do not commit, push, open a PR or change\nproject instructions.\n---\nname: cakebrew-ui-review\ndescription: Review a user-visible Cakebrew change for AppKit conventions, layout, appearance, accessibility, and UI journey coverage after implementation stabilizes.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Ui Review\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the changed surface independently; do not\nedit source files.\n\n- Inspect the branch and workspace diff to identify affected journeys.\n- Launch against `-BPMockBrew` and verify the relevant state visually.\n- Check layout, dark mode, badges, accessibility identity and macOS idioms.\n- Prefer an existing or proposed `CakebrewUITests` assertion for behavior that\n  should not depend on visual judgment.\n- Account for the documented locked-screen and headless-CI limitations before\n  classifying a failure.\n\nReturn prioritized, reproducible findings with file or UI location and expected\nbehavior. Say explicitly when the gate passes. Send fixes back to the\nimplementer rather than making them in the review pass.\n---\nname: cakebrew-verify\ndescription: Run Cakebrew's complete local verification gate after implementation or fixes, including required builds, tests, warnings checks, and UI-target coverage.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Verify\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and use its current commands as the authority. Do\nnot edit source files.\n\nRun the gate from the beginning in its documented order:\n\n1. Debug build, warning-free.\n2. Release build, warning-free.\n3. Full unit suite.\n4. UI test target compilation.\n5. UI journeys before PR creation, subject to the documented display checks.\n\nValidate that each instrument actually exercised the intended target; do not\ntreat an exit code or silent output alone as proof. Separate genuine product or\nsetup failures from sandbox, signing, locked-screen or headless-environment\nartifacts. Return a compact command-by-command result with decisive diagnostics\nand an explicit pass/fail. Route failures to the implementer.\n---\nname: cakebrew-workflow\ndescription: Orchestrate a complete Cakebrew feature, bugfix, or behavior-changing refactor through planning, TDD implementation, UI review when applicable, verification, expert review, and delivery gates.\n---\n\n# Cakebrew Workflow\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely before acting. It is the sole authority for the\nworkflow, commands, architecture and delivery rules; this skill only routes the\nwork through the configured roles.\n\n1. Use `cakebrew-planner` with `$cakebrew-plan` to validate assumptions and\n   define the smallest vertical slice. Any spike is disposable.\n2. Inspect repository state, synchronize the latest `main`, preserve unrelated\n   changes, and create the required branch before production edits.\n3. Give `cakebrew-implementer` explicit ownership of the slice and invoke\n   `$cakebrew-implement`. Require observed red-green-refactor evidence.\n4. If a user-visible surface changed, run `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` with\n   `$cakebrew-ui-review` after implementation is stable.\n5. Run `cakebrew-verifier` with `$cakebrew-verify`, then\n   `cakebrew-code-reviewer` with `$cakebrew-code-review`.\n6. Route every actionable finding back to the implementer. After any source\n   edit, repeat verification and expert review; repeat UI review too when the\n   fix changes the visible surface.\n7. Keep delivery with the primary agent. Invoke `$cakebrew-deliver` only when\n   the user has authorized the relevant commit, push, PR or merge action.\n\nDo not run a mutating agent concurrently with a verifier or reviewer against\nthe same worktree. Use isolated worktrees when independent passes truly need to\nrun in parallel. Return concise role summaries and evidence to the primary\nagent instead of raw logs.\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Code Review\"\n  short_description: \"Independently review the full Cakebrew diff\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-code-review to perform the pre-commit expert review of this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Deliver\"\n  short_description: \"Deliver a verified Cakebrew slice safely\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-deliver to perform the authorized delivery steps for this verified Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Implement\"\n  short_description: \"Implement one Cakebrew slice with strict TDD\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-implement to implement this scoped Cakebrew slice with red-green-refactor evidence.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Plan\"\n  short_description: \"Plan and de-risk a Cakebrew vertical slice\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-plan to investigate and scope this Cakebrew change before implementation.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew UI Review\"\n  short_description: \"Review Cakebrew UI, UX, and accessibility\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-ui-review to review the user-visible changes in this Cakebrew branch.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Verify\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's complete verification gate\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-verify to verify the current Cakebrew branch from the start.\"\ninterface:\n  display_name: \"Cakebrew Workflow\"\n  short_description: \"Run Cakebrew's gated engineering lifecycle\"\n  default_prompt: \"Use $cakebrew-workflow to deliver this Cakebrew change through every required gate.\"\n"},"started_at_ms":1788831442461,"completed_at_ms":1788831442461}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:37:22.581Z","ordinal":22081,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a07ea9-a694-7392-9c71-c1c21de29524","call_id":"call_mV2gjA4Lgh10klRo4PbiHM2l","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 10696)\nTotal output lines: 5\n\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn30view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents.md: (400) Unsupported content-type: text/markdown\n\n{\"chunk_id\":\"af792c\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000003429,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":10354,\"output\":\"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\\n\\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew (Objective-C / AppKit).\\n\\n**This file is the single source of truth** for how work happens in this repo —\\nconventions, architecture decisions, and the delivery workflow. It is written\\nfor coding agents and human contributors alike. `CLAUDE.md` is a pointer to it.\\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` is a short human-facing entry point that links here rather\\nthan restating anything. Do not add a second workflow, roadmap, or status\\ndocument: issues are the live status, and a second instruction file drifts.\\n\\nFollow this exactly; it encodes decisions the maintainer has already made, and\\nmeasurements that cost real time to obtain.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## The workflow\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    A[1. Plan, prototype & spike] --> B[2. Inspect & branch]\\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\\n    C --> D[4. TDD: red, green, refactor]\\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX review gate]\\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification gate]\\n    G --> H\\n    H --> I[8. Expert code review gate]\\n    I -- Findings or changes --> D\\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\\n    J --> K[10. Pull request]\\n    K --> L[11. Gated merge, linear history]\\n```\\n\\n### Phase 0 — Discovery\\n\\n#### 1. Plan, prototype and spike\\n\\n**Spike before you commit to a design.** Explore the problem, then build the\\nsmallest throwaway prototype that can test framework assumptions, feasibility,\\nintegration boundaries, edge cases or performance risks. Never assume a\\nthird-party API behaves as documented. Two examples from this repo's history:\\nthe App Sandbox question was settled by measuring what actually broke, and\\nlayered app-icon support was settled by feeding `actool` a probe entry and\\nreading the warning. Both answers were the opposite of the documentation-level\\nguess.\\n\\nWeigh alternatives on architectural fit, complexity, performance and\\nmaintenance burden. Then break the design into an ordered list of thin vertical\\nslices, and **throw the prototype away** — production code is rebuilt under\\nTDD, not promoted from a spike. Clear all spike code and scratch artifacts from\\nthe workspace before implementation begins.\\n\\n### Phase 1 — Context and scoping\\n\\n#### 2. Inspect and branch\\n\\n**Inspect before mutating.** Read the repo state, branches, and working tree\\nfirst. Preserve unrelated staged, unstaged and untracked changes — this machine\\nroutinely carries modifications under `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/` that\\nare not yours to commit or discard. Stash them around a rebase; never\\n`git checkout --` a file you did not write.\\n\\n**Branch off latest `main`.** Prefixes: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`,\\n`chore/`, `test/`, `perf/`. Never commit to `main` directly.\\n\\n#### 3. Scope a thin vertical slice\\n\\n**Take one thin vertical slice** — the smallest cohesive end-to-end outcome\\nthat can be tested, reviewed and shipped on its own. One PR per logical unit of\\nwork; unrelated changes go on their own branch. A horizontal layer spanning the\\nwhole app is not a slice.\\n\\n### Phase 2 — Test-driven implementation\\n\\n#### 4. Red, green, refactor\\n\\n**Red → green → refactor, and the red is not optional.** Write the smallest\\nfailing test first and confirm it fails *for the expected reason* — a compile\\nerror on a not-yet-existing API counts, a crash in the runner does not. Then\\nthe minimum code to pass. Then clean up with the suite green.\\n\\nThe test and the code that satisfies it land in the **same commit**, and the\\ncommit message names what the test covers. Every behaviour change ships with a\\ntest written first: bugs, features, behaviour-changing refactors. Only pure\\nformatting and documentation are exempt.\\n\\nIf you write the test and the implementation together and never observe the\\nred, you have not done TDD — **prove the test bites** by mutation instead:\\nbreak the implementation deliberately, confirm the suite fails, and restore it.\\nSay which of the two you did in the PR.\\n\\n#### 5. Inspect the workspace diff\\n\\n**Then inspect the whole workspace diff**, including untracked files\\n(`git status --untracked-files=all`). Remove scratch files, probes and\\ndebugging artifacts. Temporary instrumentation must not reach a commit.\\n\\n### Phase 3 — Quality gates\\n\\nUse distinct reviewer roles or separate passes so that planning, implementation,\\nUI review, verification and expert code review do not collapse into one\\nself-review. Give reviewers the diff and observed evidence, not merely the\\nimplementation intent. Run the gates in order. **If any gate causes a code\\nchange, restart from the verification gate and obtain fresh code review\\napproval** — a fix invalidates every result that came before it.\\n\\n#### 6. UI/UX and design review\\n\\n**UI/UX review** *(only if a user-facing surface changed)*. Verify it visually:\\nlaunch the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`), look at it, confirm layout, dark mode,\\nand badges. For behaviour rather than looks, add a `CakebrewUITests` journey —\\nan assertion beats eyeballing a screenshot. Check accessibility and platform\\nidioms, not just that it renders.\\n\\n#### 7. Verification and automated checks\\n\\n**Verification.** Build **Debug and Release**, both warning-free — a new\\nwarning is a failure. Run the full unit suite. Compile the UI test target.\\nRun the UI journeys before opening the PR. Treat every warning or failing check\\nas a finding; after any code edit, rerun the full verification sequence from\\nthe beginning.\\n\\n#### 8. Pre-commit expert code review\\n\\n**Code review** over the full branch diff plus anything uncommitted. Language\\nidioms, memory and concurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases.\\nResolve every actionable finding, then re-verify and repeat the review until it\\nis approved.\\n\\n### Phase 4 — Integration and delivery\\n\\n#### 9. Structured atomic commit\\n\\n**Conventional Commits.** `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`, where type\\nis one of `feat` `fix` `refactor` `docs` `chore` `test` `perf` `build` `ci`\\nand scope names the area (`sidebar`, `reload`, `toolbar`, `brewfile`, `l10n`,\\n…). The body explains *why*, and names what the test covers. History before\\nthis convention was adopted is left alone; do not rewrite it.\\n\\nCommit only the reviewed and verified slice. The test and production code for a\\nbehaviour land together in the same atomic commit.\\n\\n#### 10. Pull request\\n\\n**Open a PR with the `gh` CLI**, never the web UI, and never a draft unless\\nasked. Describe what changed, why, and how it was tested — including what you\\ncould *not* verify and the reason.\\n\\n#### 11. Gated merge and linear history\\n\\n**Green CI is the merge gate.** Both jobs (Build & Test, UI Tests) must pass.\\nNever merge on pending or failing checks. If a UI test fails on CI, read the\\nfailure and the `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the job log before assuming a\\nflake — re-run once only when the evidence says infrastructure. If a human or\\nautomated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass an assigned\\nreview.\\n\\n**Merge and clean up:** `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch` (squash is\\nthe maintainer's explicit choice, and keeps history linear and bisectable),\\nthen `git checkout main`, `git pull --ff-only`.\\n\\n> **Stacked PRs:** retarget a child branch's base to `main` *before* merging its\\n> parent. Deleting a merged branch auto-closes any PR still based on it, and\\n> GitHub will not reopen a PR whose base is gone.\\n\\n### Quick reference\\n\\n| Stage | Pass criteria |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Discovery | Feasibility measured, alternatives weighed, vertical slices ordered, spike discarded |\\n| Setup | Repository inspected, unrelated work preserved, branch created from latest `main` |\\n| Scope | Smallest independently shippable end-to-end outcome selected |\\n| TDD | Focused test observed red, minimal implementation green, refactor stays green |\\n| Diff | Staged, unstaged and untracked files inspected; no scratch or debug artifacts |\\n| UI review | Changed surfaces checked for layout, appearance, accessibility and platform idioms |\\n| Verification | Debug and Release warning-free; unit suite, UI target and UI journeys pass |\\n| Code review | Full branch and workspace diff reviewed; all findings resolved; verification repeated after edits |\\n| Delivery | Atomic Conventional Commit, ready PR, assigned approvals and both CI jobs green |\\n| Merge | Squash merge completed, branch deleted, local `main` fast-forwarded |\\n\\n### Configured skills and subagents\\n\\nFor every feature, bugfix or behaviour-changing refactor, the primary agent\\ninvokes `$cakebrew-workflow` and coordinates the configured roles below. The\\nprimary agent owns requirements, cross-role decisions and delivery; subagents\\nreturn bounded evidence and never silently expand their assignment.\\n\\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Use |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary agent | Route the slice through every applicable phase and repeat invalidated gates |\\n| Planner / architect | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measure unknowns, compare alternatives and define the vertical slice before implementation |\\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Own explicitly assigned files and produce red-green-refactor evidence |\\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Independently review every user-visible change after implementation stabilizes |\\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Run the complete applicable verification sequence without editing source |\\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Independently review the full branch and workspace diff after verification passes |\\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary agent | Perform only the commit, PR or merge actions the user authorized |\\n\\nRun the mutating implementer separately from gate agents. UI review,\\nverification and code review do not fix their own findings: send them back to\\nthe implementer, then rerun every invalidated gate. If independent work must run\\nin parallel, give each agent an isolated worktree and explicit file ownership.\\n\\nThe skill and custom-agent files are routing adapters, not additional sources\\nof project policy. They must defer to this file. Change workflow rules here\\nfirst and keep the adapters narrow.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Principles for agents working here\\n\\n**Prototypes are disposable.** Spike code exists to answer a question. Once\\nanswered, delete it and rebuild under TDD. Do not promote a spike.\\n\\n**Separate the roles.** Planner, implementer, UI reviewer, verifier and code\\nreviewer are different jobs with different biases. One prompt wearing all five\\nhats reviews its own work and finds it good. Use separate agents or separate\\npasses, and give the reviewer the diff rather than the intent.\\n\\n**Validate the instrument before believing its silence.** A probe that prints\\nnothing has not proven anything — it may not have run, may have hit a stale\\nbinary, or may have been filtered out. Confirm the probe executed and observed\\nwhat you think it did. Concretely, in this repo: `xcodebuild analyze` exits 0\\neven with findings; `xcodebuild -scheme Cakebrew` never compiles the UI test\\ntarget; `xcodebuild test` output has to be *grepped for the assertion*, not\\njust for `TEST SUCCEEDED`; and a `log show` predicate that matches nothing\\nlooks exactly like a feature that did not fire.\\n\\n**Isolate shared state when working in parallel.** Agents on one machine\\ncontend for real singletons: the Xcode derived-data directory, the app's\\n`NSUserDefaults` domain, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, the running app itself,\\nand the display. Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or\\nreview pass is running against it; use a git worktree instead.\\n\\n**One source of truth.** This file. Pointer files may reference it; nothing may\\nrestate it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Build & test commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\n# Unit tests (fast, hermetic — run these locally every time)\\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\\\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\n# UI tests (own scheme; needs ad-hoc signing; on CI this is authoritative)\\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nAfter touching `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither command above\\ndoes, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\\n\\n```sh\\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs both jobs on `macos-26` (latest SDK),\\nbuilds Debug and Release, runs the static analyzer, and uploads crash logs from\\n`~/Library/Logs/DiagnosticReports` on failure. A separate weekly\\n`brew-compat.yml` pushes the runner's *real* brew output through the parsers,\\nbecause fixture tests cannot notice upstream drift.\\n\\n## Testing architecture\\n\\n- **Mock brew:** launching the app with `-BPMockBrew` swaps\\n  `BPHomebrewInterface` for `BPMockHomebrewInterface` (resolved in\\n  `+sharedInterface`). The mock serves deterministic fixtures (`mockwget`,\\n  `mockgit` [pinned], `mockchrome`/`mockvscode` [casks], …) and stubs every\\n  operation as a no-op. **Any new interface method must get a mock override**\\n  so UI tests never shell out to real brew — `BPMockFidelityTests` fails if a\\n  mutating selector lacks one. Opt-in launch flags shape it for a specific\\n  journey: `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`, `-BPMockEmptyCleanup`, and\\n  `-BPMockSlowCatalog` (holds the catalog calls so progress and cancel are\\n  observable; the mock is otherwise instant, which makes both unobservable).\\n- **Unit tests** (`CakebrewTests/`) cover parsers, model logic, and manager\\n  state. The `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` parsers are the standard TDD seam:\\n  pure input → `BPFormula` output. Private classes are re-declared in the test\\n  file to reach them.\\n- **UI tests** (`CakebrewUITests/`) are journey tests against the mock. The\\n  shared launch helper waits for the initial load to settle (mockwget rendered)\\n  before navigating — do not remove that; it closes a reselect race in\\n  `homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`. Sidebar item *names* repeat across groups\\n  (two \\\"Installed\\\", two \\\"Outdated\\\"), so journeys address rows by their stable\\n  unlocalized identifier — `[self sidebarRow:@\\\"sidebar.casks.installed\\\"]` —\\n  never by index. Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` (pinned rows carry\\n  a pin glyph after the name).\\n- **CI environment limits:** the headless runner's window is never key, so\\n  typing/keyboard focus is untestable; system file panels (NSSave/NSOpenPanel)\\n  are out-of-process and undrivable. Pattern: unit-test the logic, UI-test\\n  presence/navigation.\\n- **Five ways a green local UI run lies.** Each of these has cost a red CI run\\n  or a misdiagnosed failure:\\n  1. **`isHittable` is meaningless on CI.** Hit testing needs a key window, so\\n     it reports `false` there whatever is on screen. Assert with `exists` and\\n     geometry (`frame.size`) instead.\\n  2. **Anything persisted that changes the launch view makes the suite\\n     order-dependent.** Each journey would inherit whatever the last one left\\n     stored. Pin it in the shared launch helper through the argument domain,\\n     which outranks stored defaults — `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1`,\\n     `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \\\"\\\"` — never by special-casing `-BPMockBrew` in\\n     the app, so the feature itself stays exercised.\\n  3. **Neither verify command compiles the UI test target.** `-scheme Cakebrew`\\n     and `-scheme CakebrewTests` both skip `CakebrewUITests.m`, so a syntax\\n     error there passes locally and fails on CI. After touching it, run\\n     `xcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests …` — about two\\n     seconds, versus ~7 minutes for the suite.\\n  4. **An alert's buttons are mirrored to the Touch Bar.** `self.app.buttons[@\\\"Yes\\\"]`\\n     therefore matches twice, and `firstMatch` can resolve to the mirror, which\\n     is not clickable (\\\"cannot be called with Touch Bar elements\\\"). Scope to the\\n     sheet: `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons[@\\\"Yes\\\"]`. Note that\\n     `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` tolerates a multi-match query, so an\\n     existing assertion passing is not evidence that clicking will work.\\n  5. **A locked screen breaks the suite in ways that look like product bugs.**\\n     XCUITest cannot drive a locked display: clicks resolve to infinite points\\n     and elements never become interactable. Before believing a UI failure that\\n     appeared without a related change, check\\n     `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked`; `screencapture` failing with\\n     \\\"could not create image from window\\\" is the same signal. A ~7 minute run\\n     can also be killed part-way by display sleep.\\n\\n## Architecture crib sheet\\n\\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` — all brew execution funnels through\\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async, streams) or\\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may be nil — always\\n  guard (`invokeOutputBlock:withString:`), passing nil is legitimate.\\n- **List pattern:** a `BPListMode` enum case → a `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall`\\n  subclass (arguments + line parser) → a `BPHomebrewManager` property fetched\\n  in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:` → a sidebar item + badge → a mock\\n  fixture. Follow this groove for any new list.\\n- **Casks** are `BPFormula` instances with `cask == YES` (set by the cask list\\n  parsers; survives copy/coding). Operations dispatch on that flag in\\n  `BPInstallationWindowController` (`--cask` variants). `statusForFormula:` is\\n  namespace-aware — casks read only the cask lists.\\n- **Slow catalogs** (`brew formulae`, `brew casks`) share a 24h disk cache\\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). `brew casks` can take 80+ s cold.\\n- **Sidebar:** `FormulaeSideBarItem` enum values are **outline row indices**\\n  (groups included). Inserting a row renumbers everything after it — update\\n  the enum, the View-menu item tags in `MainMenu.xib`, and check every\\n  `switch`/comparison on the enum (`grep -rn FormulaeSideBarItem`).\\n- **Never `reloadData` the sidebar to refresh a badge.** Reloading an\\n  `NSOutlineView` clears its selection, and `-configureSidebarSettings` restores\\n  the user's last row at setup — so a stray reload silently drops it, and the\\n  next `sourceListSelectionDidChange` persists whatever replaced it. Use\\n  `-refreshBadgeForListMode:`, which redraws one row with `reloadItem:`. The\\n  old all-at-once reload got away with it only because it ran inside\\n  `-homebrewManagerFinishedUpdating:`, which reselects immediately afterwards.\\n- **Row …696 tokens truncated…chanism (routinely rejected, and they hardcode a prefix that\\ndiffers between `/usr/local` and `/opt/homebrew`), so they buy nothing for\\nDeveloper ID distribution.\\n\\n**Therefore:** don't add `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. Hardened runtime,\\nno ATS weakening, a privacy manifest, and validated user input are the\\nhardening this app can actually carry.\\n\\n## Signed local builds (helper testing)\\n\\nDay-to-day development needs nothing special: an Xcode build (Cmd-R) is signed\\nwith your Apple Development certificate, and that **already satisfies the\\nhelper's designated requirement** (it pins `anchor apple generic` plus the team\\nOU, which development certs carry). So the debug build can drive the helper.\\n\\nTo exercise helper registration and Login Items approval for real, the app has\\nto be signed and living in `/Applications`:\\n\\n```sh\\nscripts/install-signed.sh          # Release, or pass Debug\\n```\\n\\nIt builds into a temporary derived-data dir (so it can't leave a stale\\nunsigned helper in your normal one), verifies both signatures, replaces\\n`/Applications/Cakebrew.app`, and prints the next steps.\\n\\nA final build phase, **Verify embedded helper signature**, fails the build if\\nthe embedded helper wouldn't satisfy the app's requirement — otherwise the app\\nsilently refuses to talk to its own helper at runtime. It skips automatically\\nfor unsigned/ad-hoc builds (CI), and declares the helper as a script input\\nbecause Xcode sandboxes script phases.\\n\\n**Distribution certificates:** notarized releases need a *Developer ID\\nApplication* certificate, which is separate from Apple Development. Until one\\nexists in the account, `.github/workflows/release.yml` cannot run.\\n\\n## OS support policy\\n\\nMinimum deployment target = **latest macOS minus one** (currently 15.0);\\nbuild and CI against the **latest SDK** (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned;\\nCI runner tracks the newest image, currently `macos-26`). When a new major\\nmacOS ships: bump the deployment target one, bump the CI runner, never pin\\n`SDKROOT`. `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\\n\\n## Known environment quirks (primary dev machine)\\n\\n- **`git push` hangs** on the Entire pre-push hook (session-log sync). Push\\n  with `git push --no-verify` — it skips only that hook, nothing code-related.\\n- **Local XCUITest works** (since 2026-08-15). It used to die at init with\\n  \\\"Timed out while enabling automation mode\\\"; the cause was macOS developer\\n  mode being off, fixed once with `sudo DevToolsSecurity -enable`. Being in the\\n  `_developer` group is not sufficient on its own, and Accessibility permission\\n  is *not* required. If that init timeout ever returns, check\\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. The full local run takes ~6 minutes (vs ~4s\\n  for the unit suite), so it is a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one. CI remains\\n  the merge gate.\\n- **A locked screen blocks the whole visual toolchain**, not just XCUITest:\\n  `screencapture` fails with \\\"could not create image from window\\\", and the UI\\n  suite fails with `Failed to activate application … (current state: Running\\n  Background)`. Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before\\n  believing either. There is no way around it from a shell — the work is a\\n  pre-PR step that needs the display awake.\\n- **You cannot force one app to light mode from the command line.**\\n  `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` in the argument domain is ignored: AppKit reads\\n  the appearance from the system preference directly rather than through\\n  `NSUserDefaults` precedence, so the app comes up in whatever the system is.\\n  Capturing a light-mode screenshot means flipping System Settings ▸ Appearance\\n  by hand.\\n- Commit signing is temporarily disabled (`git -c commit.gpgsign=false`) while\\n  the 1Password SSH agent is broken. Re-enable when fixed.\\nname = \\\"cakebrew-code-reviewer\\\"\\ndescription = \\\"Independent pre-commit Cakebrew reviewer focused on correctness, regressions, Objective-C/AppKit safety, architecture, and missing tests.\\\"\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"read-only\\\"\\ndeveloper_instructions = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\\nInvoke $cakebrew-code-review. Review the full branch diff plus staged, unstaged, and untracked files after verification passes.\\nDo not edit source files. Lead with actionable findings ordered by severity and include precise file and line evidence. Avoid style-only comments. Explicitly approve the gate when no actionable findings remain; route fixes to the implementer and require fresh verification afterward.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nname = \\\"cakebrew-implementer\\\"\\ndescription = \\\"Cakebrew implementation specialist for one explicitly assigned vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD.\\\"\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\ndeveloper_instructions = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\\nInvoke $cakebrew-implement. You own only the files and behavior explicitly assigned by the primary agent. You are not alone in the codebase: preserve unrelated work and accommodate concurrent changes without reverting them.\\nObserve the focused test fail for the expected reason before production implementation, then make the minimum change, refactor green, and inspect the whole workspace diff.\\nReturn changed files and red-green-refactor evidence. Do not approve your own UI, verification, or code-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs to the primary agent.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nname = \\\"cakebrew-planner\\\"\\ndescription = \\\"Cakebrew discovery and planning specialist for spikes, architectural decisions, and thin vertical slice definition before implementation.\\\"\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\ndeveloper_instructions = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\\nInvoke $cakebrew-plan and stay in the planner role. Inspect and measure assumptions, compare alternatives, and return the smallest testable vertical slice with acceptance criteria and an expected red test.\\nAny spike is disposable. Keep it isolated, remove it before returning, and never promote it into production code.\\nDo not implement the slice, commit, push, open a PR, or modify project instructions. Preserve every unrelated change.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nname = \\\"cakebrew-ui-reviewer\\\"\\ndescription = \\\"Independent Cakebrew AppKit UI and UX reviewer for visual changes, accessibility, platform conventions, and UI journey coverage.\\\"\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\ndeveloper_instructions = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\\nInvoke $cakebrew-ui-review. Review the implemented user-visible surface against the branch and workspace diff using the mock build and the repository's documented UI constraints.\\nDo not edit source files. Return reproducible findings ordered by impact, or explicitly approve the UI gate when no actionable findings remain. Route fixes to the implementer.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nname = \\\"cakebrew-verifier\\\"\\ndescription = \\\"Cakebrew verification specialist for warning-free Debug and Release builds, unit tests, UI target compilation, and UI journeys.\\\"\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\ndeveloper_instructions = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\nRead AGENTS.md completely before acting; it is the sole authority for this repository.\\nInvoke $cakebrew-verify and run the complete applicable verification gate from the beginning. Validate that each instrument exercised the intended target.\\nDo not edit source files. Separate real failures from sandbox, signing, locked-display, and headless-CI artifacts. Return compact command evidence and an explicit pass or fail; route failures to the implementer.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nsandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\napproval_policy = \\\"never\\\"\\n[features]\\nhooks = true\\ndiff --git a/.codex/config.toml b/.codex/config.toml\\nindex 3d76ec0..b28a6e6 100644\\n--- a/.codex/config.toml\\n+++ b/.codex/config.toml\\n@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@\\n \\n+sandbox_mode = \\\"workspace-write\\\"\\n+approval_policy = \\\"never\\\"\\n [features]\\n hooks = true\\n@AGENTS.md\\n# Contributing\\n\\n**[AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) is the single source of truth** for how work happens\\nhere — the workflow, the conventions, and the decisions already made. It is\\nwritten for human contributors and coding agents alike.\\n\\nThis file deliberately does not restate any of it. Two documents describing one\\nworkflow drift apart, and then neither can be trusted.\\n\\n## Start here\\n\\n1. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) once, end to end. It is not long, and most of it\\n   is things that cost someone a wasted afternoon to learn.\\n2. Branch off `main`, take one thin slice, write the failing test first.\\n3. Open a PR with `gh`. Green CI is the merge gate.\\n\\n## The commands you will need every time\\n\\n```sh\\n# App build, both configurations — both must be warning-free\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\n# Unit tests — seconds, run them every time\\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\\\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\n# UI tests — a pre-PR step, not an inner-loop one (~8 minutes)\\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf you touch `CakebrewUITests.m`, compile that target — neither test command\\nabove does, so a syntax error there passes locally and fails on CI:\\n\\n```sh\\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Reporting bugs\\n\\nPlease use the issue templates. The environment questions are not boilerplate:\\nthe macOS version, the Homebrew version and prefix, and whether the problem\\nreproduces under `-BPMockBrew` are what separate a parser bug from an\\nenvironment one.\\n\\nSecurity issues go through [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a public issue.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-code-review\\ndescription: Perform the independent pre-commit expert review of a Cakebrew branch and all uncommitted files after verification passes.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Code Review\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the full branch diff against its base plus\\nall staged, unstaged and untracked files. Do not edit source files.\\n\\nPrioritize correctness, regressions, Objective-C and AppKit idioms, memory and\\nconcurrency safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, mock fidelity and\\nmissing tests. Trace the actual execution path before reporting a problem.\\nAvoid style-only comments unless they reveal a concrete maintenance or defect\\nrisk.\\n\\nReturn findings ordered by severity with precise file and line references,\\nimpact and a reproducible rationale. If there are no actionable findings, say\\nthe gate is approved. Any fix belongs to the implementer and invalidates the\\nprior verification result.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-deliver\\ndescription: Commit, publish, review, merge, and clean up a fully verified Cakebrew slice when the user explicitly requests the applicable delivery actions.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Deliver\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Delivery does not grant authority: perform only the\\ncommit, push, PR or merge actions the user requested.\\n\\n- Confirm UI review when applicable, complete verification, and expert approval\\n  all correspond to the current source state.\\n- Reinspect the entire workspace and stage only the intended slice. Preserve\\n  unrelated modifications and untracked files.\\n- Create the atomic Conventional Commit required by `AGENTS.md`, including why\\n  the change exists and what the test covers.\\n- Open a ready PR with `gh` only when requested, reporting all verification and\\n  any genuine gaps.\\n- Wait for both CI jobs and every assigned human or automated review. Never\\n  bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\\n- Squash merge and clean up only with explicit merge authorization, including\\n  the stacked-PR precaution in `AGENTS.md`.\\n\\nStop and report any stale gate evidence, changed head, failed CI, unresolved\\nreview, authentication issue or authorization boundary.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-implement\\ndescription: Implement an assigned Cakebrew vertical slice under strict red-green-refactor TDD after its scope and acceptance criteria are established.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Implement\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and accept explicit ownership of the assigned files\\nor behavior. Other work may exist in the checkout; never revert or absorb it.\\n\\n1. Confirm the slice, acceptance criteria and intended test seam.\\n2. Add the smallest focused test first and run it to observe the expected red.\\n3. Implement only enough production code to make it green.\\n4. Refactor with the focused test and relevant fast suite green.\\n5. Inspect all staged, unstaged and untracked files and remove scratch or debug\\n   artifacts created by this implementation.\\n\\nIf red was not observed, perform and report the mutation proof required by\\n`AGENTS.md`. Stop after implementation evidence; do not self-approve the UI,\\nverification or expert-review gates. Never commit or publish; delivery belongs\\nto the primary agent under `$cakebrew-deliver`.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-plan\\ndescription: Plan or spike a Cakebrew change before implementation when requirements, architecture, framework behavior, integration boundaries, or vertical-slice scope need to be resolved.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Plan\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Stay in the planner role: do not implement\\nproduction code.\\n\\n- Inspect the repository and trace the relevant execution paths.\\n- Identify unknowns that require measurement. Use the smallest disposable\\n  spike needed, keep it out of production paths, and remove it before returning.\\n- Compare viable alternatives by architectural fit, complexity, performance\\n  and maintenance burden.\\n- Define the smallest cohesive vertical slice, its acceptance criteria, test\\n  seams and expected red test.\\n- Report assumptions, evidence, risks, rejected alternatives and an ordered\\n  slice plan. Clearly distinguish measured behavior from inference.\\n\\nPreserve all unrelated changes. Do not commit, push, open a PR or change\\nproject instructions.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-ui-review\\ndescription: Review a user-visible Cakebrew change for AppKit conventions, layout, appearance, accessibility, and UI journey coverage after implementation stabilizes.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Ui Review\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely. Review the changed surface independently; do not\\nedit source files.\\n\\n- Inspect the branch and workspace diff to identify affected journeys.\\n- Launch against `-BPMockBrew` and verify the relevant state visually.\\n- Check layout, dark mode, badges, accessibility identity and macOS idioms.\\n- Prefer an existing or proposed `CakebrewUITests` assertion for behavior that\\n  should not depend on visual judgment.\\n- Account for the documented locked-screen and headless-CI limitations before\\n  classifying a failure.\\n\\nReturn prioritized, reproducible findings with file or UI location and expected\\nbehavior. Say explicitly when the gate passes. Send fixes back to the\\nimplementer rather than making them in the review pass.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-verify\\ndescription: Run Cakebrew's complete local verification gate after implementation or fixes, including required builds, tests, warnings checks, and UI-target coverage.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Verify\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely and use its current commands as the authority. Do\\nnot edit source files.\\n\\nRun the gate from the beginning in its documented order:\\n\\n1. Debug build, warning-free.\\n2. Release build, warning-free.\\n3. Full unit suite.\\n4. UI test target compilation.\\n5. UI journeys before PR creation, subject to the documented display checks.\\n\\nValidate that each instrument actually exercised the intended target; do not\\ntreat an exit code or silent output alone as proof. Separate genuine product or\\nsetup failures from sandbox, signing, locked-screen or headless-environment\\nartifacts. Return a compact command-by-command result with decisive diagnostics\\nand an explicit pass/fail. Route failures to the implementer.\\n---\\nname: cakebrew-workflow\\ndescription: Orchestrate a complete Cakebrew feature, bugfix, or behavior-changing refactor through planning, TDD implementation, UI review when applicable, verification, expert review, and delivery gates.\\n---\\n\\n# Cakebrew Workflow\\n\\nRead `AGENTS.md` completely before acting. It is the sole authority for the\\nworkflow, commands, architecture and delivery rules; this skill only routes the\\nwork through the configured roles.\\n\\n1. Use `cakebrew-planner` with `$cakebrew-plan` to validate assumptions and\\n   define the smallest vertical slice. Any spike is disposable.\\n2. Inspect repository state, synchronize the latest `main`, preserve unrelated\\n   changes, and create the required branch before production edits.\\n3. Give `cakebrew-implementer` explicit ownership of the slice and invoke\\n   `$cakebrew-implement`. Require observed red-green-refactor evidence.\\n4. If a user-visible surface changed, run `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` with\\n   `$cakebrew-ui-review` after implementation is stable.\\n5. Run `cakebrew-verifier` with `$cakebrew-verify`, then\\n   `cakebrew-code-reviewer` with `$cakebrew-code-review`.\\n6. Route every actionable finding back to the implementer. After any source\\n   edit, repeat verification and expert review; repeat UI review too when the\\n   fix changes the visible surface.\\n7. Keep delivery with the primary agent. Invoke `$cakebrew-deliver` only when\\n   the user has authorized the relevant commit, push, PR or merge action.\\n\\nDo not run a mutating agent concurrently with a verifier or reviewer against\\nthe same worktree. Use isolated worktrees when independent passes truly need to\\nrun in parallel. Return concise role summaries and evidence to the primary\\nagent instead of raw logs.\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Code Review\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Independently review the full Cakebrew diff\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-code-review to perform the pre-commit expert review of this Cakebrew branch.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Deliver\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Deliver a verified Cakebrew slice safely\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-deliver to perform the authorized delivery steps for this verified Cakebrew branch.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Implement\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Implement one Cakebrew slice with strict TDD\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-implement to implement this scoped Cakebrew slice with red-green-refactor evidence.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Plan\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Plan and de-risk a Cakebrew vertical slice\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-plan to investigate and scope this Cakebrew change before implementation.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew UI Review\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Review Cakebrew UI, UX, and accessibility\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-ui-review to review the user-visible changes in this Cakebrew branch.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Verify\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Run Cakebrew's complete verification gate\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-verify to verify the current Cakebrew branch from the start.\\\"\\ninterface:\\n  display_name: \\\"Cakebrew Workflow\\\"\\n  short_description: \\\"Run Cakebrew's gated engineering lifecycle\\\"\\n  default_prompt: \\\"Use $cakebrew-workflow to deliver this Cakebrew change through every required gate.\\\"\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","create_time":1788831442.58088}}}
