{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:39:46.384Z","ordinal":22114,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-016ebda6-b64a-4a4f-a5e8-6858ded9cdb4","process_id":"99754","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","git fetch origin main && git switch -c docs/agent-workflow origin/main"],"cwd":"file:///Users/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/cakebrew","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git fetch origin main && git switch -c docs/agent-workflow origin/main"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"From https://github.com/scottdensmore/Cakebrew\n * branch            main       -> FETCH_HEAD\nM\t.codex/config.toml\nbranch 'docs/agent-workflow' set up to track 'origin/main'.\nSwitched to a new branch 'docs/agent-workflow'\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"From https://github.com/scottdensmore/Cakebrew\n * branch            main       -> FETCH_HEAD\nM\t.codex/config.toml\nbranch 'docs/agent-workflow' set up to track 'origin/main'.\nSwitched to a new branch 'docs/agent-workflow'\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":249007365},"formatted_output":"From https://github.com/scottdensmore/Cakebrew\n * branch            main       -> FETCH_HEAD\nM\t.codex/config.toml\nbranch 'docs/agent-workflow' set up to track 'origin/main'.\nSwitched to a new branch 'docs/agent-workflow'\n"},"started_at_ms":1788831586134,"completed_at_ms":1788831586384}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:39:46.606Z","ordinal":22115,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d4b4e8f1-257f-4eb1-95dc-6a1411ccf082","process_id":"13489","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat /Users/scottdensmore/Downloads/workflow.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/cakebrew","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"cat /Users/scottdensmore/Downloads/workflow.md","name":"workflow.md","path":"/Users/scottdensmore/Downloads/workflow.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# The Autonomous Engineering Workflow\n*A structured, multi-agent TDD framework for high-assurance software delivery.*\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, Prototype & Spike] --> B[2. Inspect & Branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope Thin Vertical Slice]\n    C --> D[4. Test-Driven Implementation - TDD]\n    D --> E[5. Workspace Diff Inspection]\n    E --> F{User-Visible Changes?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX & Design Review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification & Automated Checks]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Pre-Commit Expert Code Review]\n    I -- Findings / Changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Structured Atomic Commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull Request Creation]\n    K --> L[11. Gated Merge & Linear History]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Workflow Lifecycle\n\n### Phase 0: Discovery & Planning\n1. **Explore, Prototype & Plan (The Spike Phase)**\n   * **Feasibility & Spikes:** Before writing production code or locking into a design, build lightweight, throwaway prototypes (spikes) to explore technical approaches, test framework/API assumptions, and uncover hidden edge cases or performance bottlenecks.\n   * **Evaluate Alternatives:** Weigh tradeoffs across architectural fit, complexity, performance, and maintenance burden.\n   * **Break Down Slices:** Convert the validated design into an ordered implementation plan made up of small, self-contained vertical slices.\n   * **Reset/Clean Workspace:** Discard or archive prototype scratch code so development starts on a clean slate.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Context & Scoping\n2. **Inspect Before Mutating**\n   * Inspect current repository state, Git branches, working trees, and configuration files before modifying anything.\n   * Preserve all unrelated staged, unstaged, or untracked changes.\n3. **Dedicated Branching**\n   * Always branch from the latest base (`main` / `trunk`).\n   * Never commit directly to the primary branch.\n4. **Thin Vertical Slices**\n   * Take the first slice from the planning phase—the smallest cohesive, end-to-end outcome (user-visible feature, operational improvement, or bugfix) that can be tested, reviewed, and shipped independently.\n   * Avoid large horizontal layers in a single PR to maintain quick rollback and review velocity.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Test-Driven Implementation (TDD)\n5. **Red–Green–Refactor Discipline**\n   * **Red:** Write or update a focused automated test before touching production code; confirm it fails for the exact expected reason.\n   * **Green:** Implement the minimal necessary change to pass the test.\n   * **Refactor:** Clean and optimize code while keeping the test suite green.\n6. **Diff & Workspace Inspection**\n   * Inspect the full diff including staged, unstaged, and untracked files (`git status --untracked-files=all`).\n   * Clean up accidental edits, intermediate scratch files, and debugging artifacts.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: Multi-Stage Quality Gates (Specialized Sub-Agents / Roles)\n7. **UI / UX Review Gate** *(if user-facing surface touched)*\n   * A dedicated reviewer checks accessibility, design consistency, platform design idioms, and layout behavior before technical verification.\n8. **Verification Gate (Automated Validation)**\n   * **Multi-Configuration Builds & Warnings:** Compile in all target modes (e.g., Debug/Release) and treat compiler warnings as actionable findings.\n   * **Unit & Integration Suites:** Run the entire test suite.\n   * **End-to-End Journey Coverage:** Validate full user workflows through automated drivers or UI Automation.\n   * **Rule:** If verification exposes an issue requiring code edits, resolve the issue and **rerun the full verification suite from the start**.\n9. **Expert Code Review Gate**\n   * An expert review is conducted across the branch diff and all uncommitted files.\n   * Checks language idioms, memory/async safety, performance, architecture, and edge cases.\n   * Any change resulting from review feedback must loop back through verification and receive fresh code review approval.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Integration & Delivery\n10. **Conventional, Structured Commits**\n    * Commit only after verification and code review approval.\n    * Use Conventional Commits (`<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`) with clear descriptions of *why* changes were made.\n11. **Pull Request Discipline**\n    * PRs are created directly from the fully reviewed and verified state.\n    * Open normal, ready-to-review PRs (avoid draft PRs unless explicitly requested).\n12. **Gated Merge & Linear History**\n    * **Reviewer Gating:** If a human or automated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass assigned reviews.\n    * **Clean CI:** Require 100% green checks on remote CI pipelines.\n    * **Linear History:** Use squash merges for short-lived feature branches to preserve a clean, bisectable history.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Engineering Principles for AI / Autonomous Agents\n\n### A. Intentional Separation Between Prototyping and Production\n* **Throwaway vs. Production Code:** Treat exploratory spikes as disposable instruments used solely to learn and de-risk. Once the architecture is proven, rebuild the feature using strict TDD rather than committing prototype spaghetti.\n* **De-Risk Unknowns Early:** Never assume third-party library behavior or complex API integration will work as documented; prototype the integration boundary first.\n\n### B. Role Specialization (Separation of Concerns)\nAvoid having a single agent prompt serve as architect, developer, QA, and reviewer all at once. Separate duties into distinct roles or sub-agents:\n* **Planner / Architect:** Explores options, runs spikes, and outlines the slice-by-slice plan.\n* **Implementer:** Focuses on writing tests and minimal functional code per slice.\n* **UI Reviewer:** Focuses on design patterns, accessibility, and visual polish.\n* **Verifier:** Operates the build pipeline, runs tests, checks compiler diagnostics, and drives E2E journeys.\n* **Code Reviewer:** Performs static code audits and enforces best practices.\n\n### C. \"Validate the Instrument Before Believing Its Silence\"\n* When an automated probe or test doesn't output an error, ensure the probe actually ran and didn't fail silently or test against stale binaries.\n* Beware of caching, build-output mismatches, or test runners masking execution failures.\n* When doing mutation testing (introducing a deliberate bug to see if tests catch it), confirm the test fails for the *expected reason*, not because the runner crashed.\n\n### D. Safe Concurrency & Shared State Isolation\nWhen running parallel agents or worktrees on the same machine:\n* **Isolate Shared Resources:** Isolate system-level singletons (ports, app registrations, test databases, shared fixtures).\n* **Worktree Mutex:** Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or code review agent is running against it.\n\n### E. Single Source of Truth (SSOT)\n* Maintain a single instruction file (e.g., `AGENTS.md`) as the authoritative source for project conventions, architecture decisions, and workflows. Pointer files (like `CLAUDE.md`) should merely reference this master file.\n* Avoid redundant status or roadmap documents that drift out of sync; let issues/backlog trackers serve as the live status.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Reference Checklist\n\n| Stage | Action | Criteria to Pass |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **0. Discovery** | Plan & Prototype | Feasibility proven via spikes, tradeoffs evaluated, vertical slices mapped out. |\n| **1. Setup** | Inspect & Branch | Clean workspace, dedicated feature branch created from latest base. |\n| **2. Scoping** | Thin Slice | Smallest coherent, end-to-end outcome selected. |\n| **3. Build** | TDD Loop | Focused test fails first, minimal code makes it pass, refactored cleanly. |\n| **4. UI Review** | Design Audit | Design standards, accessibility, and UI patterns validated. |\n| **5. Verifier** | Verification Suite | Builds clean in all modes, static checks pass, tests green, E2E journeys verified. |\n| **6. Code Review** | Peer/Expert Review | All uncommitted files reviewed; actionable feedback resolved. |\n| **7. Ship** | Commit & PR | Conventional commit message, PR created, assigned reviews completed, squashed to main. |\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# The Autonomous Engineering Workflow\n*A structured, multi-agent TDD framework for high-assurance software delivery.*\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, Prototype & Spike] --> B[2. Inspect & Branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope Thin Vertical Slice]\n    C --> D[4. Test-Driven Implementation - TDD]\n    D --> E[5. Workspace Diff Inspection]\n    E --> F{User-Visible Changes?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX & Design Review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification & Automated Checks]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Pre-Commit Expert Code Review]\n    I -- Findings / Changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Structured Atomic Commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull Request Creation]\n    K --> L[11. Gated Merge & Linear History]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Workflow Lifecycle\n\n### Phase 0: Discovery & Planning\n1. **Explore, Prototype & Plan (The Spike Phase)**\n   * **Feasibility & Spikes:** Before writing production code or locking into a design, build lightweight, throwaway prototypes (spikes) to explore technical approaches, test framework/API assumptions, and uncover hidden edge cases or performance bottlenecks.\n   * **Evaluate Alternatives:** Weigh tradeoffs across architectural fit, complexity, performance, and maintenance burden.\n   * **Break Down Slices:** Convert the validated design into an ordered implementation plan made up of small, self-contained vertical slices.\n   * **Reset/Clean Workspace:** Discard or archive prototype scratch code so development starts on a clean slate.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Context & Scoping\n2. **Inspect Before Mutating**\n   * Inspect current repository state, Git branches, working trees, and configuration files before modifying anything.\n   * Preserve all unrelated staged, unstaged, or untracked changes.\n3. **Dedicated Branching**\n   * Always branch from the latest base (`main` / `trunk`).\n   * Never commit directly to the primary branch.\n4. **Thin Vertical Slices**\n   * Take the first slice from the planning phase—the smallest cohesive, end-to-end outcome (user-visible feature, operational improvement, or bugfix) that can be tested, reviewed, and shipped independently.\n   * Avoid large horizontal layers in a single PR to maintain quick rollback and review velocity.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Test-Driven Implementation (TDD)\n5. **Red–Green–Refactor Discipline**\n   * **Red:** Write or update a focused automated test before touching production code; confirm it fails for the exact expected reason.\n   * **Green:** Implement the minimal necessary change to pass the test.\n   * **Refactor:** Clean and optimize code while keeping the test suite green.\n6. **Diff & Workspace Inspection**\n   * Inspect the full diff including staged, unstaged, and untracked files (`git status --untracked-files=all`).\n   * Clean up accidental edits, intermediate scratch files, and debugging artifacts.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: Multi-Stage Quality Gates (Specialized Sub-Agents / Roles)\n7. **UI / UX Review Gate** *(if user-facing surface touched)*\n   * A dedicated reviewer checks accessibility, design consistency, platform design idioms, and layout behavior before technical verification.\n8. **Verification Gate (Automated Validation)**\n   * **Multi-Configuration Builds & Warnings:** Compile in all target modes (e.g., Debug/Release) and treat compiler warnings as actionable findings.\n   * **Unit & Integration Suites:** Run the entire test suite.\n   * **End-to-End Journey Coverage:** Validate full user workflows through automated drivers or UI Automation.\n   * **Rule:** If verification exposes an issue requiring code edits, resolve the issue and **rerun the full verification suite from the start**.\n9. **Expert Code Review Gate**\n   * An expert review is conducted across the branch diff and all uncommitted files.\n   * Checks language idioms, memory/async safety, performance, architecture, and edge cases.\n   * Any change resulting from review feedback must loop back through verification and receive fresh code review approval.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Integration & Delivery\n10. **Conventional, Structured Commits**\n    * Commit only after verification and code review approval.\n    * Use Conventional Commits (`<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`) with clear descriptions of *why* changes were made.\n11. **Pull Request Discipline**\n    * PRs are created directly from the fully reviewed and verified state.\n    * Open normal, ready-to-review PRs (avoid draft PRs unless explicitly requested).\n12. **Gated Merge & Linear History**\n    * **Reviewer Gating:** If a human or automated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass assigned reviews.\n    * **Clean CI:** Require 100% green checks on remote CI pipelines.\n    * **Linear History:** Use squash merges for short-lived feature branches to preserve a clean, bisectable history.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Engineering Principles for AI / Autonomous Agents\n\n### A. Intentional Separation Between Prototyping and Production\n* **Throwaway vs. Production Code:** Treat exploratory spikes as disposable instruments used solely to learn and de-risk. Once the architecture is proven, rebuild the feature using strict TDD rather than committing prototype spaghetti.\n* **De-Risk Unknowns Early:** Never assume third-party library behavior or complex API integration will work as documented; prototype the integration boundary first.\n\n### B. Role Specialization (Separation of Concerns)\nAvoid having a single agent prompt serve as architect, developer, QA, and reviewer all at once. Separate duties into distinct roles or sub-agents:\n* **Planner / Architect:** Explores options, runs spikes, and outlines the slice-by-slice plan.\n* **Implementer:** Focuses on writing tests and minimal functional code per slice.\n* **UI Reviewer:** Focuses on design patterns, accessibility, and visual polish.\n* **Verifier:** Operates the build pipeline, runs tests, checks compiler diagnostics, and drives E2E journeys.\n* **Code Reviewer:** Performs static code audits and enforces best practices.\n\n### C. \"Validate the Instrument Before Believing Its Silence\"\n* When an automated probe or test doesn't output an error, ensure the probe actually ran and didn't fail silently or test against stale binaries.\n* Beware of caching, build-output mismatches, or test runners masking execution failures.\n* When doing mutation testing (introducing a deliberate bug to see if tests catch it), confirm the test fails for the *expected reason*, not because the runner crashed.\n\n### D. Safe Concurrency & Shared State Isolation\nWhen running parallel agents or worktrees on the same machine:\n* **Isolate Shared Resources:** Isolate system-level singletons (ports, app registrations, test databases, shared fixtures).\n* **Worktree Mutex:** Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or code review agent is running against it.\n\n### E. Single Source of Truth (SSOT)\n* Maintain a single instruction file (e.g., `AGENTS.md`) as the authoritative source for project conventions, architecture decisions, and workflows. Pointer files (like `CLAUDE.md`) should merely reference this master file.\n* Avoid redundant status or roadmap documents that drift out of sync; let issues/backlog trackers serve as the live status.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Reference Checklist\n\n| Stage | Action | Criteria to Pass |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **0. Discovery** | Plan & Prototype | Feasibility proven via spikes, tradeoffs evaluated, vertical slices mapped out. |\n| **1. Setup** | Inspect & Branch | Clean workspace, dedicated feature branch created from latest base. |\n| **2. Scoping** | Thin Slice | Smallest coherent, end-to-end outcome selected. |\n| **3. Build** | TDD Loop | Focused test fails first, minimal code makes it pass, refactored cleanly. |\n| **4. UI Review** | Design Audit | Design standards, accessibility, and UI patterns validated. |\n| **5. Verifier** | Verification Suite | Builds clean in all modes, static checks pass, tests green, E2E journeys verified. |\n| **6. Code Review** | Peer/Expert Review | All uncommitted files reviewed; actionable feedback resolved. |\n| **7. Ship** | Commit & PR | Conventional commit message, PR created, assigned reviews completed, squashed to main. |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5451},"formatted_output":"# The Autonomous Engineering Workflow\n*A structured, multi-agent TDD framework for high-assurance software delivery.*\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, Prototype & Spike] --> B[2. Inspect & Branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope Thin Vertical Slice]\n    C --> D[4. Test-Driven Implementation - TDD]\n    D --> E[5. Workspace Diff Inspection]\n    E --> F{User-Visible Changes?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX & Design Review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification & Automated Checks]\n    G --> H\n    H --> I[8. Pre-Commit Expert Code Review]\n    I -- Findings / Changes --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Structured Atomic Commit]\n    J --> K[10. Pull Request Creation]\n    K --> L[11. Gated Merge & Linear History]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Workflow Lifecycle\n\n### Phase 0: Discovery & Planning\n1. **Explore, Prototype & Plan (The Spike Phase)**\n   * **Feasibility & Spikes:** Before writing production code or locking into a design, build lightweight, throwaway prototypes (spikes) to explore technical approaches, test framework/API assumptions, and uncover hidden edge cases or performance bottlenecks.\n   * **Evaluate Alternatives:** Weigh tradeoffs across architectural fit, complexity, performance, and maintenance burden.\n   * **Break Down Slices:** Convert the validated design into an ordered implementation plan made up of small, self-contained vertical slices.\n   * **Reset/Clean Workspace:** Discard or archive prototype scratch code so development starts on a clean slate.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Context & Scoping\n2. **Inspect Before Mutating**\n   * Inspect current repository state, Git branches, working trees, and configuration files before modifying anything.\n   * Preserve all unrelated staged, unstaged, or untracked changes.\n3. **Dedicated Branching**\n   * Always branch from the latest base (`main` / `trunk`).\n   * Never commit directly to the primary branch.\n4. **Thin Vertical Slices**\n   * Take the first slice from the planning phase—the smallest cohesive, end-to-end outcome (user-visible feature, operational improvement, or bugfix) that can be tested, reviewed, and shipped independently.\n   * Avoid large horizontal layers in a single PR to maintain quick rollback and review velocity.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Test-Driven Implementation (TDD)\n5. **Red–Green–Refactor Discipline**\n   * **Red:** Write or update a focused automated test before touching production code; confirm it fails for the exact expected reason.\n   * **Green:** Implement the minimal necessary change to pass the test.\n   * **Refactor:** Clean and optimize code while keeping the test suite green.\n6. **Diff & Workspace Inspection**\n   * Inspect the full diff including staged, unstaged, and untracked files (`git status --untracked-files=all`).\n   * Clean up accidental edits, intermediate scratch files, and debugging artifacts.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: Multi-Stage Quality Gates (Specialized Sub-Agents / Roles)\n7. **UI / UX Review Gate** *(if user-facing surface touched)*\n   * A dedicated reviewer checks accessibility, design consistency, platform design idioms, and layout behavior before technical verification.\n8. **Verification Gate (Automated Validation)**\n   * **Multi-Configuration Builds & Warnings:** Compile in all target modes (e.g., Debug/Release) and treat compiler warnings as actionable findings.\n   * **Unit & Integration Suites:** Run the entire test suite.\n   * **End-to-End Journey Coverage:** Validate full user workflows through automated drivers or UI Automation.\n   * **Rule:** If verification exposes an issue requiring code edits, resolve the issue and **rerun the full verification suite from the start**.\n9. **Expert Code Review Gate**\n   * An expert review is conducted across the branch diff and all uncommitted files.\n   * Checks language idioms, memory/async safety, performance, architecture, and edge cases.\n   * Any change resulting from review feedback must loop back through verification and receive fresh code review approval.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Integration & Delivery\n10. **Conventional, Structured Commits**\n    * Commit only after verification and code review approval.\n    * Use Conventional Commits (`<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`) with clear descriptions of *why* changes were made.\n11. **Pull Request Discipline**\n    * PRs are created directly from the fully reviewed and verified state.\n    * Open normal, ready-to-review PRs (avoid draft PRs unless explicitly requested).\n12. **Gated Merge & Linear History**\n    * **Reviewer Gating:** If a human or automated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass assigned reviews.\n    * **Clean CI:** Require 100% green checks on remote CI pipelines.\n    * **Linear History:** Use squash merges for short-lived feature branches to preserve a clean, bisectable history.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Engineering Principles for AI / Autonomous Agents\n\n### A. Intentional Separation Between Prototyping and Production\n* **Throwaway vs. Production Code:** Treat exploratory spikes as disposable instruments used solely to learn and de-risk. Once the architecture is proven, rebuild the feature using strict TDD rather than committing prototype spaghetti.\n* **De-Risk Unknowns Early:** Never assume third-party library behavior or complex API integration will work as documented; prototype the integration boundary first.\n\n### B. Role Specialization (Separation of Concerns)\nAvoid having a single agent prompt serve as architect, developer, QA, and reviewer all at once. Separate duties into distinct roles or sub-agents:\n* **Planner / Architect:** Explores options, runs spikes, and outlines the slice-by-slice plan.\n* **Implementer:** Focuses on writing tests and minimal functional code per slice.\n* **UI Reviewer:** Focuses on design patterns, accessibility, and visual polish.\n* **Verifier:** Operates the build pipeline, runs tests, checks compiler diagnostics, and drives E2E journeys.\n* **Code Reviewer:** Performs static code audits and enforces best practices.\n\n### C. \"Validate the Instrument Before Believing Its Silence\"\n* When an automated probe or test doesn't output an error, ensure the probe actually ran and didn't fail silently or test against stale binaries.\n* Beware of caching, build-output mismatches, or test runners masking execution failures.\n* When doing mutation testing (introducing a deliberate bug to see if tests catch it), confirm the test fails for the *expected reason*, not because the runner crashed.\n\n### D. Safe Concurrency & Shared State Isolation\nWhen running parallel agents or worktrees on the same machine:\n* **Isolate Shared Resources:** Isolate system-level singletons (ports, app registrations, test databases, shared fixtures).\n* **Worktree Mutex:** Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or code review agent is running against it.\n\n### E. Single Source of Truth (SSOT)\n* Maintain a single instruction file (e.g., `AGENTS.md`) as the authoritative source for project conventions, architecture decisions, and workflows. Pointer files (like `CLAUDE.md`) should merely reference this master file.\n* Avoid redundant status or roadmap documents that drift out of sync; let issues/backlog trackers serve as the live status.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Reference Checklist\n\n| Stage | Action | Criteria to Pass |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **0. Discovery** | Plan & Prototype | Feasibility proven via spikes, tradeoffs evaluated, vertical slices mapped out. |\n| **1. Setup** | Inspect & Branch | Clean workspace, dedicated feature branch created from latest base. |\n| **2. Scoping** | Thin Slice | Smallest coherent, end-to-end outcome selected. |\n| **3. Build** | TDD Loop | Focused test fails first, minimal code makes it pass, refactored cleanly. |\n| **4. UI Review** | Design Audit | Design standards, accessibility, and UI patterns validated. |\n| **5. Verifier** | Verification Suite | Builds clean in all modes, static checks pass, tests green, E2E journeys verified. |\n| **6. Code Review** | Peer/Expert Review | All uncommitted files reviewed; actionable feedback resolved. |\n| **7. Ship** | Commit & PR | Conventional commit message, PR created, assigned reviews completed, squashed to main. |\n"},"started_at_ms":1788831586606,"completed_at_ms":1788831586606}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T01:39:46.715Z","ordinal":22116,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a07eab-d99b-7853-b480-7920f4f65827","call_id":"call_aduAJh6F0aA4YzP2fW0YWNbq","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 6.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f02d69\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.249008721,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":56,\"output\":\"From https://github.com/scottdensmore/Cakebrew\\n * branch            main       -> FETCH_HEAD\\nM\\t.codex/config.toml\\nbranch 'docs/agent-workflow' set up to track 'origin/main'.\\nSwitched to a new branch 'docs/agent-workflow'\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"c8168f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005451,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2030,\"output\":\"# The Autonomous Engineering Workflow\\n*A structured, multi-agent TDD framework for high-assurance software delivery.*\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    A[1. Plan, Prototype & Spike] --> B[2. Inspect & Branch]\\n    B --> C[3. Scope Thin Vertical Slice]\\n    C --> D[4. Test-Driven Implementation - TDD]\\n    D --> E[5. Workspace Diff Inspection]\\n    E --> F{User-Visible Changes?}\\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI/UX & Design Review]\\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification & Automated Checks]\\n    G --> H\\n    H --> I[8. Pre-Commit Expert Code Review]\\n    I -- Findings / Changes --> D\\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Structured Atomic Commit]\\n    J --> K[10. Pull Request Creation]\\n    K --> L[11. Gated Merge & Linear History]\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Core Workflow Lifecycle\\n\\n### Phase 0: Discovery & Planning\\n1. **Explore, Prototype & Plan (The Spike Phase)**\\n   * **Feasibility & Spikes:** Before writing production code or locking into a design, build lightweight, throwaway prototypes (spikes) to explore technical approaches, test framework/API assumptions, and uncover hidden edge cases or performance bottlenecks.\\n   * **Evaluate Alternatives:** Weigh tradeoffs across architectural fit, complexity, performance, and maintenance burden.\\n   * **Break Down Slices:** Convert the validated design into an ordered implementation plan made up of small, self-contained vertical slices.\\n   * **Reset/Clean Workspace:** Discard or archive prototype scratch code so development starts on a clean slate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 1: Context & Scoping\\n2. **Inspect Before Mutating**\\n   * Inspect current repository state, Git branches, working trees, and configuration files before modifying anything.\\n   * Preserve all unrelated staged, unstaged, or untracked changes.\\n3. **Dedicated Branching**\\n   * Always branch from the latest base (`main` / `trunk`).\\n   * Never commit directly to the primary branch.\\n4. **Thin Vertical Slices**\\n   * Take the first slice from the planning phase—the smallest cohesive, end-to-end outcome (user-visible feature, operational improvement, or bugfix) that can be tested, reviewed, and shipped independently.\\n   * Avoid large horizontal layers in a single PR to maintain quick rollback and review velocity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 2: Test-Driven Implementation (TDD)\\n5. **Red–Green–Refactor Discipline**\\n   * **Red:** Write or update a focused automated test before touching production code; confirm it fails for the exact expected reason.\\n   * **Green:** Implement the minimal necessary change to pass the test.\\n   * **Refactor:** Clean and optimize code while keeping the test suite green.\\n6. **Diff & Workspace Inspection**\\n   * Inspect the full diff including staged, unstaged, and untracked files (`git status --untracked-files=all`).\\n   * Clean up accidental edits, intermediate scratch files, and debugging artifacts.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 3: Multi-Stage Quality Gates (Specialized Sub-Agents / Roles)\\n7. **UI / UX Review Gate** *(if user-facing surface touched)*\\n   * A dedicated reviewer checks accessibility, design consistency, platform design idioms, and layout behavior before technical verification.\\n8. **Verification Gate (Automated Validation)**\\n   * **Multi-Configuration Builds & Warnings:** Compile in all target modes (e.g., Debug/Release) and treat compiler warnings as actionable findings.\\n   * **Unit & Integration Suites:** Run the entire test suite.\\n   * **End-to-End Journey Coverage:** Validate full user workflows through automated drivers or UI Automation.\\n   * **Rule:** If verification exposes an issue requiring code edits, resolve the issue and **rerun the full verification suite from the start**.\\n9. **Expert Code Review Gate**\\n   * An expert review is conducted across the branch diff and all uncommitted files.\\n   * Checks language idioms, memory/async safety, performance, architecture, and edge cases.\\n   * Any change resulting from review feedback must loop back through verification and receive fresh code review approval.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Phase 4: Integration & Delivery\\n10. **Conventional, Structured Commits**\\n    * Commit only after verification and code review approval.\\n    * Use Conventional Commits (`<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>`) with clear descriptions of *why* changes were made.\\n11. **Pull Request Discipline**\\n    * PRs are created directly from the fully reviewed and verified state.\\n    * Open normal, ready-to-review PRs (avoid draft PRs unless explicitly requested).\\n12. **Gated Merge & Linear History**\\n    * **Reviewer Gating:** If a human or automated reviewer is assigned, wait for approval; never bypass assigned reviews.\\n    * **Clean CI:** Require 100% green checks on remote CI pipelines.\\n    * **Linear History:** Use squash merges for short-lived feature branches to preserve a clean, bisectable history.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Key Engineering Principles for AI / Autonomous Agents\\n\\n### A. Intentional Separation Between Prototyping and Production\\n* **Throwaway vs. Production Code:** Treat exploratory spikes as disposable instruments used solely to learn and de-risk. Once the architecture is proven, rebuild the feature using strict TDD rather than committing prototype spaghetti.\\n* **De-Risk Unknowns Early:** Never assume third-party library behavior or complex API integration will work as documented; prototype the integration boundary first.\\n\\n### B. Role Specialization (Separation of Concerns)\\nAvoid having a single agent prompt serve as architect, developer, QA, and reviewer all at once. Separate duties into distinct roles or sub-agents:\\n* **Planner / Architect:** Explores options, runs spikes, and outlines the slice-by-slice plan.\\n* **Implementer:** Focuses on writing tests and minimal functional code per slice.\\n* **UI Reviewer:** Focuses on design patterns, accessibility, and visual polish.\\n* **Verifier:** Operates the build pipeline, runs tests, checks compiler diagnostics, and drives E2E journeys.\\n* **Code Reviewer:** Performs static code audits and enforces best practices.\\n\\n### C. \\\"Validate the Instrument Before Believing Its Silence\\\"\\n* When an automated probe or test doesn't output an error, ensure the probe actually ran and didn't fail silently or test against stale binaries.\\n* Beware of caching, build-output mismatches, or test runners masking execution failures.\\n* When doing mutation testing (introducing a deliberate bug to see if tests catch it), confirm the test fails for the *expected reason*, not because the runner crashed.\\n\\n### D. Safe Concurrency & Shared State Isolation\\nWhen running parallel agents or worktrees on the same machine:\\n* **Isolate Shared Resources:** Isolate system-level singletons (ports, app registrations, test databases, shared fixtures).\\n* **Worktree Mutex:** Never mutate sources in a workspace while a verification or code review agent is running against it.\\n\\n### E. Single Source of Truth (SSOT)\\n* Maintain a single instruction file (e.g., `AGENTS.md`) as the authoritative source for project conventions, architecture decisions, and workflows. Pointer files (like `CLAUDE.md`) should merely reference this master file.\\n* Avoid redundant status or roadmap documents that drift out of sync; let issues/backlog trackers serve as the live status.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Quick Reference Checklist\\n\\n| Stage | Action | Criteria to Pass |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **0. Discovery** | Plan & Prototype | Feasibility proven via spikes, tradeoffs evaluated, vertical slices mapped out. |\\n| **1. Setup** | Inspect & Branch | Clean workspace, dedicated feature branch created from latest base. |\\n| **2. Scoping** | Thin Slice | Smallest coherent, end-to-end outcome selected. |\\n| **3. Build** | TDD Loop | Focused test fails first, minimal code makes it pass, refactored cleanly. |\\n| **4. UI Review** | Design Audit | Design standards, accessibility, and UI patterns validated. |\\n| **5. Verifier** | Verification Suite | Builds clean in all modes, static checks pass, tests green, E2E journeys verified. |\\n| **6. Code Review** | Peer/Expert Review | All uncommitted files reviewed; actionable feedback resolved. |\\n| **7. Ship** | Commit & PR | Conventional commit message, PR created, assigned reviews completed, squashed to main. |\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07ea9-3b2d-7200-b64b-ccfecb9fc7fb","create_time":1788831586.71529}}}
