{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T02:13:27.958Z","ordinal":22372,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07eca-579f-72b0-95b4-dd6f4e0bfedc","session_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","root_turn_id":"01a07eca-579f-72b0-95b4-dd6f4e0bfedc","response_id":"resp_0229170f3e6ec7c3016a9f6f3eb12887d18a3799cd9e1758de","usage":{"input_tokens":101990,"cached_input_tokens":100992,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":298,"reasoning_output_tokens":88,"total_tokens":102288},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":203224,"cached_input_tokens":201856,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":600,"reasoning_output_tokens":88,"total_tokens":203824},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":301054861,"cached_input_tokens":296965376,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":566721,"reasoning_output_tokens":196889,"total_tokens":301621582}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T02:13:28.138Z","ordinal":22373,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a055ce-e4d9-77c1-ac18-0944a8c71200","turn_id":"01a07eca-579f-72b0-95b4-dd6f4e0bfedc","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-aaeabdd8-c9fd-4269-af90-71301ab21cd4","process_id":"35720","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat AGENTS.md && git diff HEAD^ HEAD --binary -- AGENTS.md CONTRIBUTING.md .agents/skills .codex/agents | shasum -a 256 && mktemp -d /private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.XXXXXX"],"cwd":"file:///Users/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/cakebrew","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat AGENTS.md && git diff HEAD^ HEAD --binary -- AGENTS.md CONTRIBUTING.md .agents/skills .codex/agents | shasum -a 256 && mktemp -d /private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.XXXXXX"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew, written in Objective-C / AppKit.\nThis file is the sole authority for workflow, conventions and architecture.\n`CLAUDE.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` are pointers; skills and custom agents are\nrouting adapters, not independent policy. Update rules here first. Keep live\nstatus and roadmaps in issues, not additional instruction or status documents.\n\n## Engineering workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype and spike] --> B[2. Inspect and branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. Red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI and UX review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification]\n    G --> H\n    H -- Findings --> D\n    H -- Pass --> I[8. Expert code review]\n    I -- Findings --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Ready pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated squash merge]\n    L -- Code or test fixes needed --> D\n```\n\n### Discovery and scoping — steps 1–3\n\n1. **Plan and measure before committing to a design.** Inspect relevant code,\n   integration boundaries and tests. Test uncertain framework/API behavior,\n   feasibility, edge cases and performance with the smallest disposable spike.\n   Compare architectural fit, complexity, performance and maintenance burden;\n   produce ordered, independently shippable slices. Discard your spike and\n   scratch artifacts before production implementation; rebuild under TDD\n   rather than promoting prototype code.\n2. **Inspect before any mutation, including a spike.** Check repository state,\n   branches, worktrees and relevant configuration. Preserve unrelated staged,\n   unstaged and untracked files, especially `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/`.\n   Do not discard another person's work to obtain a clean tree. Isolate it or\n   stash and restore it when necessary for a rebase. Branch from latest `main`\n   using `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, `test/` or `perf/`.\n   Never commit directly to `main`.\n3. **Select one thin vertical slice.** Define the smallest cohesive end-to-end\n   outcome, acceptance criteria, exclusions, owned files and expected failing\n   test. One logical unit per PR; unrelated changes need separate branches.\n   A horizontal layer spanning the application is not a slice.\n\n### Implementation — steps 4–5\n\n4. **Red → green → refactor.** Write the smallest focused test first and\n   observe failure for the expected reason. A compile error for a missing API\n   counts; a runner crash does not. Implement only enough to pass, then\n   refactor with the suite green. Every behavior change needs this evidence,\n   including bugfixes and behavior-changing refactors. If red was missed,\n   deliberately mutate the implementation, observe the expected test failure,\n   and restore it. Report which method proved the test bites. Tests and the\n   code satisfying them belong in the same commit. Only pure documentation\n   and formatting are exempt from behavioral TDD.\n5. **Inspect the complete workspace.** Review the branch diff, staged and\n   unstaged changes, and `git status --untracked-files=all`. Remove only your\n   scratch files, probes and temporary instrumentation. Preserve unrelated\n   changes and exclude them from the slice.\n\n### Quality gates — steps 6–8\n\nRun these gates in order on stable sources. Reviewers receive the actual diff\nand observed evidence, not just implementation intent. They report findings;\nthey do not fix their own findings.\n\n6. **UI/UX review, when user-visible behavior or appearance changes.** A\n   separate reviewer launches the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`) and checks layout,\n   light/dark appearance, badges, accessibility and AppKit idioms. Behavior\n   needs a `CakebrewUITests` journey, not screenshots alone. Report anything\n   that could not be exercised; an environment limitation is not an approval.\n7. **Verification.** Build Debug and Release warning-free, run the full unit\n   suite and applicable integration checks, compile the UI test target, and\n   run the full UI journeys before opening the PR. Inspect diagnostics and\n   static-analysis results; every warning or failed check is a finding.\n   Confirm tests actually executed against the intended source and binary.\n   For instruction/documentation-only changes, use syntax, link and routing\n   validation plus independent policy review; app builds do not validate prose.\n   This exception does not cover executable code, tests or build/CI changes.\n8. **Independent expert review.** Review the full branch diff and all\n   uncommitted files after verification passes. Cover Objective-C idioms,\n   memory and async safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, regressions\n   and missing tests. Resolve every actionable finding before approval.\n\n**Fix loop:** send findings to the implementer. After any code or test edit,\nrestart the entire verification sequence and obtain fresh expert approval;\nrepeat UI review when the fix changes a user-facing surface. No partial rerun\nor prior approval substitutes for gates on the changed state. Updated policy\nor instructions likewise need fresh applicable validation and review.\n\n### Delivery — steps 9–11\n\nThe primary agent owns delivery. Perform only the commit, publish, PR and merge\nactions the user has authorized; completing a stage does not authorize the next.\n\n9. **Atomic Conventional Commit.** Stage only the verified, reviewed slice.\n   Use `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>` with type `feat`, `fix`,\n   `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `test`, `perf`, `build` or `ci`. The body explains\n   why and names the test coverage. Do not rewrite historical conventions.\n10. **Ready PR using `gh`.** No web UI; no draft unless requested. Explain\n    what changed, why, red-first or mutation evidence, verification results\n    and any unverified behavior with its reason.\n11. **Gated squash merge.** All required CI must be green, including both\n    **Build & Test** and **UI Tests**, for the current PR head. Wait for every\n    assigned human or automated review to approve and resolve actionable\n    feedback. Never bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n\nDuring delivery, diagnose failures and route in-scope code/test fixes through\nthe fix loop; hold publication or merge until its gates pass. Stale evidence\nrequires revalidation, not abandonment of the task. Stop for missing authority,\ncredentials, required user interaction or scope expansion. Do not treat an\nenvironment failure as a product defect or silently waive a gate.\n\nFor a CI UI failure, read the assertion and `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the\njob log before calling it flaky. Rerun once only when evidence indicates\ninfrastructure; otherwise fix the cause through the workflow.\n\nBefore deleting a stacked PR's parent branch, retarget the child PR to `main`;\ndeletion otherwise auto-closes it. Recheck the child's diff and gates against\nits new base. Merge with `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch`, then switch\nto `main` and `git pull --ff-only`, preserving unrelated work and respecting\nbranches checked out in other worktrees.\n\n## Role orchestration and evidence\n\nFor each feature, bugfix or behavior-changing refactor, the primary agent uses\n`$cakebrew-workflow` and delegates to the configured roles below. Planning,\nimplementation and gates must not collapse into one self-review. If a custom\nagent is unavailable, use a separate agent with its matching skill; disclose\nany unavailable gate rather than claiming it passed.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Required handoff |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary | Scope, decisions, role sequencing, authorized delivery |\n| Planner | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measured unknowns, alternatives, ordered slices, acceptance criteria, expected red, spike cleanup |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Owned diff, red/green or mutation evidence, remaining risks |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Observed visual/journey evidence; approval, findings or blocker |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Commands, diagnostics, test counts/skips, artifacts; pass, fail or blocker |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Approval or actionable findings with severity, file/line, impact and evidence |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary | Commit/PR/head identity, CI and assigned-review status, merge/cleanup result |\n\nGive each assignment its worktree, base/head and uncommitted diff, acceptance\ncriteria, explicit file ownership, evidence locations and exclusions. Tell\nmutating agents they are not alone and must preserve others' edits. Subagents\nmay not expand scope, edit project policy or perform delivery unless those\nactions are explicitly their assignment. Gate agents never edit sources.\n\nHandoffs must identify the state examined: worktree, base/head and relevant\nuncommitted file state. Include concise observed results and log/artifact paths,\nnot raw log floods. A result for another tree or stale binary is not evidence.\n\n**Safe concurrency:** use isolated worktrees for independent mutating work.\nNever change sources while a verifier or reviewer examines that workspace.\nSeparate derived-data directories do not isolate the app's `NSUserDefaults`,\ncatalog cache, running process, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app` or display.\nSerialize UI/runtime work unless those resources are safely isolated. Preserve\nand restore user state; do not replace the installed app as incidental testing.\n\n## Build and test commands\n\nUse a separate `-derivedDataPath` per concurrent build/worktree. Select the\nintended architecture explicitly if Xcode reports ambiguous destinations.\nRun the following sequence for executable changes:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n**Validate the instrument:** the app and unit schemes do not compile\n`CakebrewUITests.m`. After editing it, compile its own target even during the\ninner loop. Inspect actual assertions, executed test counts and skips, not\nonly `TEST SUCCEEDED`. `xcodebuild analyze` can exit zero with findings; read\nthe diagnostics. An empty `log show` result proves nothing unless the probe\nran and the predicate captured the intended event.\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds both configurations, runs static\nanalysis and both test jobs on `macos-26`, and uploads diagnostic crash logs\non failure. Weekly `brew-compat.yml` exercises parsers against real Homebrew\noutput; fixtures alone cannot detect upstream drift.\n\n## Testing contracts\n\n- **Mock boundary:** `-BPMockBrew` resolves `+sharedInterface` to\n  `BPMockHomebrewInterface`. Fixtures include `mockwget`, pinned `mockgit`,\n  `mockchrome` and `mockvscode`; operations are no-ops. Every new interface\n  method needs a mock override so UI tests never run real brew.\n  `BPMockFidelityTests` guards mutating selectors. Use `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`,\n  `-BPMockEmptyCleanup` and `-BPMockSlowCatalog` for their respective journeys;\n  the slow catalog makes progress and cancellation observable.\n- **Unit seams:** `CakebrewTests/` covers parsers, models and manager state.\n  `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` provides pure input → `BPFormula` tests;\n  private classes can be re-declared in tests.\n- **Deterministic journeys:** the shared UI launch helper waits for the\n  initial load (`mockwget`) before navigation to avoid the manager's reselect\n  race. Pin persisted launch-view settings in its argument domain, including\n  `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1` and `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"`; do not bypass\n  persistence in application code just because `-BPMockBrew` is present.\n- **Stable identity:** sidebar names repeat across groups. Use unlocalized\n  identifiers such as `sidebar.casks.installed`, not row indices or titles.\n  Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` because pinned rows append a glyph.\n- **Headless CI:** windows are not key, so typing/focus and `isHittable` are\n  unreliable. Assert `exists` and geometry (`frame.size`). System file panels\n  are out-of-process: unit-test their logic and UI-test presence/navigation.\n- **Sheet buttons:** scope actions to `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons`\n  to exclude Touch Bar mirrors. `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` accepting a\n  multi-match query does not prove the intended button can be clicked.\n\n## Architecture and UI conventions\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` funnels brew execution through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async/streaming) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may legitimately be\n  nil; guard them with `invokeOutputBlock:withString:`.\n- New lists follow: `BPListMode` → `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall` subclass\n  (arguments/parser) → manager property in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:`\n  → sidebar item/badge → mock fixture.\n- Casks are `BPFormula` objects with `cask == YES`, preserved by copying and\n  coding. `BPInstallationWindowController` dispatches `--cask` operations;\n  `statusForFormula:` reads only the matching namespace's lists.\n- Slow `brew formulae` and `brew casks` catalogs share a 24-hour disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). Avoid unnecessary cold catalog calls.\n- `FormulaeSideBarItem` values are outline row indices, including groups.\n  Insertions require updating the enum, View-menu tags in `MainMenu.xib` and\n  every switch/comparison (locate with `rg FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- Refresh badges with `refreshBadgeForListMode:` / `reloadItem:`, never sidebar\n  `reloadData`: it clears selection and can persist an unintended row.\n  Accessibility row identifiers belong on the cell's text field, not the\n  non-accessible `NSTableCellView` container.\n- Use `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …`, never `runModal` or\n  `self.view.window` (which can be nil during split-view reparenting).\n- Edit xibs as XML; run `xmllint --noout`, use unique `cbk-…` IDs and extend\n  `NSStackView` visibility-priority/custom-spacing arrays with arranged views.\n  Menu enablement uses Cocoa bindings (`currentFormula` / `currentFormulaPinned`),\n  not `validateMenuItem:`.\n- Add UI strings to all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/Localizable.strings` files and\n  run `plutil -lint`. Use English placeholders until translated; keep Homebrew\n  terms such as \"Casks\" untranslated. Base-internationalized xib menu titles\n  do not need `.strings` churn. Use SF Symbols for UI chrome, not bundled icons.\n\n## Distribution and platform constraints\n\n- Ship Developer ID signed, hardened and notarized, **without App Sandbox**.\n  Keep the privacy manifest, input validation and ATS protections. Sandbox\n  probes showed brew execution needs filesystem exceptions; even with them,\n  the redirected home directory splits Homebrew caches and sends Services\n  LaunchAgents into a container where launchd cannot use them. Do not add\n  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`; remeasure these boundaries before revisiting.\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` signs, notarizes and staples on `v*` tags;\n  its header lists required secrets. Releases need a Developer ID Application\n  certificate, separate from Apple Development; verify availability before\n  attempting distribution.\n- Ordinary Apple Development-signed Xcode builds satisfy the helper's team-OU\n  designated requirement. Real registration/Login Items testing requires a\n  signed app in `/Applications`. With authorization to replace the installed\n  app, use `scripts/install-signed.sh` (Release by default, or pass `Debug`):\n  it uses fresh derived data and verifies both signatures. Keep the final\n  **Verify embedded helper signature** build phase and its declared input;\n  it skips unsigned/ad-hoc builds intentionally.\n- Minimum macOS is latest major minus one (current project target: `15.0`).\n  Use the latest SDK (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned) and latest CI runner\n  (currently `macos-26`). At a new major release, update the minimum and runner;\n  `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Local environment diagnostics\n\n- Entire's session-sync pre-push hook hangs on this machine. Use\n  `git push --no-verify` for that known hook issue, not to bypass code checks.\n- For \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\", check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. Developer mode, not just `_developer` group\n  membership, was the local prerequisite; do not change host permissions\n  based on an unexplained runner failure. Full UI runs take minutes and belong\n  before PR creation, not in the TDD inner loop.\n- Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before visual testing.\n  A locked/sleeping display can cause infinite click coordinates, background\n  activation failures or `screencapture` errors. Ask for the display to be\n  unlocked/awake; those are not product failures and cannot be fixed in code.\n- `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` does not reliably force AppKit appearance.\n  Check the rendered appearance; coordinate a manual System Settings change\n  when needed rather than silently changing the user's global preferences.\n- While the 1Password SSH signing agent remains broken, use\n  `git -c commit.gpgsign=false` for commits. Re-enable signing when fixed.\nfaec16d94a4a5c6c0657032a58b833c60e17849acd19a305fa23d52e7ba047ca  -\n/private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.Cx78mk\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew, written in Objective-C / AppKit.\nThis file is the sole authority for workflow, conventions and architecture.\n`CLAUDE.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` are pointers; skills and custom agents are\nrouting adapters, not independent policy. Update rules here first. Keep live\nstatus and roadmaps in issues, not additional instruction or status documents.\n\n## Engineering workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype and spike] --> B[2. Inspect and branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. Red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI and UX review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification]\n    G --> H\n    H -- Findings --> D\n    H -- Pass --> I[8. Expert code review]\n    I -- Findings --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Ready pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated squash merge]\n    L -- Code or test fixes needed --> D\n```\n\n### Discovery and scoping — steps 1–3\n\n1. **Plan and measure before committing to a design.** Inspect relevant code,\n   integration boundaries and tests. Test uncertain framework/API behavior,\n   feasibility, edge cases and performance with the smallest disposable spike.\n   Compare architectural fit, complexity, performance and maintenance burden;\n   produce ordered, independently shippable slices. Discard your spike and\n   scratch artifacts before production implementation; rebuild under TDD\n   rather than promoting prototype code.\n2. **Inspect before any mutation, including a spike.** Check repository state,\n   branches, worktrees and relevant configuration. Preserve unrelated staged,\n   unstaged and untracked files, especially `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/`.\n   Do not discard another person's work to obtain a clean tree. Isolate it or\n   stash and restore it when necessary for a rebase. Branch from latest `main`\n   using `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, `test/` or `perf/`.\n   Never commit directly to `main`.\n3. **Select one thin vertical slice.** Define the smallest cohesive end-to-end\n   outcome, acceptance criteria, exclusions, owned files and expected failing\n   test. One logical unit per PR; unrelated changes need separate branches.\n   A horizontal layer spanning the application is not a slice.\n\n### Implementation — steps 4–5\n\n4. **Red → green → refactor.** Write the smallest focused test first and\n   observe failure for the expected reason. A compile error for a missing API\n   counts; a runner crash does not. Implement only enough to pass, then\n   refactor with the suite green. Every behavior change needs this evidence,\n   including bugfixes and behavior-changing refactors. If red was missed,\n   deliberately mutate the implementation, observe the expected test failure,\n   and restore it. Report which method proved the test bites. Tests and the\n   code satisfying them belong in the same commit. Only pure documentation\n   and formatting are exempt from behavioral TDD.\n5. **Inspect the complete workspace.** Review the branch diff, staged and\n   unstaged changes, and `git status --untracked-files=all`. Remove only your\n   scratch files, probes and temporary instrumentation. Preserve unrelated\n   changes and exclude them from the slice.\n\n### Quality gates — steps 6–8\n\nRun these gates in order on stable sources. Reviewers receive the actual diff\nand observed evidence, not just implementation intent. They report findings;\nthey do not fix their own findings.\n\n6. **UI/UX review, when user-visible behavior or appearance changes.** A\n   separate reviewer launches the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`) and checks layout,\n   light/dark appearance, badges, accessibility and AppKit idioms. Behavior\n   needs a `CakebrewUITests` journey, not screenshots alone. Report anything\n   that could not be exercised; an environment limitation is not an approval.\n7. **Verification.** Build Debug and Release warning-free, run the full unit\n   suite and applicable integration checks, compile the UI test target, and\n   run the full UI journeys before opening the PR. Inspect diagnostics and\n   static-analysis results; every warning or failed check is a finding.\n   Confirm tests actually executed against the intended source and binary.\n   For instruction/documentation-only changes, use syntax, link and routing\n   validation plus independent policy review; app builds do not validate prose.\n   This exception does not cover executable code, tests or build/CI changes.\n8. **Independent expert review.** Review the full branch diff and all\n   uncommitted files after verification passes. Cover Objective-C idioms,\n   memory and async safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, regressions\n   and missing tests. Resolve every actionable finding before approval.\n\n**Fix loop:** send findings to the implementer. After any code or test edit,\nrestart the entire verification sequence and obtain fresh expert approval;\nrepeat UI review when the fix changes a user-facing surface. No partial rerun\nor prior approval substitutes for gates on the changed state. Updated policy\nor instructions likewise need fresh applicable validation and review.\n\n### Delivery — steps 9–11\n\nThe primary agent owns delivery. Perform only the commit, publish, PR and merge\nactions the user has authorized; completing a stage does not authorize the next.\n\n9. **Atomic Conventional Commit.** Stage only the verified, reviewed slice.\n   Use `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>` with type `feat`, `fix`,\n   `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `test`, `perf`, `build` or `ci`. The body explains\n   why and names the test coverage. Do not rewrite historical conventions.\n10. **Ready PR using `gh`.** No web UI; no draft unless requested. Explain\n    what changed, why, red-first or mutation evidence, verification results\n    and any unverified behavior with its reason.\n11. **Gated squash merge.** All required CI must be green, including both\n    **Build & Test** and **UI Tests**, for the current PR head. Wait for every\n    assigned human or automated review to approve and resolve actionable\n    feedback. Never bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n\nDuring delivery, diagnose failures and route in-scope code/test fixes through\nthe fix loop; hold publication or merge until its gates pass. Stale evidence\nrequires revalidation, not abandonment of the task. Stop for missing authority,\ncredentials, required user interaction or scope expansion. Do not treat an\nenvironment failure as a product defect or silently waive a gate.\n\nFor a CI UI failure, read the assertion and `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the\njob log before calling it flaky. Rerun once only when evidence indicates\ninfrastructure; otherwise fix the cause through the workflow.\n\nBefore deleting a stacked PR's parent branch, retarget the child PR to `main`;\ndeletion otherwise auto-closes it. Recheck the child's diff and gates against\nits new base. Merge with `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch`, then switch\nto `main` and `git pull --ff-only`, preserving unrelated work and respecting\nbranches checked out in other worktrees.\n\n## Role orchestration and evidence\n\nFor each feature, bugfix or behavior-changing refactor, the primary agent uses\n`$cakebrew-workflow` and delegates to the configured roles below. Planning,\nimplementation and gates must not collapse into one self-review. If a custom\nagent is unavailable, use a separate agent with its matching skill; disclose\nany unavailable gate rather than claiming it passed.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Required handoff |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary | Scope, decisions, role sequencing, authorized delivery |\n| Planner | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measured unknowns, alternatives, ordered slices, acceptance criteria, expected red, spike cleanup |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Owned diff, red/green or mutation evidence, remaining risks |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Observed visual/journey evidence; approval, findings or blocker |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Commands, diagnostics, test counts/skips, artifacts; pass, fail or blocker |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Approval or actionable findings with severity, file/line, impact and evidence |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary | Commit/PR/head identity, CI and assigned-review status, merge/cleanup result |\n\nGive each assignment its worktree, base/head and uncommitted diff, acceptance\ncriteria, explicit file ownership, evidence locations and exclusions. Tell\nmutating agents they are not alone and must preserve others' edits. Subagents\nmay not expand scope, edit project policy or perform delivery unless those\nactions are explicitly their assignment. Gate agents never edit sources.\n\nHandoffs must identify the state examined: worktree, base/head and relevant\nuncommitted file state. Include concise observed results and log/artifact paths,\nnot raw log floods. A result for another tree or stale binary is not evidence.\n\n**Safe concurrency:** use isolated worktrees for independent mutating work.\nNever change sources while a verifier or reviewer examines that workspace.\nSeparate derived-data directories do not isolate the app's `NSUserDefaults`,\ncatalog cache, running process, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app` or display.\nSerialize UI/runtime work unless those resources are safely isolated. Preserve\nand restore user state; do not replace the installed app as incidental testing.\n\n## Build and test commands\n\nUse a separate `-derivedDataPath` per concurrent build/worktree. Select the\nintended architecture explicitly if Xcode reports ambiguous destinations.\nRun the following sequence for executable changes:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n**Validate the instrument:** the app and unit schemes do not compile\n`CakebrewUITests.m`. After editing it, compile its own target even during the\ninner loop. Inspect actual assertions, executed test counts and skips, not\nonly `TEST SUCCEEDED`. `xcodebuild analyze` can exit zero with findings; read\nthe diagnostics. An empty `log show` result proves nothing unless the probe\nran and the predicate captured the intended event.\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds both configurations, runs static\nanalysis and both test jobs on `macos-26`, and uploads diagnostic crash logs\non failure. Weekly `brew-compat.yml` exercises parsers against real Homebrew\noutput; fixtures alone cannot detect upstream drift.\n\n## Testing contracts\n\n- **Mock boundary:** `-BPMockBrew` resolves `+sharedInterface` to\n  `BPMockHomebrewInterface`. Fixtures include `mockwget`, pinned `mockgit`,\n  `mockchrome` and `mockvscode`; operations are no-ops. Every new interface\n  method needs a mock override so UI tests never run real brew.\n  `BPMockFidelityTests` guards mutating selectors. Use `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`,\n  `-BPMockEmptyCleanup` and `-BPMockSlowCatalog` for their respective journeys;\n  the slow catalog makes progress and cancellation observable.\n- **Unit seams:** `CakebrewTests/` covers parsers, models and manager state.\n  `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` provides pure input → `BPFormula` tests;\n  private classes can be re-declared in tests.\n- **Deterministic journeys:** the shared UI launch helper waits for the\n  initial load (`mockwget`) before navigation to avoid the manager's reselect\n  race. Pin persisted launch-view settings in its argument domain, including\n  `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1` and `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"`; do not bypass\n  persistence in application code just because `-BPMockBrew` is present.\n- **Stable identity:** sidebar names repeat across groups. Use unlocalized\n  identifiers such as `sidebar.casks.installed`, not row indices or titles.\n  Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` because pinned rows append a glyph.\n- **Headless CI:** windows are not key, so typing/focus and `isHittable` are\n  unreliable. Assert `exists` and geometry (`frame.size`). System file panels\n  are out-of-process: unit-test their logic and UI-test presence/navigation.\n- **Sheet buttons:** scope actions to `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons`\n  to exclude Touch Bar mirrors. `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` accepting a\n  multi-match query does not prove the intended button can be clicked.\n\n## Architecture and UI conventions\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` funnels brew execution through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async/streaming) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may legitimately be\n  nil; guard them with `invokeOutputBlock:withString:`.\n- New lists follow: `BPListMode` → `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall` subclass\n  (arguments/parser) → manager property in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:`\n  → sidebar item/badge → mock fixture.\n- Casks are `BPFormula` objects with `cask == YES`, preserved by copying and\n  coding. `BPInstallationWindowController` dispatches `--cask` operations;\n  `statusForFormula:` reads only the matching namespace's lists.\n- Slow `brew formulae` and `brew casks` catalogs share a 24-hour disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). Avoid unnecessary cold catalog calls.\n- `FormulaeSideBarItem` values are outline row indices, including groups.\n  Insertions require updating the enum, View-menu tags in `MainMenu.xib` and\n  every switch/comparison (locate with `rg FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- Refresh badges with `refreshBadgeForListMode:` / `reloadItem:`, never sidebar\n  `reloadData`: it clears selection and can persist an unintended row.\n  Accessibility row identifiers belong on the cell's text field, not the\n  non-accessible `NSTableCellView` container.\n- Use `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …`, never `runModal` or\n  `self.view.window` (which can be nil during split-view reparenting).\n- Edit xibs as XML; run `xmllint --noout`, use unique `cbk-…` IDs and extend\n  `NSStackView` visibility-priority/custom-spacing arrays with arranged views.\n  Menu enablement uses Cocoa bindings (`currentFormula` / `currentFormulaPinned`),\n  not `validateMenuItem:`.\n- Add UI strings to all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/Localizable.strings` files and\n  run `plutil -lint`. Use English placeholders until translated; keep Homebrew\n  terms such as \"Casks\" untranslated. Base-internationalized xib menu titles\n  do not need `.strings` churn. Use SF Symbols for UI chrome, not bundled icons.\n\n## Distribution and platform constraints\n\n- Ship Developer ID signed, hardened and notarized, **without App Sandbox**.\n  Keep the privacy manifest, input validation and ATS protections. Sandbox\n  probes showed brew execution needs filesystem exceptions; even with them,\n  the redirected home directory splits Homebrew caches and sends Services\n  LaunchAgents into a container where launchd cannot use them. Do not add\n  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`; remeasure these boundaries before revisiting.\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` signs, notarizes and staples on `v*` tags;\n  its header lists required secrets. Releases need a Developer ID Application\n  certificate, separate from Apple Development; verify availability before\n  attempting distribution.\n- Ordinary Apple Development-signed Xcode builds satisfy the helper's team-OU\n  designated requirement. Real registration/Login Items testing requires a\n  signed app in `/Applications`. With authorization to replace the installed\n  app, use `scripts/install-signed.sh` (Release by default, or pass `Debug`):\n  it uses fresh derived data and verifies both signatures. Keep the final\n  **Verify embedded helper signature** build phase and its declared input;\n  it skips unsigned/ad-hoc builds intentionally.\n- Minimum macOS is latest major minus one (current project target: `15.0`).\n  Use the latest SDK (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned) and latest CI runner\n  (currently `macos-26`). At a new major release, update the minimum and runner;\n  `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Local environment diagnostics\n\n- Entire's session-sync pre-push hook hangs on this machine. Use\n  `git push --no-verify` for that known hook issue, not to bypass code checks.\n- For \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\", check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. Developer mode, not just `_developer` group\n  membership, was the local prerequisite; do not change host permissions\n  based on an unexplained runner failure. Full UI runs take minutes and belong\n  before PR creation, not in the TDD inner loop.\n- Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before visual testing.\n  A locked/sleeping display can cause infinite click coordinates, background\n  activation failures or `screencapture` errors. Ask for the display to be\n  unlocked/awake; those are not product failures and cannot be fixed in code.\n- `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` does not reliably force AppKit appearance.\n  Check the rendered appearance; coordinate a manual System Settings change\n  when needed rather than silently changing the user's global preferences.\n- While the 1Password SSH signing agent remains broken, use\n  `git -c commit.gpgsign=false` for commits. Re-enable signing when fixed.\nfaec16d94a4a5c6c0657032a58b833c60e17849acd19a305fa23d52e7ba047ca  -\n/private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.Cx78mk\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":4768},"formatted_output":"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\n\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew, written in Objective-C / AppKit.\nThis file is the sole authority for workflow, conventions and architecture.\n`CLAUDE.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` are pointers; skills and custom agents are\nrouting adapters, not independent policy. Update rules here first. Keep live\nstatus and roadmaps in issues, not additional instruction or status documents.\n\n## Engineering workflow\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[1. Plan, prototype and spike] --> B[2. Inspect and branch]\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\n    C --> D[4. Red, green, refactor]\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI and UX review]\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification]\n    G --> H\n    H -- Findings --> D\n    H -- Pass --> I[8. Expert code review]\n    I -- Findings --> D\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\n    J --> K[10. Ready pull request]\n    K --> L[11. Gated squash merge]\n    L -- Code or test fixes needed --> D\n```\n\n### Discovery and scoping — steps 1–3\n\n1. **Plan and measure before committing to a design.** Inspect relevant code,\n   integration boundaries and tests. Test uncertain framework/API behavior,\n   feasibility, edge cases and performance with the smallest disposable spike.\n   Compare architectural fit, complexity, performance and maintenance burden;\n   produce ordered, independently shippable slices. Discard your spike and\n   scratch artifacts before production implementation; rebuild under TDD\n   rather than promoting prototype code.\n2. **Inspect before any mutation, including a spike.** Check repository state,\n   branches, worktrees and relevant configuration. Preserve unrelated staged,\n   unstaged and untracked files, especially `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/`.\n   Do not discard another person's work to obtain a clean tree. Isolate it or\n   stash and restore it when necessary for a rebase. Branch from latest `main`\n   using `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, `test/` or `perf/`.\n   Never commit directly to `main`.\n3. **Select one thin vertical slice.** Define the smallest cohesive end-to-end\n   outcome, acceptance criteria, exclusions, owned files and expected failing\n   test. One logical unit per PR; unrelated changes need separate branches.\n   A horizontal layer spanning the application is not a slice.\n\n### Implementation — steps 4–5\n\n4. **Red → green → refactor.** Write the smallest focused test first and\n   observe failure for the expected reason. A compile error for a missing API\n   counts; a runner crash does not. Implement only enough to pass, then\n   refactor with the suite green. Every behavior change needs this evidence,\n   including bugfixes and behavior-changing refactors. If red was missed,\n   deliberately mutate the implementation, observe the expected test failure,\n   and restore it. Report which method proved the test bites. Tests and the\n   code satisfying them belong in the same commit. Only pure documentation\n   and formatting are exempt from behavioral TDD.\n5. **Inspect the complete workspace.** Review the branch diff, staged and\n   unstaged changes, and `git status --untracked-files=all`. Remove only your\n   scratch files, probes and temporary instrumentation. Preserve unrelated\n   changes and exclude them from the slice.\n\n### Quality gates — steps 6–8\n\nRun these gates in order on stable sources. Reviewers receive the actual diff\nand observed evidence, not just implementation intent. They report findings;\nthey do not fix their own findings.\n\n6. **UI/UX review, when user-visible behavior or appearance changes.** A\n   separate reviewer launches the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`) and checks layout,\n   light/dark appearance, badges, accessibility and AppKit idioms. Behavior\n   needs a `CakebrewUITests` journey, not screenshots alone. Report anything\n   that could not be exercised; an environment limitation is not an approval.\n7. **Verification.** Build Debug and Release warning-free, run the full unit\n   suite and applicable integration checks, compile the UI test target, and\n   run the full UI journeys before opening the PR. Inspect diagnostics and\n   static-analysis results; every warning or failed check is a finding.\n   Confirm tests actually executed against the intended source and binary.\n   For instruction/documentation-only changes, use syntax, link and routing\n   validation plus independent policy review; app builds do not validate prose.\n   This exception does not cover executable code, tests or build/CI changes.\n8. **Independent expert review.** Review the full branch diff and all\n   uncommitted files after verification passes. Cover Objective-C idioms,\n   memory and async safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, regressions\n   and missing tests. Resolve every actionable finding before approval.\n\n**Fix loop:** send findings to the implementer. After any code or test edit,\nrestart the entire verification sequence and obtain fresh expert approval;\nrepeat UI review when the fix changes a user-facing surface. No partial rerun\nor prior approval substitutes for gates on the changed state. Updated policy\nor instructions likewise need fresh applicable validation and review.\n\n### Delivery — steps 9–11\n\nThe primary agent owns delivery. Perform only the commit, publish, PR and merge\nactions the user has authorized; completing a stage does not authorize the next.\n\n9. **Atomic Conventional Commit.** Stage only the verified, reviewed slice.\n   Use `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>` with type `feat`, `fix`,\n   `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `test`, `perf`, `build` or `ci`. The body explains\n   why and names the test coverage. Do not rewrite historical conventions.\n10. **Ready PR using `gh`.** No web UI; no draft unless requested. Explain\n    what changed, why, red-first or mutation evidence, verification results\n    and any unverified behavior with its reason.\n11. **Gated squash merge.** All required CI must be green, including both\n    **Build & Test** and **UI Tests**, for the current PR head. Wait for every\n    assigned human or automated review to approve and resolve actionable\n    feedback. Never bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\n\nDuring delivery, diagnose failures and route in-scope code/test fixes through\nthe fix loop; hold publication or merge until its gates pass. Stale evidence\nrequires revalidation, not abandonment of the task. Stop for missing authority,\ncredentials, required user interaction or scope expansion. Do not treat an\nenvironment failure as a product defect or silently waive a gate.\n\nFor a CI UI failure, read the assertion and `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the\njob log before calling it flaky. Rerun once only when evidence indicates\ninfrastructure; otherwise fix the cause through the workflow.\n\nBefore deleting a stacked PR's parent branch, retarget the child PR to `main`;\ndeletion otherwise auto-closes it. Recheck the child's diff and gates against\nits new base. Merge with `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch`, then switch\nto `main` and `git pull --ff-only`, preserving unrelated work and respecting\nbranches checked out in other worktrees.\n\n## Role orchestration and evidence\n\nFor each feature, bugfix or behavior-changing refactor, the primary agent uses\n`$cakebrew-workflow` and delegates to the configured roles below. Planning,\nimplementation and gates must not collapse into one self-review. If a custom\nagent is unavailable, use a separate agent with its matching skill; disclose\nany unavailable gate rather than claiming it passed.\n\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Required handoff |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary | Scope, decisions, role sequencing, authorized delivery |\n| Planner | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measured unknowns, alternatives, ordered slices, acceptance criteria, expected red, spike cleanup |\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Owned diff, red/green or mutation evidence, remaining risks |\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Observed visual/journey evidence; approval, findings or blocker |\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Commands, diagnostics, test counts/skips, artifacts; pass, fail or blocker |\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Approval or actionable findings with severity, file/line, impact and evidence |\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary | Commit/PR/head identity, CI and assigned-review status, merge/cleanup result |\n\nGive each assignment its worktree, base/head and uncommitted diff, acceptance\ncriteria, explicit file ownership, evidence locations and exclusions. Tell\nmutating agents they are not alone and must preserve others' edits. Subagents\nmay not expand scope, edit project policy or perform delivery unless those\nactions are explicitly their assignment. Gate agents never edit sources.\n\nHandoffs must identify the state examined: worktree, base/head and relevant\nuncommitted file state. Include concise observed results and log/artifact paths,\nnot raw log floods. A result for another tree or stale binary is not evidence.\n\n**Safe concurrency:** use isolated worktrees for independent mutating work.\nNever change sources while a verifier or reviewer examines that workspace.\nSeparate derived-data directories do not isolate the app's `NSUserDefaults`,\ncatalog cache, running process, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app` or display.\nSerialize UI/runtime work unless those resources are safely isolated. Preserve\nand restore user state; do not replace the installed app as incidental testing.\n\n## Build and test commands\n\nUse a separate `-derivedDataPath` per concurrent build/worktree. Select the\nintended architecture explicitly if Xcode reports ambiguous destinations.\nRun the following sequence for executable changes:\n\n```sh\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\"-\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\"\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\"\"\n```\n\n**Validate the instrument:** the app and unit schemes do not compile\n`CakebrewUITests.m`. After editing it, compile its own target even during the\ninner loop. Inspect actual assertions, executed test counts and skips, not\nonly `TEST SUCCEEDED`. `xcodebuild analyze` can exit zero with findings; read\nthe diagnostics. An empty `log show` result proves nothing unless the probe\nran and the predicate captured the intended event.\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds both configurations, runs static\nanalysis and both test jobs on `macos-26`, and uploads diagnostic crash logs\non failure. Weekly `brew-compat.yml` exercises parsers against real Homebrew\noutput; fixtures alone cannot detect upstream drift.\n\n## Testing contracts\n\n- **Mock boundary:** `-BPMockBrew` resolves `+sharedInterface` to\n  `BPMockHomebrewInterface`. Fixtures include `mockwget`, pinned `mockgit`,\n  `mockchrome` and `mockvscode`; operations are no-ops. Every new interface\n  method needs a mock override so UI tests never run real brew.\n  `BPMockFidelityTests` guards mutating selectors. Use `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`,\n  `-BPMockEmptyCleanup` and `-BPMockSlowCatalog` for their respective journeys;\n  the slow catalog makes progress and cancellation observable.\n- **Unit seams:** `CakebrewTests/` covers parsers, models and manager state.\n  `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` provides pure input → `BPFormula` tests;\n  private classes can be re-declared in tests.\n- **Deterministic journeys:** the shared UI launch helper waits for the\n  initial load (`mockwget`) before navigation to avoid the manager's reselect\n  race. Pin persisted launch-view settings in its argument domain, including\n  `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1` and `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \"\"`; do not bypass\n  persistence in application code just because `-BPMockBrew` is present.\n- **Stable identity:** sidebar names repeat across groups. Use unlocalized\n  identifiers such as `sidebar.casks.installed`, not row indices or titles.\n  Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` because pinned rows append a glyph.\n- **Headless CI:** windows are not key, so typing/focus and `isHittable` are\n  unreliable. Assert `exists` and geometry (`frame.size`). System file panels\n  are out-of-process: unit-test their logic and UI-test presence/navigation.\n- **Sheet buttons:** scope actions to `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons`\n  to exclude Touch Bar mirrors. `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` accepting a\n  multi-match query does not prove the intended button can be clicked.\n\n## Architecture and UI conventions\n\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` funnels brew execution through\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async/streaming) or\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may legitimately be\n  nil; guard them with `invokeOutputBlock:withString:`.\n- New lists follow: `BPListMode` → `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall` subclass\n  (arguments/parser) → manager property in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:`\n  → sidebar item/badge → mock fixture.\n- Casks are `BPFormula` objects with `cask == YES`, preserved by copying and\n  coding. `BPInstallationWindowController` dispatches `--cask` operations;\n  `statusForFormula:` reads only the matching namespace's lists.\n- Slow `brew formulae` and `brew casks` catalogs share a 24-hour disk cache\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). Avoid unnecessary cold catalog calls.\n- `FormulaeSideBarItem` values are outline row indices, including groups.\n  Insertions require updating the enum, View-menu tags in `MainMenu.xib` and\n  every switch/comparison (locate with `rg FormulaeSideBarItem`).\n- Refresh badges with `refreshBadgeForListMode:` / `reloadItem:`, never sidebar\n  `reloadData`: it clears selection and can persist an unintended row.\n  Accessibility row identifiers belong on the cell's text field, not the\n  non-accessible `NSTableCellView` container.\n- Use `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …`, never `runModal` or\n  `self.view.window` (which can be nil during split-view reparenting).\n- Edit xibs as XML; run `xmllint --noout`, use unique `cbk-…` IDs and extend\n  `NSStackView` visibility-priority/custom-spacing arrays with arranged views.\n  Menu enablement uses Cocoa bindings (`currentFormula` / `currentFormulaPinned`),\n  not `validateMenuItem:`.\n- Add UI strings to all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/Localizable.strings` files and\n  run `plutil -lint`. Use English placeholders until translated; keep Homebrew\n  terms such as \"Casks\" untranslated. Base-internationalized xib menu titles\n  do not need `.strings` churn. Use SF Symbols for UI chrome, not bundled icons.\n\n## Distribution and platform constraints\n\n- Ship Developer ID signed, hardened and notarized, **without App Sandbox**.\n  Keep the privacy manifest, input validation and ATS protections. Sandbox\n  probes showed brew execution needs filesystem exceptions; even with them,\n  the redirected home directory splits Homebrew caches and sends Services\n  LaunchAgents into a container where launchd cannot use them. Do not add\n  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`; remeasure these boundaries before revisiting.\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` signs, notarizes and staples on `v*` tags;\n  its header lists required secrets. Releases need a Developer ID Application\n  certificate, separate from Apple Development; verify availability before\n  attempting distribution.\n- Ordinary Apple Development-signed Xcode builds satisfy the helper's team-OU\n  designated requirement. Real registration/Login Items testing requires a\n  signed app in `/Applications`. With authorization to replace the installed\n  app, use `scripts/install-signed.sh` (Release by default, or pass `Debug`):\n  it uses fresh derived data and verifies both signatures. Keep the final\n  **Verify embedded helper signature** build phase and its declared input;\n  it skips unsigned/ad-hoc builds intentionally.\n- Minimum macOS is latest major minus one (current project target: `15.0`).\n  Use the latest SDK (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned) and latest CI runner\n  (currently `macos-26`). At a new major release, update the minimum and runner;\n  `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\n\n## Local environment diagnostics\n\n- Entire's session-sync pre-push hook hangs on this machine. Use\n  `git push --no-verify` for that known hook issue, not to bypass code checks.\n- For \"Timed out while enabling automation mode\", check\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. Developer mode, not just `_developer` group\n  membership, was the local prerequisite; do not change host permissions\n  based on an unexplained runner failure. Full UI runs take minutes and belong\n  before PR creation, not in the TDD inner loop.\n- Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before visual testing.\n  A locked/sleeping display can cause infinite click coordinates, background\n  activation failures or `screencapture` errors. Ask for the display to be\n  unlocked/awake; those are not product failures and cannot be fixed in code.\n- `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` does not reliably force AppKit appearance.\n  Check the rendered appearance; coordinate a manual System Settings change\n  when needed rather than silently changing the user's global preferences.\n- While the 1Password SSH signing agent remains broken, use\n  `git -c commit.gpgsign=false` for commits. Re-enable signing when fixed.\nfaec16d94a4a5c6c0657032a58b833c60e17849acd19a305fa23d52e7ba047ca  -\n/private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.Cx78mk\n"},"started_at_ms":1788833608137,"completed_at_ms":1788833608138}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-08T02:13:33.421Z","ordinal":22374,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a07eca-c66d-77f3-b7d4-a6ac5ed2a61f","call_id":"call_4UWT7W8AMdKtYEOROVbDjqOq","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 5.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f78498\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001531,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":0,\"output\":\"\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"9e7fb6\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000004768,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":4558,\"output\":\"# Agent Guide — Cakebrew\\n\\nCakebrew is a native macOS GUI for Homebrew, written in Objective-C / AppKit.\\nThis file is the sole authority for workflow, conventions and architecture.\\n`CLAUDE.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` are pointers; skills and custom agents are\\nrouting adapters, not independent policy. Update rules here first. Keep live\\nstatus and roadmaps in issues, not additional instruction or status documents.\\n\\n## Engineering workflow\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    A[1. Plan, prototype and spike] --> B[2. Inspect and branch]\\n    B --> C[3. Scope a thin vertical slice]\\n    C --> D[4. Red, green, refactor]\\n    D --> E[5. Inspect the whole workspace diff]\\n    E --> F{User-visible change?}\\n    F -- Yes --> G[6. UI and UX review]\\n    F -- No --> H[7. Verification]\\n    G --> H\\n    H -- Findings --> D\\n    H -- Pass --> I[8. Expert code review]\\n    I -- Findings --> D\\n    I -- Approved --> J[9. Atomic commit]\\n    J --> K[10. Ready pull request]\\n    K --> L[11. Gated squash merge]\\n    L -- Code or test fixes needed --> D\\n```\\n\\n### Discovery and scoping — steps 1–3\\n\\n1. **Plan and measure before committing to a design.** Inspect relevant code,\\n   integration boundaries and tests. Test uncertain framework/API behavior,\\n   feasibility, edge cases and performance with the smallest disposable spike.\\n   Compare architectural fit, complexity, performance and maintenance burden;\\n   produce ordered, independently shippable slices. Discard your spike and\\n   scratch artifacts before production implementation; rebuild under TDD\\n   rather than promoting prototype code.\\n2. **Inspect before any mutation, including a spike.** Check repository state,\\n   branches, worktrees and relevant configuration. Preserve unrelated staged,\\n   unstaged and untracked files, especially `.claude/`, `.codex/` and `.entire/`.\\n   Do not discard another person's work to obtain a clean tree. Isolate it or\\n   stash and restore it when necessary for a rebase. Branch from latest `main`\\n   using `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, `test/` or `perf/`.\\n   Never commit directly to `main`.\\n3. **Select one thin vertical slice.** Define the smallest cohesive end-to-end\\n   outcome, acceptance criteria, exclusions, owned files and expected failing\\n   test. One logical unit per PR; unrelated changes need separate branches.\\n   A horizontal layer spanning the application is not a slice.\\n\\n### Implementation — steps 4–5\\n\\n4. **Red → green → refactor.** Write the smallest focused test first and\\n   observe failure for the expected reason. A compile error for a missing API\\n   counts; a runner crash does not. Implement only enough to pass, then\\n   refactor with the suite green. Every behavior change needs this evidence,\\n   including bugfixes and behavior-changing refactors. If red was missed,\\n   deliberately mutate the implementation, observe the expected test failure,\\n   and restore it. Report which method proved the test bites. Tests and the\\n   code satisfying them belong in the same commit. Only pure documentation\\n   and formatting are exempt from behavioral TDD.\\n5. **Inspect the complete workspace.** Review the branch diff, staged and\\n   unstaged changes, and `git status --untracked-files=all`. Remove only your\\n   scratch files, probes and temporary instrumentation. Preserve unrelated\\n   changes and exclude them from the slice.\\n\\n### Quality gates — steps 6–8\\n\\nRun these gates in order on stable sources. Reviewers receive the actual diff\\nand observed evidence, not just implementation intent. They report findings;\\nthey do not fix their own findings.\\n\\n6. **UI/UX review, when user-visible behavior or appearance changes.** A\\n   separate reviewer launches the mock build (`-BPMockBrew`) and checks layout,\\n   light/dark appearance, badges, accessibility and AppKit idioms. Behavior\\n   needs a `CakebrewUITests` journey, not screenshots alone. Report anything\\n   that could not be exercised; an environment limitation is not an approval.\\n7. **Verification.** Build Debug and Release warning-free, run the full unit\\n   suite and applicable integration checks, compile the UI test target, and\\n   run the full UI journeys before opening the PR. Inspect diagnostics and\\n   static-analysis results; every warning or failed check is a finding.\\n   Confirm tests actually executed against the intended source and binary.\\n   For instruction/documentation-only changes, use syntax, link and routing\\n   validation plus independent policy review; app builds do not validate prose.\\n   This exception does not cover executable code, tests or build/CI changes.\\n8. **Independent expert review.** Review the full branch diff and all\\n   uncommitted files after verification passes. Cover Objective-C idioms,\\n   memory and async safety, performance, architecture, edge cases, regressions\\n   and missing tests. Resolve every actionable finding before approval.\\n\\n**Fix loop:** send findings to the implementer. After any code or test edit,\\nrestart the entire verification sequence and obtain fresh expert approval;\\nrepeat UI review when the fix changes a user-facing surface. No partial rerun\\nor prior approval substitutes for gates on the changed state. Updated policy\\nor instructions likewise need fresh applicable validation and review.\\n\\n### Delivery — steps 9–11\\n\\nThe primary agent owns delivery. Perform only the commit, publish, PR and merge\\nactions the user has authorized; completing a stage does not authorize the next.\\n\\n9. **Atomic Conventional Commit.** Stage only the verified, reviewed slice.\\n   Use `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary>` with type `feat`, `fix`,\\n   `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `test`, `perf`, `build` or `ci`. The body explains\\n   why and names the test coverage. Do not rewrite historical conventions.\\n10. **Ready PR using `gh`.** No web UI; no draft unless requested. Explain\\n    what changed, why, red-first or mutation evidence, verification results\\n    and any unverified behavior with its reason.\\n11. **Gated squash merge.** All required CI must be green, including both\\n    **Build & Test** and **UI Tests**, for the current PR head. Wait for every\\n    assigned human or automated review to approve and resolve actionable\\n    feedback. Never bypass pending, failing or requested-change gates.\\n\\nDuring delivery, diagnose failures and route in-scope code/test fixes through\\nthe fix loop; hold publication or merge until its gates pass. Stale evidence\\nrequires revalidation, not abandonment of the task. Stop for missing authority,\\ncredentials, required user interaction or scope expansion. Do not treat an\\nenvironment failure as a product defect or silently waive a gate.\\n\\nFor a CI UI failure, read the assertion and `CAKEBREW_UI_TREE_*` dump in the\\njob log before calling it flaky. Rerun once only when evidence indicates\\ninfrastructure; otherwise fix the cause through the workflow.\\n\\nBefore deleting a stacked PR's parent branch, retarget the child PR to `main`;\\ndeletion otherwise auto-closes it. Recheck the child's diff and gates against\\nits new base. Merge with `gh pr merge <n> --squash --delete-branch`, then switch\\nto `main` and `git pull --ff-only`, preserving unrelated work and respecting\\nbranches checked out in other worktrees.\\n\\n## Role orchestration and evidence\\n\\nFor each feature, bugfix or behavior-changing refactor, the primary agent uses\\n`$cakebrew-workflow` and delegates to the configured roles below. Planning,\\nimplementation and gates must not collapse into one self-review. If a custom\\nagent is unavailable, use a separate agent with its matching skill; disclose\\nany unavailable gate rather than claiming it passed.\\n\\n| Role | Skill | Custom agent | Required handoff |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Orchestrator | `$cakebrew-workflow` | Primary | Scope, decisions, role sequencing, authorized delivery |\\n| Planner | `$cakebrew-plan` | `cakebrew-planner` | Measured unknowns, alternatives, ordered slices, acceptance criteria, expected red, spike cleanup |\\n| Implementer | `$cakebrew-implement` | `cakebrew-implementer` | Owned diff, red/green or mutation evidence, remaining risks |\\n| UI reviewer | `$cakebrew-ui-review` | `cakebrew-ui-reviewer` | Observed visual/journey evidence; approval, findings or blocker |\\n| Verifier | `$cakebrew-verify` | `cakebrew-verifier` | Commands, diagnostics, test counts/skips, artifacts; pass, fail or blocker |\\n| Code reviewer | `$cakebrew-code-review` | `cakebrew-code-reviewer` | Approval or actionable findings with severity, file/line, impact and evidence |\\n| Delivery | `$cakebrew-deliver` | Primary | Commit/PR/head identity, CI and assigned-review status, merge/cleanup result |\\n\\nGive each assignment its worktree, base/head and uncommitted diff, acceptance\\ncriteria, explicit file ownership, evidence locations and exclusions. Tell\\nmutating agents they are not alone and must preserve others' edits. Subagents\\nmay not expand scope, edit project policy or perform delivery unless those\\nactions are explicitly their assignment. Gate agents never edit sources.\\n\\nHandoffs must identify the state examined: worktree, base/head and relevant\\nuncommitted file state. Include concise observed results and log/artifact paths,\\nnot raw log floods. A result for another tree or stale binary is not evidence.\\n\\n**Safe concurrency:** use isolated worktrees for independent mutating work.\\nNever change sources while a verifier or reviewer examines that workspace.\\nSeparate derived-data directories do not isolate the app's `NSUserDefaults`,\\ncatalog cache, running process, `/Applications/Cakebrew.app` or display.\\nSerialize UI/runtime work unless those resources are safely isolated. Preserve\\nand restore user state; do not replace the installed app as incidental testing.\\n\\n## Build and test commands\\n\\nUse a separate `-derivedDataPath` per concurrent build/worktree. Select the\\nintended architecture explicitly if Xcode reports ambiguous destinations.\\nRun the following sequence for executable changes:\\n\\n```sh\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Debug -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\nxcodebuild build -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme Cakebrew \\\\\\n  -configuration Release -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\nxcodebuild test -workspace Cakebrew.xcworkspace -scheme CakebrewTests \\\\\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\\n\\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n\\nxcodebuild test -scheme CakebrewUITests -destination 'platform=macOS' \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=\\\"-\\\" CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO \\\\\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\\\"\\\" PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER=\\\"\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**Validate the instrument:** the app and unit schemes do not compile\\n`CakebrewUITests.m`. After editing it, compile its own target even during the\\ninner loop. Inspect actual assertions, executed test counts and skips, not\\nonly `TEST SUCCEEDED`. `xcodebuild analyze` can exit zero with findings; read\\nthe diagnostics. An empty `log show` result proves nothing unless the probe\\nran and the predicate captured the intended event.\\n\\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) builds both configurations, runs static\\nanalysis and both test jobs on `macos-26`, and uploads diagnostic crash logs\\non failure. Weekly `brew-compat.yml` exercises parsers against real Homebrew\\noutput; fixtures alone cannot detect upstream drift.\\n\\n## Testing contracts\\n\\n- **Mock boundary:** `-BPMockBrew` resolves `+sharedInterface` to\\n  `BPMockHomebrewInterface`. Fixtures include `mockwget`, pinned `mockgit`,\\n  `mockchrome` and `mockvscode`; operations are no-ops. Every new interface\\n  method needs a mock override so UI tests never run real brew.\\n  `BPMockFidelityTests` guards mutating selectors. Use `-BPMockEmptyOutdated`,\\n  `-BPMockEmptyCleanup` and `-BPMockSlowCatalog` for their respective journeys;\\n  the slow catalog makes progress and cancellation observable.\\n- **Unit seams:** `CakebrewTests/` covers parsers, models and manager state.\\n  `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall*` provides pure input → `BPFormula` tests;\\n  private classes can be re-declared in tests.\\n- **Deterministic journeys:** the shared UI launch helper waits for the\\n  initial load (`mockwget`) before navigation to avoid the manager's reselect\\n  race. Pin persisted launch-view settings in its argument domain, including\\n  `-BPLastSelectedSidebarRow 1` and `-BPSortColumnIdentifier \\\"\\\"`; do not bypass\\n  persistence in application code just because `-BPMockBrew` is present.\\n- **Stable identity:** sidebar names repeat across groups. Use unlocalized\\n  identifiers such as `sidebar.casks.installed`, not row indices or titles.\\n  Match table cells with `value BEGINSWITH` because pinned rows append a glyph.\\n- **Headless CI:** windows are not key, so typing/focus and `isHittable` are\\n  unreliable. Assert `exists` and geometry (`frame.size`). System file panels\\n  are out-of-process: unit-test their logic and UI-test presence/navigation.\\n- **Sheet buttons:** scope actions to `self.app.sheets.firstMatch.buttons`\\n  to exclude Touch Bar mirrors. `waitForExistenceWithTimeout:` accepting a\\n  multi-match query does not prove the intended button can be clicked.\\n\\n## Architecture and UI conventions\\n\\n- `BPHomebrewInterface` funnels brew execution through\\n  `performBrewCommandWithArguments:dataReturnBlock:` (async/streaming) or\\n  `performSyncBrewCommandWithArguments:`. Output blocks may legitimately be\\n  nil; guard them with `invokeOutputBlock:withString:`.\\n- New lists follow: `BPListMode` → `BPHomebrewInterfaceListCall` subclass\\n  (arguments/parser) → manager property in `reloadFromInterfaceRebuildingCache:`\\n  → sidebar item/badge → mock fixture.\\n- Casks are `BPFormula` objects with `cask == YES`, preserved by copying and\\n  coding. `BPInstallationWindowController` dispatches `--cask` operations;\\n  `statusForFormula:` reads only the matching namespace's lists.\\n- Slow `brew formulae` and `brew casks` catalogs share a 24-hour disk cache\\n  (`allFormulae.cache.bin`, two keys). Avoid unnecessary cold catalog calls.\\n- `FormulaeSideBarItem` values are outline row indices, including groups.\\n  Insertions require updating the enum, View-menu tags in `MainMenu.xib` and\\n  every switch/comparison (locate with `rg FormulaeSideBarItem`).\\n- Refresh badges with `refreshBadgeForListMode:` / `reloadItem:`, never sidebar\\n  `reloadData`: it clears selection and can persist an unintended row.\\n  Accessibility row identifiers belong on the cell's text field, not the\\n  non-accessible `NSTableCellView` container.\\n- Use `beginSheetModalForWindow:_appDelegate.window …`, never `runModal` or\\n  `self.view.window` (which can be nil during split-view reparenting).\\n- Edit xibs as XML; run `xmllint --noout`, use unique `cbk-…` IDs and extend\\n  `NSStackView` visibility-priority/custom-spacing arrays with arranged views.\\n  Menu enablement uses Cocoa bindings (`currentFormula` / `currentFormulaPinned`),\\n  not `validateMenuItem:`.\\n- Add UI strings to all six `Cakebrew/*.lproj/Localizable.strings` files and\\n  run `plutil -lint`. Use English placeholders until translated; keep Homebrew\\n  terms such as \\\"Casks\\\" untranslated. Base-internationalized xib menu titles\\n  do not need `.strings` churn. Use SF Symbols for UI chrome, not bundled icons.\\n\\n## Distribution and platform constraints\\n\\n- Ship Developer ID signed, hardened and notarized, **without App Sandbox**.\\n  Keep the privacy manifest, input validation and ATS protections. Sandbox\\n  probes showed brew execution needs filesystem exceptions; even with them,\\n  the redirected home directory splits Homebrew caches and sends Services\\n  LaunchAgents into a container where launchd cannot use them. Do not add\\n  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`; remeasure these boundaries before revisiting.\\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` signs, notarizes and staples on `v*` tags;\\n  its header lists required secrets. Releases need a Developer ID Application\\n  certificate, separate from Apple Development; verify availability before\\n  attempting distribution.\\n- Ordinary Apple Development-signed Xcode builds satisfy the helper's team-OU\\n  designated requirement. Real registration/Login Items testing requires a\\n  signed app in `/Applications`. With authorization to replace the installed\\n  app, use `scripts/install-signed.sh` (Release by default, or pass `Debug`):\\n  it uses fresh derived data and verifies both signatures. Keep the final\\n  **Verify embedded helper signature** build phase and its declared input;\\n  it skips unsigned/ad-hoc builds intentionally.\\n- Minimum macOS is latest major minus one (current project target: `15.0`).\\n  Use the latest SDK (`SDKROOT = macosx`, never pinned) and latest CI runner\\n  (currently `macos-26`). At a new major release, update the minimum and runner;\\n  `LSMinimumSystemVersion` derives from the build setting.\\n\\n## Local environment diagnostics\\n\\n- Entire's session-sync pre-push hook hangs on this machine. Use\\n  `git push --no-verify` for that known hook issue, not to bypass code checks.\\n- For \\\"Timed out while enabling automation mode\\\", check\\n  `DevToolsSecurity -status` first. Developer mode, not just `_developer` group\\n  membership, was the local prerequisite; do not change host permissions\\n  based on an unexplained runner failure. Full UI runs take minutes and belong\\n  before PR creation, not in the TDD inner loop.\\n- Check `ioreg -n Root -d 1 -r -k IOConsoleLocked` before visual testing.\\n  A locked/sleeping display can cause infinite click coordinates, background\\n  activation failures or `screencapture` errors. Ask for the display to be\\n  unlocked/awake; those are not product failures and cannot be fixed in code.\\n- `-AppleInterfaceStyle Light` does not reliably force AppKit appearance.\\n  Check the rendered appearance; coordinate a manual System Settings change\\n  when needed rather than silently changing the user's global preferences.\\n- While the 1Password SSH signing agent remains broken, use\\n  `git -c commit.gpgsign=false` for commits. Re-enable signing when fixed.\\nfaec16d94a4a5c6c0657032a58b833c60e17849acd19a305fa23d52e7ba047ca  -\\n/private/tmp/cakebrew-workflow-pr.Cx78mk\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"934237\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.00209782,\"session_id\":78815,\"original_token_count\":0,\"output\":\"\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a07eca-579f-72b0-95b4-dd6f4e0bfedc","create_time":1788833613.421714}}}
